WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.920
- Welcome to the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.930
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:06.000
for everyday life.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.330
[authoritative music]

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:10.320
I'm Andrew Huberman,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.980
and I'm a professor of
neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:14.890
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.400
Today, we are going to discuss salt,

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.243
also referred to as sodium.

00:00:19.243 --> 00:00:21.630
Now, most of us think of salt

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:24.390
as something that we
put on and in our food,

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:25.800
maybe something to avoid.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.800
Maybe some of you are actually
trying to get more salt.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.920
Some of you are trying to get less salt.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.480
We all seem to associate salt

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.470
with things like blood
pressure, et cetera.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:36.380
Today, we are going to go down
a different set of avenues

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:37.213
related to salt.

00:00:37.213 --> 00:00:40.370
We will certainly cover how
salt regulates blood pressure.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:41.860
We are also going to talk about

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.010
how the brain regulates
our appetite for salt

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:47.260
or our aversion for salt.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:48.580
We are also going to talk about

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.470
how our sensing of salty tastes

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:54.640
actually mediates how much sugar we crave

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.400
and whether or not we
ingest more or less sugar

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.050
than we actually need,

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:00.470
so what you're going to learn today

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.500
is that the so-called salt system,

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.470
meaning the cells and
connections in our brain and body

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.900
that mediate salt craving and avoidance,

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.550
are regulating many, many
aspects of our health

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.980
and our ability to perform
in various contexts,

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:15.310
things like athletic performance,

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:17.540
things like cognitive performance.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.820
We're also going to talk
about aging and dementia

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.420
and avoiding aging and dementia

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.520
and what role salt and salt
avoidance might play in that.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:26.990
We're going to touch on some themes that,

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:28.880
for some of you, might seem controversial,

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.600
and indeed, if they are controversial,

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.520
I'll be sure to highlight them as such.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.280
I'm going to cover a lot of new data

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:35.970
that point to the possibility,

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.230
I want to emphasize the possibility,

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:40.930
that for some people,
more salt might help them

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:44.080
in terms of health, cognitive,
and bodily functioning,

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.440
and for other people, less
salt is going to be better.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.540
I'm going to talk about what
the various parameters are.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.330
I'm going to give you
some guidelines that,

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.230
in concert with your physician,

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.430
who you should absolutely talk to

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:56.060
before adding or changing anything

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.920
to your diet or supplementation regime,

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.930
can help you arrive at a salt intake

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:01.300
that's going to optimize your mental,

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.300
physical health, and performance,

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.540
so we're going to cover neurobiology.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.360
We're going to cover hormone biology.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.650
We're going to talk about liver function.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.600
We're going to talk about kidney function

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.450
and, of course, brain function.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.610
I'm excited to share this
information with you today.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:16.480
I'm certain you're going to come
away with a lot of information

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.090
and actionable items.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.020
I'm pleased to announce

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.370
that I'm hosting two live events this May.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:22.840
The first live event will take place

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.450
in Seattle, Washington on May 17th.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:26.940
The second live event will take place

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.063
in Portland, Oregon on May 18th.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.360
Presale tickets for these
two events are now available

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.250
at hubermanlab.com/tour.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.180
I should mention that

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.240
while I do hope to visit other
cities in the near future

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.470
to do more live events,

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:41.303
right now,

00:02:41.303 --> 00:02:43.360
these are the only two live
events I have scheduled,

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.000
at least for the six months,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.610
so once again, if you go
to hubermanlab.com/tour,

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.490
you can access the presale tickets.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.380
I hope to see you at these live events,

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:54.360
and as always, thank you for
your interest in science.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.170
Before we dive into the
topic of today's episode,

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:59.810
I want to highlight a
really exciting new study.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:02.290
This is a study from Diego Bohorquez' lab

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.170
at Duke University.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.540
The Bohorquez Lab studies interactions

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.870
between the gut and the brain

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:11.220
and has made some incredible discoveries

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.350
of the so-called neuropod cells.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:15.890
Neuropod cells are neurons, nerve cells,

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:17.840
that reside in our gut

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.320
and that detect things like
fatty acids, amino acids,

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.410
and some neuropod cells sense sugar.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:26.900
Previous work from this
laboratory has shown

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.270
that when we ingest sugar,

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:32.890
these neuropod cells respond to that sugar

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:34.570
and send electrical signals

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:36.540
up a little wire that we call an axon

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.040
through the vagus nerve, for
those of you that want to know,

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.970
and into the brain,

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:44.060
and through subsequent
stations of neural processing,

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:46.430
evoke the release of dopamine.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:49.950
Dopamine is a molecule
known to promote craving

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:52.540
and motivation and indeed action,

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.670
and what these neuropod
cells that send sugar

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:58.180
are thought to do is to promote
seeking and consumption,

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:00.960
eating of more sugary foods.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.610
Now, the incredible thing is
that it's all subconscious.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:06.100
This is a taste system in the gut

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.600
that is not available to
your conscious awareness.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.360
Now, of course, when
you ingest sweet foods,

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:12.920
you taste them on your mouth too,

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:14.170
and so part of the reason

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.090
that you crave sweet foods, perhaps,

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.840
is because they taste good to you,

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:19.500
and the other reason

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.300
is that these neuropod cells
are driving a chemical craving

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.790
below your conscious detection,

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:26.400
so they're really two systems.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.400
Your gut is sensing, at a
subconscious level, what's in it

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:34.070
and sending signals to your
brain that work in concert,

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:36.420
in parallel with the signals
coming from your mouth

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:38.680
and your experience of
the taste of the food.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:40.620
Now, that alone is incredible,

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.220
and it's been the subject of
many important landmark papers

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:44.593
over the last decade or so.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.970
You can imagine how this
system would be very important

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:49.300
for things like hidden sugars

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.610
when nowadays, in a
lot of processed foods,

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:53.670
they're putting hidden sugars.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:55.240
They're putting a lot of things

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:58.850
that cause your gut to
send signals to your brain

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:01.200
that make you crave more of those foods,

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.820
so for those of you
that really love sugar,

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.480
just understand it's not just
about how that sugar tastes.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.020
The new study from the Bohorquez Lab

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.970
deserves attention, I believe.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:11.940
This is a paper published just recently,

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.720
February 25th this year, 2022,

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.800
in "Nature Neuroscience,"
an excellent journal,

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.287
and the title of the paper is

00:05:18.287 --> 00:05:20.397
"The Preference for Sugar over Sweetener

00:05:20.397 --> 00:05:22.560
"Depends on a Gut Sensor Cell."

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.010
The Bohorquez Lab has now
discovered a neuropod cell,

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.770
meaning a category of neurons,

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:32.330
that can distinguish between
sweet things in the gut

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:36.380
that contain calories,
for instance, sugar,

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:40.190
and things in the gut that are sweet

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:42.010
but do not contain calories:

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:44.280
artificial sweeteners
like aspartame, sucralose,

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:45.810
and so forth.

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:46.700
There are also, of course,

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:48.940
nonartificial, noncaloric sweeteners

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.390
like stevia, monk fruit, et cetera.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:52.300
They did not explore

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:54.690
the full gallery of artificial sweeteners.

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:57.470
What they did find,
however, ought to pertain

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:01.430
to all forms of sweet,
noncaloric substances.

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:02.263
What they discovered

00:06:02.263 --> 00:06:04.480
was that there is a
signature pattern of signals

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:06.720
sent from the gut to the brain

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.070
when we ingest artificial
or noncaloric sweeteners.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:11.970
This is important

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.300
because what it says is that
at a subconscious level,

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:15.390
the gut can distinguish

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:17.150
between sweet things that contain calories

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:18.640
and sweet things that do not.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:23.410
Now, what the downstream
consequences of this sensing is

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.440
or what they are isn't yet clear.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:27.370
Now, I believe everyone

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:29.720
should be aware of these kinds of studies

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:30.700
for a couple of reasons.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:32.060
First of all,

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.520
it's important to understand
that what you crave,

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:36.830
meaning the foods you crave
and the drinks you crave,

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:39.320
is, in part, due to your
conscious experience

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:40.850
of the taste of those things,

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:44.250
but also due to biochemical
and neural events

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:47.610
that start in the body
and impinge on your brain

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:49.830
and cause you to seek out certain things

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:52.030
even though you might not know

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:53.600
why you're seeking out more sugar.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.560
You find that you're
craving a lot of sugar,

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:58.120
or you're craving a lot of foods
with artificial sweeteners,

00:06:58.120 --> 00:06:59.920
and you don't necessarily know why.

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:01.570
Now, artificial sweeteners themselves

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:03.390
are a somewhat controversial topic.

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:05.100
I want to highlight that.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:08.840
Some months back, I described
a study from Yale University

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:11.790
about how one can condition
the insulin system.

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:13.990
Insulin is involved in
mobilizing of blood sugar

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:15.910
and so forth in the body,
as many of you know,

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:18.120
and I described some studies

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.460
that were done from Yale
University School of Medicine

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:22.300
looking at how artificial sweeteners

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:26.170
can actually evoke an insulin response

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:29.060
under certain conditions.

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:30.810
Now, a couple of key things.

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:34.420
I got a little bit of pushback
after covering those studies,

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:37.410
and I encourage pushback all the time.

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:38.590
Pushback is one of those things

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:41.320
that forces all of us to
drill deeper into a topic.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:42.153
I want to be clear.

00:07:42.153 --> 00:07:42.986
First of all,

00:07:42.986 --> 00:07:45.730
I am not one to demonize
artificial sweeteners.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:47.960
There is evidence, in animal models,

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:49.380
in animal models,

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:53.280
that artificial sweeteners can
disrupt the gut microbiome,

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.750
but those were fairly high
doses of artificial sweeteners,

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:57.980
and it's unclear if the same
thing pertains to humans,

00:07:57.980 --> 00:07:59.380
still unclear, I should say,

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:01.460
has not been investigated thoroughly.

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:03.640
Some people don't like the
taste of artificial sweeteners.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:04.510
Some people do.

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:05.343
Some people find

00:08:05.343 --> 00:08:08.490
that they really help them
avoid excessive caloric intake.

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:09.730
Some people believe,

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:12.550
and yet I should emphasize
there still isn't evidence

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:15.330
that they can adjust the
insulin response in all people.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:16.640
I just want to repeat that three times

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:18.763
so that people are clear on that fact.

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:21.940
What these new data emphasize, however,

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:23.480
is that we need to understand

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:26.320
how artificial sweeteners are consumed

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:27.153
at the level of the gut,

00:08:27.153 --> 00:08:29.160
or I should say registered
at the level of the gut

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:31.910
and how that changes brain function

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:33.127
because one thing that I'm familiar with

00:08:33.127 --> 00:08:34.500
and that many people report

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:37.270
is that when they first
taste artificial sweeteners,

00:08:37.270 --> 00:08:39.290
they taste sort of not right to them.

00:08:39.290 --> 00:08:40.160
They don't like the taste,

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:42.900
but over time, they actually
start to crave that taste.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:43.733
I've experienced this.

00:08:43.733 --> 00:08:45.040
I used to drink a lot of diet sodas

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:46.510
when I was in graduate school,

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:47.990
so this would be aspartame,

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:50.750
and I found that I would,
I actually needed them.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:51.810
Now, maybe it was the caffeine.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:53.800
Maybe I just liked the sweet
taste or the carbonation.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.240
We actually have a drive for carbonation,

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:58.310
which is a topic of a future episode,

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:01.100
but when I finally quit them,

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:02.780
for reasons that were independent

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:04.630
of any fear of artificial sweeteners,

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:07.350
I found that I didn't like the taste.

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:10.480
Nowadays, I only occasionally
drink a diet soda.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:11.650
I usually do that if I'm on a plane

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:12.930
and there's nothing else available to me,

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:14.310
so I don't demonize them.

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:16.530
I might drink one every once in a while.

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:17.363
No big deal.

00:09:17.363 --> 00:09:19.140
I also want to be clear I consume stevia

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:21.130
on a number of different supplements

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:22.510
and foods that I consume.

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:25.350
Stevia, of course, is a
plant-based noncaloric sweetener,

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:27.570
so I, myself, consume
artificial sweeteners.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:28.910
Many people hate them.

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:30.440
Many people like them

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:32.330
and find them useful for their nutrition,

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:34.530
and in fact, to keep their caloric intake

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:36.290
in a range that's right for them,

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:38.640
and many people, like myself,

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.900
are curious about them
and somewhat wary of them

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:44.210
and yet continue to consume
them in small amounts.

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:46.440
I think most people probably
fall into that category.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:48.940
I should also mention that
many food manufacturers

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:51.400
put artificial sweeteners,

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:54.400
such as sucralose, et cetera, into foods,

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:56.260
and it's always been unclear

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:58.210
as to why they might want to do that,

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:02.270
and yet we know that the
sweet taste consumption,

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:03.640
even if it doesn't contain calories,

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:06.730
can drive more craving of sweet food,

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:09.670
so there may be a logic or a
strategy to why they do that.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:12.330
Again, a topic for
exploration on today's podcast

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:13.260
and in future podcasts

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:15.210
because where we're headed today

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:18.840
is a discussion about how
salt and salt sensing,

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:20.770
both consciously and unconsciously,

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:22.890
can adjust our craving for other things,

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:25.350
like sugar and water and so on,

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:27.010
so I want to highlight this beautiful work

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:27.843
from the Bohorquez Lab.

00:10:27.843 --> 00:10:29.380
We'll put a link to the study.

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:32.540
I want to open this as a
chapter for further exploration.

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:35.120
I like to think that the
listeners of this podcast

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.000
