00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:08.340
Okay, let's talk about salt.

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:10.190
Salt has many, many important functions

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:11.640
in the brain and body.

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:14.240
For instance, it regulates fluid balance,

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:18.710
how much fluid you desire and
how much fluid you excrete.

00:15:18.710 --> 00:15:22.200
It also regulates your
desire for salt itself,

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.090
meaning your salt appetite.

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:28.050
You have a homeostatically
driven salt appetite.

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:29.400
I'll talk about the mechanisms today,

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:31.330
make them all very clear.

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:34.390
What that means is that
you crave salty things,

00:15:34.390 --> 00:15:35.710
beverages and foods,

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:37.500
when your salt stores are low,

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:40.800
and you tend to avoid
salty beverages and foods

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:42.350
when your salt stores are high,

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:43.920
although that's not always the case.

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:46.790
There are circumstances where
you'll continue to crave salt

00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:48.610
even though you don't need salt

00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:51.940
or indeed even if you
need to eliminate salt

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:52.790
from your system.

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:56.760
Salt also regulates your
appetite for other nutrients,

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:59.400
things like sugar, things
like carbohydrates,

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:01.550
and today, we'll explore all of that.

00:16:01.550 --> 00:16:04.040
Technically, salt is a mineral,

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:06.510
and I should mention that when I say salt,

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:09.160
I am indeed referring to
sodium, in most cases,

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:11.880
although I will be clear to
distinguish salt from sodium,

00:16:11.880 --> 00:16:14.080
meaning table salt from sodium.

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:16.070
Most people don't realize this,

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:18.630
but one gram of table salt

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:22.930
contains about 388 milligrams of sodium,

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:25.710
so technically, we should be
talking about sodium today

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:26.543
and not salt.

00:16:26.543 --> 00:16:28.000
I will use them interchangeably

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:31.880
unless I'm referring to some
specific recommendations

00:16:31.880 --> 00:16:36.340
or ideas about trying to
define your ideal salt,

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:38.310
aka sodium intake, okay?

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:39.270
So this is important.

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:40.980
I think, right off the bat,

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:43.770
a lot of people get themselves
into a place of confusion

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:46.130
and potentially even to a place of trouble

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:49.230
by thinking that table salt in grams

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:51.677
always equates to sodium in grams,

00:16:51.677 --> 00:16:53.940
and that's simply not the case.

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:56.970
Today, we're going to
explore the neural mechanisms

00:16:56.970 --> 00:17:00.640
by which we regulate our salt appetite

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:02.810
and the way that the
brain and body interact

00:17:02.810 --> 00:17:06.250
in the context of
salt-seeking, salt avoidance,

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:08.070
how to determine when we need more salt,

00:17:08.070 --> 00:17:09.490
when we need less salt.

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:10.890
We'll talk about kidney function.

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:12.370
We'll get into all of it,

00:17:12.370 --> 00:17:14.120
and we're going to do
it very systematically,

00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:16.280
so let's start in the brain.

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:19.830
We all harbor small sets of neurons.

00:17:19.830 --> 00:17:21.820
We call these sets of neurons nuclei,

00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:24.350
meaning little clusters of neurons,

00:17:24.350 --> 00:17:27.783
that sense the levels of
salt in our brain and body.

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:31.040
There are couple brain
regions that do this,

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:33.230
and these brain regions
are very, very special,

00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:37.483
special because they lack
biological fences around them

00:17:37.483 --> 00:17:39.400
that other brain areas have,

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:42.380
and those fences, or I
should say that fence,

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:43.490
goes by a particular name,

00:17:43.490 --> 00:17:47.720
and that name is the
blood-brain barrier, or BBB.

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:50.660
Most substances that are
circulating around in your body

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:52.590
do not have access to the brain,

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:54.490
in particular, large molecules

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:56.550
can't just pass into the brain.

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:59.000
The brain is a privileged
organ in this sense.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:00.890
There are couple other
organs that are privileged

00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:03.210
and that have very strict barriers,

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:05.597
very particular fences, if you will,

00:18:05.597 --> 00:18:06.700
and those other organs

00:18:06.700 --> 00:18:08.650
include things like
the ovaries and testes,

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:11.250
and that makes sense for
the following reason:

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:15.320
First of all, the brain,
at least most of the brain,

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:18.040
cannot regenerate after injury.

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:20.550
You just simply can't replace
brain cells after injury.

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:22.870
I know people get really
excited about neurogenesis,

00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:23.970
the birth of new neurons,

00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:26.650
and indeed, neurogenesis
has been demonstrated

00:18:26.650 --> 00:18:27.570
in animal models,

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:30.520
and to some extent it exists
in humans in a few places,

00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:32.450
for instance, the olfactory bulb,

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:34.500
where neurons are responsible

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:36.470
for detecting odorants in the environment,

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:37.470
for smell, that is,

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:40.870
and in a little subregion of
the hippocampus, a memory area,

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:42.820
there's probably some neurogenesis,

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:44.750
but the bulk of really good data out there

00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:46.440
point to the fact that in humans

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:48.700
there's not much turnover of neurons.

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:51.210
What that means is that the
neurons you're born with

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:53.630
are the ones that you're going to be using

00:18:53.630 --> 00:18:56.280
most, if not all, of your life.

00:18:56.280 --> 00:18:58.300
In fact, you're born
with many more neurons

00:18:58.300 --> 00:18:59.600
than you'll have later,

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:02.080
and there's a process of
naturally occurring cell death

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:04.430
called apoptosis, that
occurs during development,

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:06.480
so you actually are born
with many more neurons

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:07.400
than you have later in life,

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:10.570
and that's the reflection
of a normal, healthy process

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:13.080
of nerve cell elimination,

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:15.100
so the estimates vary,

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:18.060
but anywhere from 1/3 to maybe even 1/2

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:20.280
or even 2/3 of neurons,
depending on the brain area,

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:22.090
are just going to die across development.

00:19:22.090 --> 00:19:23.190
That might sound terrible,

00:19:23.190 --> 00:19:24.290
but that's actually one of the ways

00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:26.890
in which you go from being kind
of like a little potato bug

00:19:26.890 --> 00:19:28.820
flopping around helplessly in your crib

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:31.180
to being an organism
that can walk and talk

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:34.220
and articulate and calculate math

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:36.500
or do whatever it is
that you do for a living,

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:39.920
so the brain has a set of elements,

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:41.510
these nerve cells and other cells,

00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:44.620
and it needs to use those
for the entire lifespan,

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:47.240
so having a BBB, a blood-brain
barrier around the brain,

00:19:47.240 --> 00:19:49.140
is absolutely critical.

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:53.860
The ovaries and testes have
a barrier for, we assume,

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:56.340
the reason that they
contain the genetic material

00:19:56.340 --> 00:19:59.370
by which we can pass on
our genes to our offspring,

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:00.650
progeny, meaning make children,

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:02.680
and those children will have our genes,

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:04.580
or at least half of them,

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:06.730
the other half from
the partner, of course.

00:20:08.170 --> 00:20:12.920
If the cells within the
ovaries and testes are mutated,

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:14.760
well, then, you can get
mutations in offspring,

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:17.110
so that's very costly in
the evolutionary sense,

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:17.943
so it makes sense

00:20:17.943 --> 00:20:19.840
that you would have a
barrier from the blood

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:21.730
so if you ingest what's called a mutagen,

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:25.080
if you ingest something that
can mutate the genes of cells,

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:28.930
you can imagine why
there would be a premium

00:20:28.930 --> 00:20:30.440
on not allowing those mutagens

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:33.530
to get into the brain, the
ovaries, or the testes, okay?

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:34.990
So the brain has this BBB,

00:20:34.990 --> 00:20:36.490
this blood-brain barrier around it,

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:37.560
which makes it very, very hard

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:39.080
for substances to pass into the brain

00:20:39.080 --> 00:20:41.150
unless those substances are very small

00:20:41.150 --> 00:20:44.170
or those substances and molecules

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:46.950
are critically required
for brain function.

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:50.680
However, there are a couple
of regions in the brain

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:53.940
that have a fence around them,

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:55.610
but that fence is weaker, okay?

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:57.870
It's sort of like going
from a really big wall

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:02.300
thick electronic 24-hour
surveillance fence

00:21:02.300 --> 00:21:03.480
where nothing can pass through

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:06.970
except only the exclusive cargo
that's allowed to go through

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:09.730
to having a little cyclone
fence with a couple holes in it,

00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:12.290
or it's a kind of a picket
fence that's falling over,

00:21:12.290 --> 00:21:14.250
and substances can move freely in

00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:17.620
from the blood circulating
in the body into the brain,

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:19.440
and it turns out that
the areas of the brain

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:21.530
that monitor salt balance

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:24.580
and other features of
what's happening in the body

00:21:24.580 --> 00:21:27.580
at the level of what we call osmolarity,

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:30.133
at the concentration of salt,

00:21:31.090 --> 00:21:34.560
reside in these little sets of neurons

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:37.470
that sit just on the other
side of these weak fences,

00:21:37.470 --> 00:21:39.820
and the most important
and famous of these,

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:43.540
for sake of today's
conversation, is one called OVLT.

00:21:43.540 --> 00:21:44.910
OVLT stands for

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:48.070
the organum vasculosum of
the lateral terminalis.

00:21:48.070 --> 00:21:50.720
It is what's called a
circumventricular organ.

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:51.870
Why circumventricular?

00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:54.230
Well, not to bog you
down with neuroanatomy,

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:57.200
but your brain is a big squishy mass

00:21:57.200 --> 00:21:59.010
of neurons and other cell types,

00:21:59.010 --> 00:22:00.880
but it has to be nourished,

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:03.530
and through the middle of that brain,

00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:06.687
there is a tube, there's a
hollow, that creates spaces,

00:22:06.687 --> 00:22:08.930
and those spaces are called ventricles.

00:22:08.930 --> 00:22:10.020
The ventricles are spaces

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:13.860
in which cerebrospinal fluid circulates,

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:15.090
and it nourishes the brain.

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:17.840
It does a number of other things as well.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:20.370
The circumventricular organs

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:23.820
are areas of the brain that are
near that circulating fluid,

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:26.610
and that circulating fluid
has access to the bloodstream,

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:28.230
and the bloodstream has access to it,

00:22:28.230 --> 00:22:30.110
and this structure that I'm referring to,

00:22:30.110 --> 00:22:33.460
OVLT, organum vasculosum
of the lateral terminalis,

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:35.910
has neurons that can sense

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:38.700
the contents of the blood

00:22:38.700 --> 00:22:41.200
and, to some extent,
the cerebrospinal fluid.

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:44.340
There are couple other brain
areas that can do this as well.

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:47.870
They go also by the name of
circumventricular organs,

00:22:47.870 --> 00:22:50.130
and I'll talk about the names
of some of those other areas,

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:51.300
but for today,

00:22:51.300 --> 00:22:53.320
and I think for sake of
most of the discussion,

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:55.510
understand that the OVLT is special.

00:22:55.510 --> 00:22:56.343
Why?

00:22:56.343 --> 00:22:58.740
Because it doesn't have
this thick barrier fence,

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:00.390
which sounds like a bad thing,

00:23:00.390 --> 00:23:04.480
and yet it's a terrific border detector.

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:07.160
The neurons in that region

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:08.360
are able to pay attention

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:10.900
to what's passing through
in the bloodstream

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:12.670
and can detect, for instance,

00:23:12.670 --> 00:23:15.300
if the levels of sodium in
the bloodstream are too low,

00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:17.330
if the level of blood pressure in the body

00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:18.600
is too low or too high,

00:23:18.600 --> 00:23:22.930
and then, the OVLT can send
signals to other brain areas,

00:23:22.930 --> 00:23:25.200
and then, those other
brain areas can do things

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:28.540
like release hormones that
can go and act on tissues

00:23:28.540 --> 00:23:31.560
in what we call the
periphery, in the body,

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:36.060
and, for instance, have the
kidneys secrete more urine

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:39.410
to get rid of salt that's
excessive salt in the body

00:23:39.410 --> 00:23:41.840
or have the kidneys hold onto urine,

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:45.140
to hold onto whatever water
or fluid that one might need,

00:23:45.140 --> 00:23:47.060
so before I go any
deeper into this pathway,

00:23:47.060 --> 00:23:50.490
just understand that the OVLT
has a very limited barrier.

00:23:50.490 --> 00:23:52.400
It can detect things in the bloodstream,

00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:55.830
and this incredible area of the brain,

00:23:55.830 --> 00:23:57.380
almost single-handedly,

00:23:57.380 --> 00:23:59.110
sets off the cascades of things

00:23:59.110 --> 00:24:02.050
that allow you to regulate
your salt balance,

00:24:02.050 --> 00:24:04.130
which turns out to be absolutely critical,

00:24:04.130 --> 00:24:05.700
not just for your ability to think

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:06.800
and for your neurons to work,

00:24:06.800 --> 00:24:08.450
but indeed, for all of life.

00:24:08.450 --> 00:24:10.350
If the OVLT doesn't function correctly,

00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:12.750
you're effectively dead or dead soon,

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:15.010
so this is a very important brain region,

00:24:15.010 --> 00:24:17.120
so let's talk about the
function of the OVLT

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:20.390
and flesh out some of the
other aspects of its circuitry,

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:22.350
of its communication
with other brain areas

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:23.190
and with the body

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:25.650
in the context of something
that we are all familiar with,

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:27.120
which is thirst.

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:29.940
Have you ever wondered
just why you get thirsty?

00:24:29.940 --> 00:24:33.380
Well, it's because neurons in your OVLT

00:24:33.380 --> 00:24:35.560
are detecting changes in your bloodstream,

00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:38.570
which detect global
changes within your body,

00:24:38.570 --> 00:24:40.143
and in response to that,

00:24:40.143 --> 00:24:44.810
your OVLT sets off certain
events within your brain and body

00:24:44.810 --> 00:24:47.400
that make you either
want to drink more fluid

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:49.103
or to stop drinking fluid.

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:52.400
There are two main kinds of thirst.

00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:54.900
The first one is called osmotic thirst,

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:57.823
and the second is called
hypovolemic thirst.

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:00.330
Osmotic thirst has to do

00:25:00.330 --> 00:25:03.230
with the concentration of
salt in your bloodstream,

00:25:03.230 --> 00:25:05.500
so let's say you ingest
something very, very salty.

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:08.250
Let's say you ingest a big bag of,

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:09.880
I confess I don't eat these very often,

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:11.760
but I really like those
kettle potato chips,

00:25:11.760 --> 00:25:12.660
and they're pretty salty.

00:25:12.660 --> 00:25:14.630
I've never actually measured
how much sodium is in them.

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:15.770
I'm sure the information is there.

00:25:15.770 --> 00:25:17.600
Every once in a while,

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:20.970
I'm particularly interested in doing so,

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:22.480
I'll just down a bag of those things,

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:24.360
and I really like them,
and they're very salty,

00:25:24.360 --> 00:25:26.530
but they almost always
make me feel thirsty,

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:28.960
and the reason is that by eating those,

00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:30.370
I've ingested a lot of sodium.

00:25:30.370 --> 00:25:32.550
Again, not a frequent occurrence for me,

00:25:32.550 --> 00:25:34.810
but happens every now and again,

00:25:34.810 --> 00:25:36.610
and I don't have too much shame about that

00:25:36.610 --> 00:25:39.460
because I think I have a pretty
healthy relationship to food

00:25:39.460 --> 00:25:40.870
and I enjoy them,

00:25:40.870 --> 00:25:42.220
and I understand

00:25:42.220 --> 00:25:45.720
that it will drive salt
levels up in my bloodstream,

00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:49.050
and that will cause me
to be thirsty, but why?

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:49.883
Why?

00:25:49.883 --> 00:25:53.810
Because neurons in the OVLT
come in two main varieties.

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:57.800
One variety senses the
osmolarity of the blood

00:25:57.800 --> 00:25:59.920
that's getting across
that weak little fence

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:01.420
that we talked about before,

00:26:01.420 --> 00:26:03.040
and when the osmolarity,

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:06.410
meaning the salt concentration
of the blood, is high,

00:26:06.410 --> 00:26:09.340
it activates these specific
neurons in the OVLT,

00:26:09.340 --> 00:26:10.190
and by activates,

00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:13.200
I mean it causes them to
send electrical potentials,

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:17.690
literally, send electrical
signals to other brain areas,

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:19.050
and those other brain areas

00:26:19.050 --> 00:26:21.960
inspire a number of
different downstream events,

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:23.970
so what are those other brain areas?

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:25.140
Well, the OVLT

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:30.360
signals to an area called
the supraoptic nucleus.

00:26:30.360 --> 00:26:33.290
The name and why it's called
the supraoptic nucleus

00:26:33.290 --> 00:26:34.970
is not necessarily important.

00:26:34.970 --> 00:26:37.860
It also signals to the so-called
paraventricular nucleus,

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:39.820
another nucleus that
sits near the ventricles

00:26:39.820 --> 00:26:43.000
and can monitor the qualities,

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:45.530
the chemical qualities of
the cerebrospinal fluid

00:26:45.530 --> 00:26:48.370
as well as, probably,
the bloodstream as well,

00:26:48.370 --> 00:26:51.860
and the consequence

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:53.610
of that communication

00:26:53.610 --> 00:26:57.220
is that a particular hormone
is eventually released

00:26:57.220 --> 00:26:59.720
from the posterior pituitary.

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:02.810
Now, the pituitary is a gland

00:27:02.810 --> 00:27:05.130
that sits near the roof of your mouth.

00:27:05.130 --> 00:27:06.470
It releases all sorts of things

00:27:06.470 --> 00:27:09.430
like growth hormone and
luteinizing hormone.

00:27:09.430 --> 00:27:11.160
Luteinizing hormone will stimulate things

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:15.020
like estrogen and testosterone
production and release

00:27:15.020 --> 00:27:17.640
from the ovaries and testes and so on.

00:27:17.640 --> 00:27:18.750
The pituitary has a bunch

00:27:18.750 --> 00:27:20.070
of different compartments and functions,

00:27:20.070 --> 00:27:22.600
but what's really cool about the pituitary

00:27:22.600 --> 00:27:24.540
is that certain regions of the pituitary

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:27.490
actually contain the axons,
the wires of neurons,

00:27:27.490 --> 00:27:29.590
and the neurons reside in the brain,

00:27:29.590 --> 00:27:34.590
and so the supraoptic nucleus
gets a signal from the OVLT.

00:27:34.950 --> 00:27:37.440
The signal is purely in the
form of electrical activity.

00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:39.440
Remember, neurons aren't
talking in one another

00:27:39.440 --> 00:27:40.770
about what's happening out there.

00:27:40.770 --> 00:27:41.603
They're not saying, "Psst!

00:27:41.603 --> 00:27:44.227
"Hey, there's too much
salt in the bloodstream.

00:27:44.227 --> 00:27:45.460
"Let's do something about it."

00:27:45.460 --> 00:27:47.860
All they receive are
so-called action potential,

00:27:47.860 --> 00:27:49.490
waves of electricity.

00:27:49.490 --> 00:27:51.850
The neurons in the supraoptic nucleus

00:27:51.850 --> 00:27:56.010
then release their own electrical signals

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:57.640
within the pituitary,

00:27:57.640 --> 00:28:00.500
and some of those neurons
and nearby neurons

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:02.700
are capable of releasing hormones

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:05.380
as well as electrical signals,

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:07.150
so from the pituitary,

00:28:07.150 --> 00:28:08.840
there's a hormonal signal that's released

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:10.950
called vasopressin.

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:15.560
Vasopressin also goes by the
name antidiuretic hormone,

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:19.750
and antidiuretic hormone has the capacity

00:28:19.750 --> 00:28:23.810
to either restrict the amount
of urine that we secrete,

00:28:23.810 --> 00:28:26.130
or, when that system is turned off,

00:28:26.130 --> 00:28:29.640
to increase the amount
of urine that we secrete,

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:33.530
so there's a complicated set
of cascades that's evoked

00:28:33.530 --> 00:28:35.960
by having high salt
concentration in the blood.

00:28:35.960 --> 00:28:38.840
There's also a complicated set
of cascades that are evoked

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:42.250
by having low concentrations
of sodium in the blood,

00:28:42.250 --> 00:28:44.220
but the pathway is nonetheless the same.

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:48.090
It's OVLT is detecting
those osmolarity changes,

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:49.730
communicating to the supraoptic nucleus.

00:28:49.730 --> 00:28:54.380
Supraoptic nucleus is either
causing the release of

00:28:54.380 --> 00:28:57.850
or is releasing vasopressin,
antidiuretic hormone,

00:28:57.850 --> 00:28:59.870
or that system is shut off

00:28:59.870 --> 00:29:03.520
so that the antidiuretic
hormone is not secreted,

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:06.270
which would allow urine to
flow more freely, right?

00:29:06.270 --> 00:29:10.480
Antidiuretic means anti release of urine,

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:12.780
and by shutting that off,

00:29:12.780 --> 00:29:15.970
you are going to cause
the release of urine.

00:29:15.970 --> 00:29:18.760
You're sort of allowing a
system to flow, so to speak.

00:29:18.760 --> 00:29:23.260
The second category of
thirst is hypovolemic thirst.

00:29:23.260 --> 00:29:24.760
Hypovolemic thirst occurs

00:29:24.760 --> 00:29:27.850
when there's a drop in
blood pressure, okay?

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:29.780
So the OVLT, as I mentioned before,

00:29:29.780 --> 00:29:31.360
can sense osmolarity

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:34.490
based on the fact that
it has these neurons

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:37.153
that can detect how much
salt is in the bloodstream,

00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:41.490
but the OVLT also harbors neurons

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:44.200
that are of the baroreceptor,

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:46.860
mechanoreceptor category.

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:50.860
Now, more on baroreceptors
and mechanoreceptors later,

00:29:50.860 --> 00:29:55.860
but baroreceptors are
essentially a receptor,

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:58.260
meaning a protein that's in a cell

00:29:58.260 --> 00:30:01.600
that responds to changes
in blood pressure,

00:30:01.600 --> 00:30:02.810
so there are a number of things

00:30:02.810 --> 00:30:04.960
that can cause decreases
in blood pressure.

00:30:05.910 --> 00:30:07.240
Some of those include, for instance,

00:30:07.240 --> 00:30:08.940
if you lose a lot of blood, right?

00:30:08.940 --> 00:30:10.640
If you're bleeding quite a lot,

00:30:10.640 --> 00:30:14.280
or in some cases, if
you vomit quite a lot,

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:16.460
or if you have extensive diarrhea

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:17.830
or any combination of those,

00:30:17.830 --> 00:30:20.730
and there are other things
that can reduce blood volume,

00:30:20.730 --> 00:30:23.290
and we will talk about
some of those later,

00:30:23.290 --> 00:30:26.653
but in the classic case
of hypovolemic thirst,

00:30:27.490 --> 00:30:29.480
one is simply losing blood,

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:31.210
and therefore, blood pressure goes down,

00:30:31.210 --> 00:30:33.090
so very simple to imagine in your mind.

00:30:33.090 --> 00:30:33.923
You have these pipes,

00:30:33.923 --> 00:30:35.370
which are the arteries,
veins, and capillaries,

00:30:35.370 --> 00:30:37.280
and when you lose some blood volume,

00:30:37.280 --> 00:30:40.010
the pressure in those arteries,
veins, and capillaries

00:30:40.010 --> 00:30:40.843
goes down.

00:30:40.843 --> 00:30:42.473
OVLT has neurons

00:30:42.473 --> 00:30:44.880
that can sense that
reduction in blood pressure

00:30:45.739 --> 00:30:48.323
because of the presence
of baroreceptors in OVLT.

00:30:49.580 --> 00:30:52.310
There are other elements

00:30:52.310 --> 00:30:54.470
that also play into the response

00:30:54.470 --> 00:30:57.610
to what we call hypovolemic thirst.

00:30:57.610 --> 00:30:58.553
For instance,

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:02.280
the kidney will secrete
something called renin.

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:05.090
Renin will activate something
called angiotensin II

00:31:05.090 --> 00:31:08.360
from the lungs, of all things, amazing,

00:31:08.360 --> 00:31:12.206
and angiotensin II itself can act on OVLT,

00:31:12.206 --> 00:31:14.330
organum vasculosum of
the lateral terminalis,

00:31:14.330 --> 00:31:18.840
which, in turn, will create thirst, okay?

00:31:18.840 --> 00:31:21.230
So in both cases, right?

00:31:21.230 --> 00:31:24.730
The osmolarity sensing system,
meaning osmotic thirst,

00:31:24.730 --> 00:31:28.680
and in hypovolemic thirst, where
blood pressure has dropped,

00:31:28.680 --> 00:31:31.740
the end result is a desire to drink more,

00:31:31.740 --> 00:31:33.350
and that desire to drink more

00:31:33.350 --> 00:31:34.860
comes through a variety of pathways

00:31:34.860 --> 00:31:36.440
that are both direct and indirect,

00:31:36.440 --> 00:31:38.830
include vasopressin and
don't include vasopressin,

00:31:38.830 --> 00:31:42.290
but I think, for just
sake of general example,

00:31:42.290 --> 00:31:43.200
and even for those of you

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:45.040
that don't have any biology background

00:31:45.040 --> 00:31:46.580
or physiology background,

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:49.970
just understand that there
are two main types of thirst.

00:31:49.970 --> 00:31:54.100
Both types of thirst, osmotic
thirst and hypovolemic thirst,

00:31:54.100 --> 00:31:56.260
are not just about seeking water,

00:31:56.260 --> 00:31:58.683
but they also are about seeking salt.

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:01.810
In very general terms,

00:32:01.810 --> 00:32:06.810
salt, aka sodium, can help retain water.

00:32:06.940 --> 00:32:10.140
Now, that doesn't mean that
salt always retains water.

00:32:10.140 --> 00:32:11.960
If you have excessive amounts of salt,

00:32:11.960 --> 00:32:14.030
will you retain excessive
amounts of water?

00:32:14.030 --> 00:32:14.870
Well, sort of.

00:32:14.870 --> 00:32:17.280
As we'll soon learn, it's all contextual,

00:32:17.280 --> 00:32:19.950
but for most cases,

00:32:19.950 --> 00:32:23.570
we can say that by having
salt in our system,

00:32:23.570 --> 00:32:25.830
our brain and our body
can function normally

00:32:25.830 --> 00:32:28.400
provided the levels of salt are adequate

00:32:28.400 --> 00:32:30.690
and not too high or too low,

00:32:30.690 --> 00:32:34.110
and thirst, while we often think of it

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:36.740
as just a way to bring
fluid into our body,

00:32:36.740 --> 00:32:39.070
is designed as a kind of

00:32:39.070 --> 00:32:41.610
a interoceptive perception.

00:32:41.610 --> 00:32:44.470
What I mean by that,
interoception, as many of know now

00:32:44.470 --> 00:32:45.550
from listening to this podcast,

00:32:45.550 --> 00:32:48.940
is a paying of attention
or a recognition, rather,

00:32:48.940 --> 00:32:50.080
a conscious recognition

00:32:50.080 --> 00:32:52.480
of the events going on within our body,

00:32:52.480 --> 00:32:54.140
so when we are thirsty,

00:32:54.140 --> 00:32:56.010
it's a certain form of interoception.

00:32:56.010 --> 00:32:58.630
We go, "Oh, I need something
or I crave something."

00:32:58.630 --> 00:33:00.250
You may not know exactly what you need,

00:33:00.250 --> 00:33:03.530
but when you are thirsty,
you're not just seeking water;

00:33:03.530 --> 00:33:07.040
you're also seeking to
balance your osmolarity,

00:33:07.040 --> 00:33:10.920
which means you may be
seeking salty fluids

00:33:11.770 --> 00:33:13.410
or foods, in some cases.

00:33:13.410 --> 00:33:15.520
You'll try and accomplish this by eating,

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:17.790
or it may be that you're trying to avoid,

00:33:17.790 --> 00:33:21.690
or you will be inspired to
avoid salty fluids and foods,

00:33:21.690 --> 00:33:24.700
but if you want to understand sodium

00:33:24.700 --> 00:33:25.810
and its roles in the body,

00:33:25.810 --> 00:33:26.980
you have to understand thirst,

00:33:26.980 --> 00:33:28.270
and if you want to understand thirst,

00:33:28.270 --> 00:33:31.320
you have to understand how fluid balance

00:33:31.320 --> 00:33:32.280
is regulated in the body.

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:34.320
That's not surprising at all,

00:33:34.320 --> 00:33:37.550
but sodium and water work together

00:33:37.550 --> 00:33:39.660
in order to generate what we call thirst.

00:33:39.660 --> 00:33:43.710
Sodium water work together in
order to either retain water

00:33:43.710 --> 00:33:46.920
or inspire us to let go
of water, to urinate,

00:33:46.920 --> 00:33:50.490
so before we can dive into
the specifics around salt

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:52.080
and how to use salt for performance

00:33:52.080 --> 00:33:55.470
and various recommendations
and things to avoid,

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:57.440
we need to drill a little bit deeper

00:33:57.440 --> 00:34:00.037
into this fluid balance
mechanism in the body,

00:34:00.037 --> 00:34:01.120
and for that reason,

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:03.130
we have to pay at least
a little bit of attention

00:34:03.130 --> 00:34:04.220
to the kidney.

00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:06.740
The kidney is an incredible organ,

00:34:06.740 --> 00:34:08.960
and one of the reasons
the kidney is so amazing

00:34:08.960 --> 00:34:13.080
is that it's responsible for
both retaining, holding onto,

00:34:13.080 --> 00:34:16.770
or allowing the release of
various substances from the body,

00:34:16.770 --> 00:34:20.500
substances like glucose or amino acids,

00:34:20.500 --> 00:34:22.103
urea, uric acid,

00:34:23.640 --> 00:34:26.170
salt, potassium, magnesium.

00:34:26.170 --> 00:34:27.620
It's basically a filter,

00:34:27.620 --> 00:34:31.150
but it's a very, very intelligent filter.

00:34:31.150 --> 00:34:33.530
I mean, intelligent meaning
it doesn't have its own mind,

00:34:33.530 --> 00:34:36.360
but the way it works is really beautiful.

00:34:36.360 --> 00:34:39.520
Basically, blood enters the kidney

00:34:39.520 --> 00:34:42.650
and it goes through a series of tubes

00:34:42.650 --> 00:34:44.350
which are arranged into loops.

00:34:44.350 --> 00:34:45.580
If you want to look more into this,

00:34:45.580 --> 00:34:48.180
there's the beautiful Loop of Henle

00:34:48.180 --> 00:34:51.120
and other aspects of the kidney design

00:34:51.120 --> 00:34:55.510
that allow certain
substances to be retained

00:34:55.510 --> 00:34:56.970
and other substances to be released,

00:34:56.970 --> 00:34:59.310
depending on how concentrated
those substances are

00:34:59.310 --> 00:35:00.143
in the blood.

00:35:02.230 --> 00:35:04.620
The kidney responds to a
number of hormonal signals,

00:35:04.620 --> 00:35:06.063
including vasopressin,

00:35:06.990 --> 00:35:09.250
in order to, for instance,
antidiuretic hormone,

00:35:09.250 --> 00:35:11.580
in order to hold onto more fluid

00:35:11.580 --> 00:35:14.083
if that's what your brain and body need,

00:35:15.030 --> 00:35:17.810
and it responds to other
hormonal signals as well,

00:35:17.810 --> 00:35:20.000
so it's a pretty complex organ.

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:23.620
Nonetheless, there's a key
point, which I already mentioned,

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:25.330
that I think most people don't realize.

00:35:25.330 --> 00:35:26.163
This is actually something

00:35:26.163 --> 00:35:27.730
that I like to tell kids when I meet them,

00:35:27.730 --> 00:35:29.240
provided that they're of appropriate age.

00:35:29.240 --> 00:35:33.060
I'll say, oftentimes, with kids
learn that I'm a scientist,

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:36.520
they'll ask a question about
something related to science,

00:35:36.520 --> 00:35:38.400
and hopefully, for my sake,

00:35:38.400 --> 00:35:40.550
it's something about neuroscience,

00:35:40.550 --> 00:35:41.940
but one thing that I'll tell kids,

00:35:41.940 --> 00:35:45.357
I'll say, "Do you know
that your urine, your pee,

00:35:45.357 --> 00:35:47.210
"is actually filtered blood?"

00:35:47.210 --> 00:35:49.520
And occasionally, that
will really terrify a kid,

00:35:49.520 --> 00:35:53.030
but that also occasionally
really terrifies an adult,

00:35:53.030 --> 00:35:55.520
but indeed, your urine is filtered blood.

00:35:55.520 --> 00:35:57.190
Basically, blood gets into the kidney.

00:35:57.190 --> 00:35:58.860
The kidney's going to
filter out certain things.

00:35:58.860 --> 00:36:00.320
Certain things are going to
be allowed to pass through

00:36:00.320 --> 00:36:02.510
and others are not, okay?

00:36:02.510 --> 00:36:05.240
So the way the kidney is designed is that

00:36:05.240 --> 00:36:09.530
about 90% of the stuff that's
absorbed from the blood

00:36:09.530 --> 00:36:12.483
is going to be absorbed early
in this series of tubes,

00:36:13.390 --> 00:36:15.090
and only a small percentage

00:36:15.090 --> 00:36:17.340
is going to be regulated or worked out

00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:19.340
as you get into what's
called the distal kidney.

00:36:19.340 --> 00:36:23.270
I mean, distal just means
the furthest part away, okay?

00:36:23.270 --> 00:36:25.540
The proximal is up close,

00:36:25.540 --> 00:36:26.373
so like your shoulder

00:36:26.373 --> 00:36:28.350
is proximal to your midline of your body,

00:36:28.350 --> 00:36:29.890
and your hand is distal,

00:36:29.890 --> 00:36:30.930
so in biological terms,

00:36:30.930 --> 00:36:32.040
you hear about proximal, distal,

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:34.360
which just means near or far from,

00:36:34.360 --> 00:36:36.150
so just to give a really simple example,

00:36:36.150 --> 00:36:39.970
let's say that you are very low on fluid.

00:36:39.970 --> 00:36:41.410
You haven't had much to drink in a while.

00:36:41.410 --> 00:36:43.483
Maybe you're walking around on a hot day.

00:36:44.380 --> 00:36:48.160
Chances are that the neurons in your OVLT

00:36:48.160 --> 00:36:51.340
will sense the increase
in osmolarity, right?

00:36:51.340 --> 00:36:54.530
The concentration of salt
is going to be increased

00:36:54.530 --> 00:36:58.170
relative to the fluid
volume that's circulating.

00:36:58.170 --> 00:36:59.240
This, of course, assumes

00:36:59.240 --> 00:37:00.890
that you haven't excreted a lot of sodium

00:37:00.890 --> 00:37:02.320
for one reason or another,

00:37:02.320 --> 00:37:05.930
but that increase in osmolarity
is detected by the OVLT.

00:37:05.930 --> 00:37:09.500
The OVLT is going to signal
a bunch of different cascades

00:37:09.500 --> 00:37:11.610
through the supraoptic nucleus, et cetera,

00:37:11.610 --> 00:37:13.730
and then, vasopressin
is going to be released

00:37:13.730 --> 00:37:14.860
into the bloodstream,

00:37:14.860 --> 00:37:18.380
and vasopressin, again, also
called antidiuretic hormone,

00:37:18.380 --> 00:37:20.680
is going to act on the kidney

00:37:20.680 --> 00:37:23.550
and change the kidney's function

00:37:23.550 --> 00:37:24.940
in a couple of different ways,

00:37:24.940 --> 00:37:28.240
some mechanical, some chemical, okay?

00:37:28.240 --> 00:37:30.660
In order to make sure that your kidney

00:37:30.660 --> 00:37:32.930
does not release much water,

00:37:32.930 --> 00:37:35.300
doesn't make you want to urinate,

00:37:35.300 --> 00:37:37.350
and in fact, even if you
would try to urinate,

00:37:37.350 --> 00:37:40.210
your body's going to tend to
hold onto its fluid stores.

00:37:40.210 --> 00:37:43.000
So very simple, straightforward example.

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:44.400
We can also give the other example

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:47.860
whereby if you are ingesting
a lot, a lot, a lot of water,

00:37:47.860 --> 00:37:49.863
and it's not a particularly hot day,

00:37:49.863 --> 00:37:51.490
and you're not sweating very much,

00:37:51.490 --> 00:37:53.490
let's assume your salt intake is constant

00:37:53.490 --> 00:37:55.790
or is low for whatever reason,

00:37:55.790 --> 00:37:57.750
well, then, the osmolarity,

00:37:57.750 --> 00:37:59.560
the salt concentration in your blood,

00:37:59.560 --> 00:38:00.393
is going to be lower.

00:38:00.393 --> 00:38:02.620
Your OVLT will detect that

00:38:02.620 --> 00:38:06.840
because of these osmosensing
neurons in your OVLT.

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:11.090
Your OVLT will fail to signal

00:38:11.090 --> 00:38:13.140
to the supraoptic nucleus,

00:38:13.140 --> 00:38:14.840
and there will not be the release

00:38:14.840 --> 00:38:16.960
of vasopressin antidiuretic hormone,

00:38:16.960 --> 00:38:20.020
and you can excrete all the water

00:38:20.020 --> 00:38:22.220
that your body wants to excrete,

00:38:22.220 --> 00:38:23.490
meaning you'll be able to urinate.

00:38:23.490 --> 00:38:24.810
There's no holding onto water

00:38:24.810 --> 00:38:26.130
at the level of the kidney, okay?

00:38:26.130 --> 00:38:27.180
Very simple examples,

00:38:27.180 --> 00:38:29.880
but hopefully, it illustrates
how events within the blood,

00:38:29.880 --> 00:38:31.200
meaning the concentration of salt

00:38:31.200 --> 00:38:33.580
relative to the amount of fluid, right?

00:38:33.580 --> 00:38:37.400
That's what osmolarity is,
is detected by the OVLT.

00:38:37.400 --> 00:38:39.970
The brain then communicates
to the pituitary.

00:38:39.970 --> 00:38:42.150
The pituitary sends a
hormone out into the blood,

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:44.270
and the hormone acts on the kidney

00:38:44.270 --> 00:38:48.430
to either hold onto or let go of fluid,

00:38:48.430 --> 00:38:51.090
meaning to prevent you
from wanting to urinate

00:38:51.090 --> 00:38:54.170
or from stimulating
you to want to urinate.

00:38:54.170 --> 00:38:57.630
Very, very simple kind of
yes/no-type situation here.

00:38:57.630 --> 00:38:59.560
There's a lot of nuance
to this in reality.

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:01.140
There are a lot of other
hormones in this pathway,

00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:04.140
but I think, for at least
this stage of the discussion,

00:39:04.140 --> 00:39:05.340
this should be sufficient.

00:39:05.340 --> 00:39:06.930
Some of you may have noticed

00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:09.380
that a molecule we've been
talking a lot about today,

00:39:09.380 --> 00:39:11.870
vasopressin, was also mentioned

00:39:11.870 --> 00:39:14.000
on a previous episode of
the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:39:14.000 --> 00:39:16.130
but in a very different context.

00:39:16.130 --> 00:39:18.330
The molecule I'm referring
to is vasopressin,

00:39:18.330 --> 00:39:19.720
and, as I mentioned,

00:39:19.720 --> 00:39:23.680
it's a hormone involved in antidiuresis,

00:39:23.680 --> 00:39:26.260
meaning preventing urination.

00:39:26.260 --> 00:39:28.190
It's an antidiuretic,

00:39:28.190 --> 00:39:30.560
but we also talked about vasopressin

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:33.800
in the context of desire,
love, and attachment.

00:39:33.800 --> 00:39:34.633
We talked about it

00:39:34.633 --> 00:39:37.170
in the context of monogamy and nonmonogamy

00:39:37.170 --> 00:39:39.960
in a species of animal
called the prairie vole.

00:39:39.960 --> 00:39:41.880
You can check out that episode.

00:39:41.880 --> 00:39:45.920
I believe vasopressin in the
nonmonogamous prairie voles

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:48.400
are mentioned in the timestamp,

00:39:48.400 --> 00:39:50.600
so it should be easy to find.

00:39:50.600 --> 00:39:52.100
Vasopressin is made

00:39:52.100 --> 00:39:54.630
at multiple locations
in the nervous system,

00:39:54.630 --> 00:39:56.790
mainly the supraoptic nucleus,

00:39:56.790 --> 00:39:59.290
and indeed, it's also involved

00:39:59.290 --> 00:40:03.210
in aspects of sexual behavior and mating.

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:05.740
Now, it does that through mechanisms

00:40:05.740 --> 00:40:08.963
that are distinct from
its antidiuretic effects.

00:40:10.520 --> 00:40:12.760
In fact, there are people

00:40:12.760 --> 00:40:16.500
who take vasopressin as an aphrodisiac.

00:40:16.500 --> 00:40:19.130
Now, I'm certainly not
suggesting people do that,

00:40:19.130 --> 00:40:22.360
but I have all the confidence in the world

00:40:22.360 --> 00:40:23.850
that the moment I talk about vasopressin,

00:40:23.850 --> 00:40:24.957
someone in the comments is going to say,

00:40:24.957 --> 00:40:26.907
"What do you think about
vasopressin nasal sprays

00:40:26.907 --> 00:40:28.180
"and this kind of thing?"

00:40:28.180 --> 00:40:30.580
Vasopressin, and indeed oxytocin,

00:40:30.580 --> 00:40:33.870
another hormone that's
involved in pair bonding

00:40:33.870 --> 00:40:36.560
and various aspects of
brain and body function,

00:40:36.560 --> 00:40:39.330
are available as nasal sprays

00:40:39.330 --> 00:40:42.280
that can get up into the
deep recesses of the brain

00:40:42.280 --> 00:40:44.610
and can impact some of these core

00:40:44.610 --> 00:40:46.130
what we call hypothalamic functions,

00:40:46.130 --> 00:40:48.600
these primitive drives and
hypothalamic functions.

00:40:48.600 --> 00:40:51.750
I would encourage a lot of caution,

00:40:51.750 --> 00:40:54.520
maybe even extreme caution
in recreational use

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:56.760
of things like vasopressin and oxytocin

00:40:56.760 --> 00:40:58.730
unless you are working with MD,

00:40:58.730 --> 00:40:59.770
an MD, excuse me,

00:40:59.770 --> 00:41:00.603
and they prescribe it

00:41:00.603 --> 00:41:01.890
or they really know what they're doing.

00:41:01.890 --> 00:41:03.640
These are powerful hormones

00:41:03.640 --> 00:41:06.540
that have a lot of different
effects on the brain and body.

00:41:06.540 --> 00:41:09.900
The way that vasopressin,
meaning antidiuretic hormone,

00:41:09.900 --> 00:41:13.880
prevents the release of
fluid as urine from the body

00:41:13.880 --> 00:41:15.110
is pretty interesting.

00:41:15.110 --> 00:41:17.230
It acts directly on the kidney,

00:41:17.230 --> 00:41:19.740
so, as I mentioned before,
blood flows into the kidney.

00:41:19.740 --> 00:41:21.050
A number of things are retained

00:41:21.050 --> 00:41:22.900
in the early part of the kidney.

00:41:22.900 --> 00:41:25.580
Vasopressin acts at a fairly distal,

00:41:25.580 --> 00:41:29.235
meaning kind of end game part

00:41:29.235 --> 00:41:32.050
of the loops of tubes through the kidney,

00:41:32.050 --> 00:41:35.470
and it increases the
permeability of those tubes.

00:41:35.470 --> 00:41:37.520
In other words, it makes
sure that the fluid

00:41:37.520 --> 00:41:40.350
that would otherwise pass
into a collecting duct

00:41:40.350 --> 00:41:41.620
and then go out to the bladder

00:41:41.620 --> 00:41:43.990
never actually makes it to the bladder.

00:41:43.990 --> 00:41:47.130
I point this out because what
antidiuretic hormone does is

00:41:47.130 --> 00:41:49.150
it prevents the bladder
from filling at all.

00:41:49.150 --> 00:41:51.390
It's not as if it locks
fluid in the bladder

00:41:51.390 --> 00:41:52.890
and prevents you from urinating.

00:41:52.890 --> 00:41:54.787
I think the way I've been
describing things up until now

00:41:54.787 --> 00:41:57.040
and the way you'll hear
about antidiuretic hormone,

00:41:57.040 --> 00:41:59.380
it might sound like it kind
of locks up the bladder

00:41:59.380 --> 00:42:01.250
or prevents you from
being able to urinate,

00:42:01.250 --> 00:42:02.170
but you have a full bladder.

00:42:02.170 --> 00:42:03.220
That would be very uncomfortable.

00:42:03.220 --> 00:42:04.430
That's not the way it works.

00:42:04.430 --> 00:42:06.740
It actually causes the tubes

00:42:06.740 --> 00:42:09.340
headed towards the bladder from the kidney

00:42:09.340 --> 00:42:10.400
to become permeable,

00:42:10.400 --> 00:42:14.380
meaning to allow fluid to go
back into the bloodstream,

00:42:14.380 --> 00:42:15.340
into the rest of the body,

00:42:15.340 --> 00:42:17.270
so that fluid never
actually fills the bladder,

00:42:17.270 --> 00:42:19.440
and so you never feel the urge to urinate.

00:42:19.440 --> 00:42:21.203
Now, this is an episode about salt.

00:42:22.250 --> 00:42:23.950
A key thing to understand about the kidney

00:42:23.950 --> 00:42:28.430
is that the kidney uses sodium
in order to conserve water,

00:42:28.430 --> 00:42:30.430
which has everything to do with the fact

00:42:30.430 --> 00:42:33.023
that sodium can actually hold water.

00:42:33.960 --> 00:42:37.690
Put differently, water
tends to follow sodium,

00:42:37.690 --> 00:42:40.570
so where we have sodium,
we tend to have water,

00:42:40.570 --> 00:42:43.740
and sodium, when it's concentrated,

00:42:43.740 --> 00:42:45.410
can hold onto water,

00:42:45.410 --> 00:42:46.810
and that's one of the main ways

00:42:46.810 --> 00:42:49.300
that the kidney holds
onto water in the body,

00:42:49.300 --> 00:42:51.240
and as we'll soon learn,

00:42:51.240 --> 00:42:54.937
there is no simple and direct
formula to say, for instance,

00:42:54.937 --> 00:42:59.637
"Okay, if salt levels are high,
a lot of water is retained,

00:42:59.637 --> 00:43:04.260
"and if salt levels are low,
a lot of water is released."

00:43:04.260 --> 00:43:06.340
On the one hand, that can be true,

00:43:06.340 --> 00:43:07.800
but it's also the case

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:09.860
because these systems are homeostatic,

00:43:09.860 --> 00:43:11.850
meaning they're always seeking balance,

00:43:11.850 --> 00:43:14.060
both within system,
within the salt system,

00:43:14.060 --> 00:43:16.970
and between systems, the
salt and water system.

00:43:16.970 --> 00:43:19.410
It's also the case, often,

00:43:19.410 --> 00:43:22.110
that if we have enough sodium,

00:43:22.110 --> 00:43:25.980
well, then we can secrete sodium
and some water will follow,

00:43:25.980 --> 00:43:30.680
or if we don't have enough
sodium, then, yes, indeed

00:43:31.670 --> 00:43:33.240
because we're not holding onto water,

00:43:33.240 --> 00:43:35.350
more fluid can be excreted,

00:43:35.350 --> 00:43:39.810
but if that condition of low
sodium lasts long enough,

00:43:39.810 --> 00:43:41.330
then we start to retain water

00:43:41.330 --> 00:43:42.567
because the body recognizes,

00:43:42.567 --> 00:43:46.150
"Ah, salt is low, and
water is being excreted,"

00:43:46.150 --> 00:43:49.350
and eventually, a system
will kick in to retain water,

00:43:49.350 --> 00:43:50.800
so I'd love to give you

00:43:50.800 --> 00:43:53.020
a simple black-and-white, yes-or-no answer

00:43:53.020 --> 00:43:54.460
for low sodium, high sodium,

00:43:54.460 --> 00:43:56.350
moderate sodium, and water balance,

00:43:56.350 --> 00:43:57.920
but it's all contextual,

00:43:57.920 --> 00:43:59.050
and when I say contextual,

00:43:59.050 --> 00:44:02.860
I mean, it will depend on
blood pressure, hypertension,

00:44:02.860 --> 00:44:04.790
prehypertension, if that's there,

00:44:04.790 --> 00:44:08.460
maybe normal tension, hormone levels,

00:44:08.460 --> 00:44:10.340
exercise, et cetera, et cetera.

00:44:10.340 --> 00:44:12.630
A pretty good example of how
complicated this can all be

00:44:12.630 --> 00:44:15.170
is one that some of you
may be familiar with.

00:44:15.170 --> 00:44:16.610
It's pretty well known

00:44:16.610 --> 00:44:19.750
that during certain phases
of the menstrual cycle,

00:44:19.750 --> 00:44:21.370
when estrogen and progesterone

00:44:21.370 --> 00:44:23.150
and other hormones are fluctuating,

00:44:23.150 --> 00:44:26.440
that water can be retained in the body.

00:44:26.440 --> 00:44:29.510
It's what's called edema,
or a swelling, sometimes,

00:44:29.510 --> 00:44:31.600
so the common assumption,

00:44:31.600 --> 00:44:32.860
and indeed, it can be true,

00:44:32.860 --> 00:44:34.840
that when estrogen levels are high,

00:44:34.840 --> 00:44:37.690
there's water retention in the body.

00:44:37.690 --> 00:44:39.830
Also, in males, if
estrogen levels are high,

00:44:39.830 --> 00:44:41.280
there can be water retention in the body.

00:44:41.280 --> 00:44:44.090
This is one of the reasons why athletes

00:44:44.090 --> 00:44:45.280
and, in particular, bodybuilders

00:44:45.280 --> 00:44:47.480
who take anabolic steroids
like testosterone,

00:44:47.480 --> 00:44:49.350
which can be converted into estrogens,

00:44:49.350 --> 00:44:50.870
sometimes they'll look,
they'll walk around,

00:44:50.870 --> 00:44:52.410
they look like they
were partially inflated.

00:44:52.410 --> 00:44:53.580
They look like they're going to pop,

00:44:53.580 --> 00:44:55.700
and it looks like a swelling of the skin,

00:44:55.700 --> 00:44:57.160
not just because they have large muscles,

00:44:57.160 --> 00:45:00.180
and that's not always,
but often, water retention

00:45:00.180 --> 00:45:03.510
due to testosterone
conversion into estrogen.

00:45:03.510 --> 00:45:05.900
Now, that all sounds consistent, right?

00:45:05.900 --> 00:45:08.340
Estrogen levels fluctuate
in the menstrual cycle.

00:45:08.340 --> 00:45:10.260
In males, where there's
an increase in estrogen,

00:45:10.260 --> 00:45:11.570
there's retention of water,

00:45:11.570 --> 00:45:14.880
but actually, estrogen acts as a diuretic,

00:45:14.880 --> 00:45:17.267
so one would think, "Okay,
when estrogen levels go up,

00:45:17.267 --> 00:45:19.240
"there should be a lot of fluid excreted,"

00:45:19.240 --> 00:45:21.320
but I bring up this example to point out

00:45:21.320 --> 00:45:23.610
that it's a very complicated
and dynamic balance

00:45:23.610 --> 00:45:25.920
between hormones and salt and fluid.

00:45:25.920 --> 00:45:28.560
You can't draw a one-to-one
relationship there,

00:45:28.560 --> 00:45:30.800
and that turns out to be
a very important point,

00:45:30.800 --> 00:45:32.350
and we can use that,

00:45:32.350 --> 00:45:34.470
not as a way to further complicate things,

00:45:34.470 --> 00:45:39.220
but as a way to understand
under which contexts

00:45:39.220 --> 00:45:43.300
less sodium intake or more
sodium intake can be beneficial,

00:45:43.300 --> 00:45:45.290
so that's where I'd like
to turn our attention now,

00:45:45.290 --> 00:45:47.120
so how much salt do we need?

00:45:47.120 --> 00:45:48.690
And what can we trust

00:45:48.690 --> 00:45:52.273
in terms of trying to guide
our ingestion of salt?

00:45:53.210 --> 00:45:55.370
First of all, I want
to be very, very clear

00:45:55.370 --> 00:45:57.560
that there are a number
of people out there

00:45:57.560 --> 00:46:00.550
that have prehypertension or hypertension.

00:46:00.550 --> 00:46:01.383
You need to know

00:46:01.383 --> 00:46:03.540
if you have prehypertension
or hypertension.

00:46:03.540 --> 00:46:06.210
You need to know if you
have normal tension,

00:46:06.210 --> 00:46:07.910
meaning normal blood pressure.

00:46:07.910 --> 00:46:10.147
Everyone should know their blood pressure.

00:46:10.147 --> 00:46:12.550
It's a absolutely crucial measurement

00:46:12.550 --> 00:46:14.440
that has a lot of impact

00:46:14.440 --> 00:46:17.400
on your immediate and
long-term health outcomes.

00:46:17.400 --> 00:46:19.470
It informs a lot about what you should do.

00:46:19.470 --> 00:46:21.620
Should you be doing more
cardiovascular exercise?

00:46:21.620 --> 00:46:24.280
Should you be ingesting more or less salt?

00:46:24.280 --> 00:46:25.330
Should you be adjusting

00:46:25.330 --> 00:46:27.360
any number of different lifestyle factors?

00:46:27.360 --> 00:46:28.780
So you need to know that,

00:46:28.780 --> 00:46:31.370
and without knowing what
your blood pressure is,

00:46:31.370 --> 00:46:34.450
I can't give a one-size-fits-all
recommendation,

00:46:34.450 --> 00:46:36.850
and indeed, I'm not going to
give medical recommendations.

00:46:36.850 --> 00:46:39.960
I'm simply going to spell out
what I know about the research,

00:46:39.960 --> 00:46:41.350
which, hopefully, will
point you in the direction

00:46:41.350 --> 00:46:42.990
of figuring out what's right for you

00:46:42.990 --> 00:46:45.540
in terms of salt and indeed fluid intake.

00:46:45.540 --> 00:46:47.030
There is a school of thought

00:46:47.030 --> 00:46:50.420
that everybody is consuming too much salt,

00:46:50.420 --> 00:46:52.520
and I do want to highlight the fact

00:46:52.520 --> 00:46:56.490
that there are dozens, if not
hundreds, of quality papers

00:46:56.490 --> 00:47:01.490
that point to the fact that a,
quote-unquote, high salt diet

00:47:01.510 --> 00:47:05.540
can be bad for various organs
and tissues in the body,

00:47:05.540 --> 00:47:06.623
including the brain.

00:47:07.850 --> 00:47:11.560
It just so happens that
because fluid balance,

00:47:11.560 --> 00:47:13.350
both inside and outside of cells,

00:47:13.350 --> 00:47:16.890
is crucial, not just for
your heart and for your lungs

00:47:16.890 --> 00:47:19.340
and for your liver and for
all the organs of your body,

00:47:19.340 --> 00:47:20.850
but also for your brain,

00:47:20.850 --> 00:47:23.300
that if the salt concentration

00:47:23.300 --> 00:47:26.190
inside of cells in your brain

00:47:26.190 --> 00:47:29.770
becomes too high, neurons suffer, right?

00:47:29.770 --> 00:47:32.080
They will draw fluid into those cells

00:47:32.080 --> 00:47:36.300
because water tends to follow
salt, as I mentioned before,

00:47:36.300 --> 00:47:38.970
and those cells can swell.

00:47:38.970 --> 00:47:41.220
You can literally get
swelling of brain tissue.

00:47:41.220 --> 00:47:44.150
Conversely, if salt levels are too low

00:47:44.150 --> 00:47:46.900
inside of cells in any tissue of the body,

00:47:46.900 --> 00:47:50.030
but in the brain included,

00:47:50.030 --> 00:47:54.870
then the cells of the
body and brain can shrink

00:47:54.870 --> 00:47:59.360
because water is pulled into
the extracellular space,

00:47:59.360 --> 00:48:00.620
away from cells,

00:48:00.620 --> 00:48:03.090
and indeed, under those conditions,

00:48:03.090 --> 00:48:05.310
brain function can suffer,

00:48:05.310 --> 00:48:08.060
and indeed, the overall health
of the brain can suffer,

00:48:08.060 --> 00:48:10.880
so there are many reports out there

00:48:10.880 --> 00:48:13.750
indicating, both in experimental models

00:48:13.750 --> 00:48:15.410
and, to some extent, in humans,

00:48:15.410 --> 00:48:17.240
that overconsumption of salt

00:48:17.240 --> 00:48:19.760
is bad for brain function and longevity,

00:48:19.760 --> 00:48:23.040
and yet there is also decent evidence,

00:48:23.040 --> 00:48:25.050
in both animal models and humans,

00:48:25.050 --> 00:48:28.240
that if salt consumption is too low,

00:48:28.240 --> 00:48:31.270
then brain health and
longevity will suffer,

00:48:31.270 --> 00:48:33.930
as will other organs
and tissues of the body,

00:48:33.930 --> 00:48:35.420
so, like most things in biology,

00:48:35.420 --> 00:48:38.730
you don't want things too high or too low.

00:48:38.730 --> 00:48:42.140
Now, I would say that the vast
majority of studies out there

00:48:42.140 --> 00:48:45.470
point to the fact that a high-salt diet

00:48:45.470 --> 00:48:48.540
is detrimental to brain
health and function.

00:48:48.540 --> 00:48:52.110
Most of the studies have
focused on that aspect

00:48:52.110 --> 00:48:55.483
of salt balance and its
consequences on brain function.

00:48:57.280 --> 00:49:00.970
One critical issue with many
of those studies, however,

00:49:00.970 --> 00:49:03.100
is that the high-salt diet

00:49:03.100 --> 00:49:06.280
is often coupled to other elements of diet

00:49:06.280 --> 00:49:07.870
that are also unhealthy,

00:49:07.870 --> 00:49:11.410
things like excessively high
levels of carbohydrates or fats

00:49:11.410 --> 00:49:14.060
or combinations of carbohydrates and fats,

00:49:14.060 --> 00:49:15.260
and so while I know

00:49:15.260 --> 00:49:16.970
there are many burning questions out there

00:49:16.970 --> 00:49:18.860
about how much salt one needs

00:49:18.860 --> 00:49:20.960
if they are on a low-carbohydrate diet

00:49:20.960 --> 00:49:24.310
or if they are fasting or
if they are on a vegan diet,

00:49:24.310 --> 00:49:26.900
there have simply not been many studies

00:49:26.900 --> 00:49:29.520
that have explored the low, moderate,

00:49:29.520 --> 00:49:31.470
and high-salt conditions

00:49:31.470 --> 00:49:34.330
on a backdrop of very
controlled nutrition,

00:49:34.330 --> 00:49:36.430
and that's probably reflective of the fact

00:49:36.430 --> 00:49:38.030
that there are not a lot

00:49:38.030 --> 00:49:40.250
of very well-controlled
nutrition studies out there.

00:49:40.250 --> 00:49:42.000
There are some, of course,

00:49:42.000 --> 00:49:44.120
but it's very hard to get people to adhere

00:49:44.120 --> 00:49:46.430
to nutritional plans in a very strict way,

00:49:46.430 --> 00:49:48.693
and to do that for
sufficient periods of time

00:49:48.693 --> 00:49:52.640
that would allow the various
health outcomes to occur.

00:49:52.640 --> 00:49:55.410
Nonetheless, there's
some interesting reports

00:49:55.410 --> 00:49:58.970
that indicate that the
amount of salt intake

00:49:59.880 --> 00:50:03.400
can indeed predict health outcomes

00:50:03.400 --> 00:50:06.380
or what we call hazardous events,

00:50:06.380 --> 00:50:09.260
things like cardiovascular
events and stroke and so forth,

00:50:09.260 --> 00:50:13.790
and what's interesting
is that indeed a lower,

00:50:13.790 --> 00:50:15.033
I'm not saying low, right?

00:50:15.033 --> 00:50:16.830
Because I don't believe
that you want your diet

00:50:16.830 --> 00:50:21.080
to be truly low in anything
except, perhaps, poison,

00:50:21.080 --> 00:50:25.860
but a lower-salt diet can reduce

00:50:25.860 --> 00:50:28.700
the number of these
so-called hazardous events,

00:50:28.700 --> 00:50:32.980
but it's a somewhat of a
shallow U-shaped function

00:50:32.980 --> 00:50:37.970
such that, yes indeed, a high salt intake

00:50:37.970 --> 00:50:40.940
can be very detrimental for your health,

00:50:40.940 --> 00:50:43.440
both in terms of
cardiovascular events, stroke,

00:50:43.440 --> 00:50:46.240
and other deleterious health events,

00:50:46.240 --> 00:50:48.210
but somewhere in the middle

00:50:48.210 --> 00:50:51.260
that actually sits quite to the right,

00:50:51.260 --> 00:50:54.430
meaning higher than what
is typically recommended

00:50:54.430 --> 00:50:55.830
for salt intake,

00:50:55.830 --> 00:50:58.790
can actually reduce the number
of these hazardous events,

00:50:58.790 --> 00:51:00.930
at least some reports point to that,

00:51:00.930 --> 00:51:02.170
and so I want to emphasize

00:51:02.170 --> 00:51:05.670
what one of those particular reports says,

00:51:05.670 --> 00:51:07.580
and I also want to be sure to counter it

00:51:07.580 --> 00:51:09.493
from the perspective of the context

00:51:09.493 --> 00:51:10.900
that that study was set in

00:51:10.900 --> 00:51:12.270
because, again, my goal here

00:51:12.270 --> 00:51:15.873
is not to give you a strict
set of recommendations at all.

00:51:15.873 --> 00:51:18.080
It's to point you to the literature,

00:51:18.080 --> 00:51:19.940
try and make that literature
as clear as possible,

00:51:19.940 --> 00:51:21.970
and allow you to evaluate for yourself,

00:51:21.970 --> 00:51:23.650
and I don't just say that to protect us.

00:51:23.650 --> 00:51:24.980
I say that to protect you

00:51:24.980 --> 00:51:26.770
because indeed you are responsible

00:51:26.770 --> 00:51:28.610
for your health and your health choices,

00:51:28.610 --> 00:51:30.353
so the paper that I'm referring to

00:51:30.353 --> 00:51:32.370
is a very interesting one.

00:51:32.370 --> 00:51:34.970
We, of course, never want
to put too much weight

00:51:34.970 --> 00:51:36.380
on any one report,

00:51:36.380 --> 00:51:39.420
but this is a paper that
was published in 2011

00:51:39.420 --> 00:51:41.940
in the "Journal of the
American Medical Association."

00:51:41.940 --> 00:51:43.277
The title of the paper is

00:51:43.277 --> 00:51:45.917
"Urinary Sodium and Potassium Excretion

00:51:45.917 --> 00:51:48.070
"and the Risk of Cardiovascular Events."

00:51:48.070 --> 00:51:50.500
We have not talked much
about potassium yet,

00:51:50.500 --> 00:51:53.620
but sodium and potassium
tend to work in concert

00:51:53.620 --> 00:51:54.790
in the brain and body

00:51:54.790 --> 00:51:55.850
in order to regulate

00:51:55.850 --> 00:51:57.600
various physiological functions in health,

00:51:57.600 --> 00:52:00.390
and we'll talk more about
potassium as time goes on.

00:52:00.390 --> 00:52:05.390
The key plot or set of data in this study,

00:52:05.395 --> 00:52:08.490
for those of you that want to
look it up, we will link to it.

00:52:08.490 --> 00:52:11.260
There are a lot of data
in here, but is Figure 1,

00:52:11.260 --> 00:52:14.240
which is basically evaluating

00:52:14.240 --> 00:52:17.300
the amount of urinary excretion of sodium,

00:52:17.300 --> 00:52:19.410
which is a somewhat indirect,

00:52:19.410 --> 00:52:21.230
but nonetheless valuable measure

00:52:21.230 --> 00:52:23.450
of how much sodium people were ingesting

00:52:24.330 --> 00:52:28.270
and plotted against that is
what they call the hazard ratio,

00:52:28.270 --> 00:52:31.300
and the hazard ratio points to

00:52:31.300 --> 00:52:33.560
the composite of
cardiovascular death, stroke,

00:52:33.560 --> 00:52:36.970
myocardial infarction, and
an infarct is an injury,

00:52:36.970 --> 00:52:39.900
and hospitalization for
congestive heart failure,

00:52:39.900 --> 00:52:41.730
and what it points to is the fact

00:52:41.730 --> 00:52:45.820
that the hazard ratio is low-ish

00:52:45.820 --> 00:52:49.780
at sodium excretion of
about 2 grams per day,

00:52:49.780 --> 00:52:52.970
but then continues to go down

00:52:52.970 --> 00:52:56.570
until about 4.5

00:52:56.570 --> 00:52:58.150
to 5 grams per day

00:52:58.150 --> 00:52:59.880
that, remember, this is sodium excretion,

00:52:59.880 --> 00:53:02.890
so it's reflective of how
much sodium was in the body,

00:53:02.890 --> 00:53:05.170
which is reflective of how
much sodium was ingested,

00:53:05.170 --> 00:53:09.120
and then, the hazard ratio
increases fairly dramatically,

00:53:09.120 --> 00:53:10.850
a very steep slope,

00:53:10.850 --> 00:53:14.540
heading anywhere from 7 to 8 to 10

00:53:14.540 --> 00:53:18.170
and out towards 12 grams of
sodium excretion per day,

00:53:18.170 --> 00:53:20.650
so the simplest way to
interpret these data

00:53:20.650 --> 00:53:24.850
are that at fairly low levels of sodium,

00:53:24.850 --> 00:53:27.690
meaning at about 2 grams per day,

00:53:27.690 --> 00:53:29.950
you run fewer health risks,

00:53:29.950 --> 00:53:31.900
but the number of risks
continues to decline

00:53:31.900 --> 00:53:34.560
as you move towards 4 and 5 grams per day,

00:53:34.560 --> 00:53:37.620
and then, as you increase
your salt intake further,

00:53:37.620 --> 00:53:40.890
then, the risk dramatically increases,

00:53:40.890 --> 00:53:42.130
so no study is holy,

00:53:42.130 --> 00:53:44.820
nor is any figure in any study

00:53:44.820 --> 00:53:47.790
or any collection of studies holy.

00:53:47.790 --> 00:53:49.020
Rather, we always want to look

00:53:49.020 --> 00:53:52.690
at what the bulk of data in
a particular field reveal.

00:53:52.690 --> 00:53:54.930
Nonetheless, I think the
plot that we described,

00:53:54.930 --> 00:53:56.460
meaning the graph that we described,

00:53:56.460 --> 00:53:57.570
is pretty interesting

00:53:57.570 --> 00:54:01.950
in light of the 2020 to 2025

00:54:01.950 --> 00:54:03.740
dietary recommendations for Americans,

00:54:03.740 --> 00:54:08.740
which is that people consume
no more than 2.3 grams,

00:54:08.800 --> 00:54:12.500
meaning 2,300 milligrams
of sodium per day.

00:54:12.500 --> 00:54:15.190
That's about 1/2 a teaspoon

00:54:15.190 --> 00:54:17.960
of salt per day.

00:54:17.960 --> 00:54:22.360
Now, most people are probably
consuming more than that

00:54:22.360 --> 00:54:26.420
because of the fact that they
are ingesting processed foods,

00:54:26.420 --> 00:54:29.680
and processed foods tend
to have more salt in them

00:54:29.680 --> 00:54:30.820
than nonprocessed foods.

00:54:30.820 --> 00:54:33.050
Now, of course, that's not
always the case, right?

00:54:33.050 --> 00:54:35.493
Sea salt is not a processed
food, in most cases,

00:54:36.400 --> 00:54:39.150
and there are a lot of unprocessed foods

00:54:39.150 --> 00:54:41.090
that can be high in sodium,

00:54:41.090 --> 00:54:42.830
but processed foods, in particular,

00:54:42.830 --> 00:54:43.830
tend to have a lot of sodium.

00:54:43.830 --> 00:54:46.410
You can see this simply by
looking at the packaging

00:54:46.410 --> 00:54:48.110
of any number of different foods,

00:54:48.110 --> 00:54:52.880
but if we are to take
this number of 2.3 grams,

00:54:52.880 --> 00:54:57.440
that's the recommended cutoff
for ingestion of sodium,

00:54:57.440 --> 00:55:01.110
it actually falls in
a portion of the curve

00:55:01.110 --> 00:55:02.820
that we were talking about a moment ago

00:55:02.820 --> 00:55:07.710
that indeed is associated with low hazard,

00:55:07.710 --> 00:55:09.570
low incidence of hazardous outcomes,

00:55:09.570 --> 00:55:11.780
cardiovascular event, stroke, et cetera,

00:55:11.780 --> 00:55:15.370
but the ingest, according to that plot,

00:55:15.370 --> 00:55:19.420
the ingestion of 4 or 5 grams of sodium,

00:55:19.420 --> 00:55:20.630
almost double,

00:55:20.630 --> 00:55:24.490
or more sodium than is
currently recommended,

00:55:24.490 --> 00:55:28.493
is associated with even lower
numbers of hazardous events,

00:55:29.330 --> 00:55:30.690
so we need to think about this,

00:55:30.690 --> 00:55:31.840
and we need to explore it

00:55:31.840 --> 00:55:34.520
in the context of other
studies, of course,

00:55:34.520 --> 00:55:37.950
and we need to evaluate
it in terms of this thing

00:55:37.950 --> 00:55:40.400
that we've been going
back to again and again,

00:55:40.400 --> 00:55:42.400
which is context, right?

00:55:42.400 --> 00:55:46.570
These recommendations of
2.3 gram per day cutoff

00:55:46.570 --> 00:55:49.530
is in the context of a
landscape where some people

00:55:49.530 --> 00:55:53.450
do indeed have hypertension
or prehypertension.

00:55:53.450 --> 00:55:55.800
The incidence of hypertension
has gone up dramatically

00:55:55.800 --> 00:55:57.450
in the last 100 years

00:55:57.450 --> 00:56:00.120
and seems to continue to go up.

00:56:00.120 --> 00:56:04.600
Whether or not that's because
of increased salt intake

00:56:04.600 --> 00:56:07.330
or whether or not it's because
of increased salt intake

00:56:07.330 --> 00:56:10.430
and other things such as
highly processed foods,

00:56:10.430 --> 00:56:11.300
that isn't clear,

00:56:11.300 --> 00:56:13.160
again, pointing to the challenge

00:56:13.160 --> 00:56:14.920
in doing these epidemiological studies

00:56:14.920 --> 00:56:17.290
and really parsing what aspects

00:56:17.290 --> 00:56:20.920
of a change in some health
metric is due to, for instance,

00:56:20.920 --> 00:56:23.120
the ingestion of more
sugars versus more salts

00:56:23.120 --> 00:56:25.760
or simply because of the
ingestion of more salts.

00:56:25.760 --> 00:56:29.430
It's a complicated, almost
barbed-wire topic by now,

00:56:29.430 --> 00:56:32.550
but we can start to pull
apart that barbed wire tangle

00:56:32.550 --> 00:56:35.377
and start to evaluate
some of the other people

00:56:35.377 --> 00:56:40.377
and other conditions that
exist out there, maybe for you,

00:56:40.560 --> 00:56:43.790
that actually warrant more sodium intake

00:56:43.790 --> 00:56:46.720
and where more sodium intake
might actually be beneficial,

00:56:46.720 --> 00:56:48.630
so, again, I want to be very, very clear

00:56:48.630 --> 00:56:50.140
that you need to know your blood pressure.

00:56:50.140 --> 00:56:52.860
If you have high blood pressure
or you're prehypertensive,

00:56:52.860 --> 00:56:54.460
you should be especially cautious

00:56:54.460 --> 00:56:57.180
about doing anything that
increases your blood pressure,

00:56:57.180 --> 00:56:59.410
and, as always, you want to,
of course, talk to your doctor

00:56:59.410 --> 00:57:00.243
about doing anything

00:57:00.243 --> 00:57:02.700
that could adjust your
health in any direction,

00:57:02.700 --> 00:57:05.740
but nonetheless, there
are some important papers

00:57:05.740 --> 00:57:08.230
that have been published in recent years.

00:57:08.230 --> 00:57:10.150
I want to point to one
of them, in particular.

00:57:10.150 --> 00:57:12.390
This is a paper that was published

00:57:12.390 --> 00:57:15.990
in the journal "Autonomic
Neuroscience: Basic and Clinical"

00:57:15.990 --> 00:57:19.520
because this paper, like
several other papers,

00:57:19.520 --> 00:57:20.610
asked the question,

00:57:20.610 --> 00:57:22.430
and indeed, they ask the
question in the title.

00:57:22.430 --> 00:57:24.547
It's a review: "Dietary Sodium and Health:

00:57:24.547 --> 00:57:28.690
"How Much Is Too Much for Those
with Orthostatic Disorders."

00:57:28.690 --> 00:57:30.230
Now, orthostatic disorders

00:57:30.230 --> 00:57:31.540
come in a bunch of different varieties,

00:57:31.540 --> 00:57:33.490
and we're going to talk
about those in a moment,

00:57:33.490 --> 00:57:35.290
but there are a number of people out there

00:57:35.290 --> 00:57:37.420
that have low blood pressure, right?

00:57:37.420 --> 00:57:40.010
People that get dizzy when they stand up,

00:57:40.010 --> 00:57:42.820
people that are feeling
chronically fatigued,

00:57:42.820 --> 00:57:45.000
and in some cases, not all,

00:57:45.000 --> 00:57:46.560
those groups can actually benefit

00:57:46.560 --> 00:57:48.670
from increasing their sodium intake.

00:57:48.670 --> 00:57:51.260
Several episodes ago on
the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:57:51.260 --> 00:57:55.070
I gave a, what, it's just
clearly what we call anec-data,

00:57:55.070 --> 00:57:56.330
which is not even really data.

00:57:56.330 --> 00:58:00.470
It's just anecdotal data of an individual

00:58:00.470 --> 00:58:03.630
who was always feeling
hungry and craving sugar,

00:58:03.630 --> 00:58:07.520
and based on the fact that they
also had low blood pressure,

00:58:07.520 --> 00:58:09.090
I had them talk to a physician,

00:58:09.090 --> 00:58:10.130
and they got permission

00:58:10.130 --> 00:58:13.020
to try a little
mini-experiment on themselves,

00:58:13.020 --> 00:58:15.060
and so they did, and
that mini-experiment was

00:58:15.060 --> 00:58:17.690
anytime they felt like
they were craving sugar

00:58:17.690 --> 00:58:19.440
or they were feeling a
little lightheaded and dizzy,

00:58:19.440 --> 00:58:22.190
rather than reaching for
something with caloric intake,

00:58:22.190 --> 00:58:23.470
they took a little bit of sea salt,

00:58:23.470 --> 00:58:25.070
a little pinch of sea salt,

00:58:25.070 --> 00:58:27.460
and put it into some water, and drank it,

00:58:27.460 --> 00:58:29.680
or, in the case of this individual,

00:58:29.680 --> 00:58:31.300
they would actually take
a little sea salt packet,

00:58:31.300 --> 00:58:33.090
and they would actually
just down a sea salt packet,

00:58:33.090 --> 00:58:35.760
and for them, that
provided tremendous relief

00:58:35.760 --> 00:58:37.660
for their dizziness,

00:58:37.660 --> 00:58:39.300
but that, of course, was in the context

00:58:39.300 --> 00:58:42.720
of somewhat abnormally low blood pressure,

00:58:42.720 --> 00:58:44.640
so I don't think that they are alone

00:58:44.640 --> 00:58:46.470
in the fact that many people out there

00:58:46.470 --> 00:58:48.993
suffer from a low blood
pressure condition.

00:58:50.090 --> 00:58:51.030
Many people out there

00:58:51.030 --> 00:58:52.750
suffer from a high blood
pressure condition,

00:58:52.750 --> 00:58:53.920
so know your blood pressure,

00:58:53.920 --> 00:58:56.850
and understand that
blood pressure, in part,

00:58:56.850 --> 00:59:00.730
is regulated by your sodium
intake and your sodium balance.

00:59:00.730 --> 00:59:01.563
Why?

00:59:01.563 --> 00:59:03.880
Well, because of the osmolarity of blood

00:59:03.880 --> 00:59:05.380
that we talked about before,

00:59:05.380 --> 00:59:09.900
where if you have a certain
concentration of sodium,

00:59:09.900 --> 00:59:12.170
meaning sufficient sodium
in your bloodstream,

00:59:12.170 --> 00:59:14.020
that will tend to draw
water into the bloodstream,

00:59:14.020 --> 00:59:14.853
and essentially,

00:59:14.853 --> 00:59:17.620
the pipes that are your
capillaries, arteries, and veins

00:59:17.620 --> 00:59:18.630
will be full.

00:59:18.630 --> 00:59:20.550
The blood pressure will
get up to your head,

00:59:20.550 --> 00:59:22.900
whereas some people, their
blood pressure is low

00:59:22.900 --> 00:59:26.070
because the osmolarity
of their blood is low,

00:59:26.070 --> 00:59:28.960
and that can have a number
of downstream consequences.

00:59:28.960 --> 00:59:32.410
I should also mention it can
be the consequence itself

00:59:32.410 --> 00:59:37.140
of challenges or even
deficits in kidney function,

00:59:37.140 --> 00:59:39.370
but all of these organs
are working together,

00:59:39.370 --> 00:59:41.100
so the encouragement here

00:59:41.100 --> 00:59:42.753
is not necessarily to ingest more sodium.

00:59:42.753 --> 00:59:44.240
It's to know your blood pressure

00:59:44.240 --> 00:59:47.890
and to address whether or not
an increase in sodium intake

00:59:47.890 --> 00:59:50.110
would actually benefit your blood pressure

00:59:50.110 --> 00:59:52.720
in a way that could relieve
some of the dizziness

00:59:52.720 --> 00:59:56.583
and other symptoms of things
like orthostatic disorders,

00:59:57.760 --> 00:59:59.810
but, of course, to do
that in a safe context

00:59:59.810 --> 01:00:00.920
and to never play games

01:00:00.920 --> 01:00:03.360
with your blood sugar
or your blood osmolarity

01:00:03.360 --> 01:00:04.380
that could set your system

01:00:04.380 --> 01:00:06.223
down a cascade of negative events.

01:00:07.170 --> 01:00:10.010
Let's look at what the
current recommendations are

01:00:10.010 --> 01:00:13.020
for people that suffer
from orthostatic disorders

01:00:13.020 --> 01:00:16.000
like orthostatic hypo,
meaning too low, tension,

01:00:16.000 --> 01:00:19.133
orthostatic hypotension,
postural tachycardia syndrome,

01:00:19.133 --> 01:00:23.270
sometimes referred to as POTS, P-O-T-S,

01:00:23.270 --> 01:00:26.850
or idiopathic orthostatic
tachycardia and syncope.

01:00:26.850 --> 01:00:29.063
These have the incredibly elaborate names.

01:00:29.960 --> 01:00:33.250
Those groups are often told
to increase their salt intake

01:00:33.250 --> 01:00:34.750
in order to combat their symptoms.

01:00:34.750 --> 01:00:36.720
The American Society of Hypertension

01:00:36.720 --> 01:00:41.310
recommends anywhere from 6,000 to 10,000.

01:00:41.310 --> 01:00:42.510
These are very high levels,

01:00:42.510 --> 01:00:45.870
so this is 6 grams to 10
grams of salt per day,

01:00:45.870 --> 01:00:47.470
keeping in mind, again,

01:00:47.470 --> 01:00:50.920
that salt is not the same as sodium,

01:00:50.920 --> 01:00:51.753
so that equates

01:00:51.753 --> 01:00:55.970
to about 2,400 to 4,000
milligrams of sodium per day.

01:00:55.970 --> 01:00:57.780
Again, if you want to
learn more about this

01:00:57.780 --> 01:00:59.190
and get more of the citations,

01:00:59.190 --> 01:01:01.130
I'll refer you back to this study

01:01:01.130 --> 01:01:02.427
on "Dietary Sodium and Health:

01:01:02.427 --> 01:01:04.840
"How Much Is Too Much for Those
with Orthostatic Disorders."

01:01:04.840 --> 01:01:07.770
We'll put a link to this
in the caption show notes,

01:01:07.770 --> 01:01:10.710
so that's not just in the U.S.

01:01:10.710 --> 01:01:11.700
The salt recommendations

01:01:11.700 --> 01:01:13.700
from the Canadian Cardiovascular Society

01:01:13.700 --> 01:01:16.010
are 10,000 milligrams of salt per day,

01:01:16.010 --> 01:01:19.160
so 4 grams of sodium is
what that equates to,

01:01:19.160 --> 01:01:20.410
and on and on and on,

01:01:20.410 --> 01:01:22.530
for things like POTS,

01:01:22.530 --> 01:01:25.330
for these postural
syndromes that result from,

01:01:25.330 --> 01:01:27.120
or I should say from these syndromes

01:01:27.120 --> 01:01:29.110
that involve low blood pressure

01:01:29.110 --> 01:01:31.550
when people stand up
or in certain postures,

01:01:31.550 --> 01:01:33.840
so I point out this paper,

01:01:33.840 --> 01:01:36.850
and I point out these
higher salt recommendations

01:01:36.850 --> 01:01:40.820
to emphasize, again, that
context is vital, right?

01:01:40.820 --> 01:01:42.250
That people with high blood pressure

01:01:42.250 --> 01:01:44.900
are going to need certain
amounts of salt intake.

01:01:44.900 --> 01:01:47.330
People with lower blood pressure,

01:01:47.330 --> 01:01:50.760
and maybe with some of these
postural orthostatic syndromes,

01:01:50.760 --> 01:01:53.180
are going to need higher amounts of salt,

01:01:53.180 --> 01:01:55.700
and for most people out there,

01:01:55.700 --> 01:01:58.220
you're going to need to
evaluate how much salt intake

01:01:58.220 --> 01:02:01.000
is going to allow your brain
and body to function optimally,

01:02:01.000 --> 01:02:03.680
and there are some fairly
straightforward ways

01:02:03.680 --> 01:02:04.560
to explore that,

01:02:04.560 --> 01:02:06.260
and there's some ways to explore that

01:02:06.260 --> 01:02:08.250
in the context of what you already know

01:02:08.250 --> 01:02:10.720
about thirst and salt appetite

01:02:10.720 --> 01:02:12.600
that can make that exploration

01:02:12.600 --> 01:02:14.070
one in which it's not going to be

01:02:14.070 --> 01:02:16.030
a constant wandering around in the dark

01:02:16.030 --> 01:02:18.280
and where you can figure
out what's right for you.

01:02:18.280 --> 01:02:21.560
For most people, a moderate
increase in salt intake

01:02:21.560 --> 01:02:23.080
is not going to be detrimental

01:02:23.080 --> 01:02:26.310
provided that you consume enough fluids,

01:02:26.310 --> 01:02:28.850
in particular, water, 'kay?

01:02:28.850 --> 01:02:31.150
Meaning if you happen
to overeat salt a bit,

01:02:31.150 --> 01:02:34.490
you will get thirsty, you
will ingest more water,

01:02:34.490 --> 01:02:36.440
and you will excrete the excess sodium.

01:02:37.940 --> 01:02:38.773
There is evidence

01:02:38.773 --> 01:02:42.580
that the body can store
sodium in various organs.

01:02:42.580 --> 01:02:44.130
That storage of sodium

01:02:44.130 --> 01:02:47.300
may or may not be a detrimental thing.

01:02:47.300 --> 01:02:49.900
In general, excess storage of sodium

01:02:49.900 --> 01:02:51.910
in tissues and organs
of the brain and body

01:02:51.910 --> 01:02:55.780
is not thought to be good
for long-term health,

01:02:55.780 --> 01:02:59.020
so eating much more sodium than you need

01:02:59.020 --> 01:03:01.773
for long periods of time
is indeed bad for you.

01:03:03.560 --> 01:03:06.300
Earlier, I mentioned that salt

01:03:06.300 --> 01:03:08.720
and your hunger and thirst for salt

01:03:08.720 --> 01:03:11.040
is homeostatically regulated,

01:03:11.040 --> 01:03:11.950
and indeed, that's the case,

01:03:11.950 --> 01:03:15.030
much like temperature is
homeostatically regulated.

01:03:15.030 --> 01:03:19.390
What that means is, if
you pay attention to it,

01:03:19.390 --> 01:03:21.930
if your salt levels are low,

01:03:21.930 --> 01:03:23.760
you will tend to crave salt

01:03:23.760 --> 01:03:26.163
and salty beverages and salty foods,

01:03:27.150 --> 01:03:30.730
and in most cases, you should
probably follow that craving

01:03:30.730 --> 01:03:33.070
provided those salty
beverages and salty foods

01:03:33.070 --> 01:03:36.200
are not bringing in a lot of other things

01:03:36.200 --> 01:03:38.900
or anything, ideally, that's bad for you,

01:03:38.900 --> 01:03:40.880
so I think it's fair to say

01:03:40.880 --> 01:03:43.010
that whether or not
you're vegan, vegetarian,

01:03:43.010 --> 01:03:44.640
carnivore, omnivore,

01:03:44.640 --> 01:03:49.320
that we should all try
to limit our ingestion

01:03:49.320 --> 01:03:51.550
of processed foods, a'ight?

01:03:51.550 --> 01:03:53.970
My read of the literature is that, sure,

01:03:53.970 --> 01:03:56.700
some processed foods are acceptable for us

01:03:56.700 --> 01:03:58.730
and aren't going to kill us outright,

01:03:58.730 --> 01:04:00.870
but that for most people in the world,

01:04:00.870 --> 01:04:02.380
eating fewer processed foods

01:04:02.380 --> 01:04:04.790
is just going to be a good thing to do,

01:04:04.790 --> 01:04:08.480
so following your salt hunger and thirst,

01:04:08.480 --> 01:04:12.180
in most cases, is going to be beneficial

01:04:12.180 --> 01:04:14.430
provided that it's in the context

01:04:14.430 --> 01:04:18.520
of eating healthy, nonprocessed
foods on whatever backdrop

01:04:18.520 --> 01:04:21.210
of nutritional and dietary
recommendations is right for you.

01:04:21.210 --> 01:04:24.630
I simply can't tell you what
to eat and what not to eat

01:04:25.490 --> 01:04:29.650
because I acknowledge the fact
that some people are vegans

01:04:29.650 --> 01:04:33.290
because of ethical reasons
related to animals,

01:04:33.290 --> 01:04:36.970
or some people are
vegans because of reasons

01:04:36.970 --> 01:04:40.060
related to the climate
and the environment.

01:04:40.060 --> 01:04:43.790
Other people do it for
specific health reasons.

01:04:43.790 --> 01:04:45.540
Likewise, I know plenty of people

01:04:45.540 --> 01:04:48.390
that eat meat and avoid
vegetables, believe it or not,

01:04:48.390 --> 01:04:49.840
and I know people that eat both,

01:04:49.840 --> 01:04:51.910
and they do this, often,

01:04:51.910 --> 01:04:53.620
each, I should say, all,

01:04:53.620 --> 01:04:56.110
citing literature that
supports their particular camp

01:04:56.110 --> 01:04:57.410
and their particular view.

01:04:57.410 --> 01:04:59.900
It's not a territory I want to get into,

01:04:59.900 --> 01:05:01.600
but with respect to salt intake

01:05:01.600 --> 01:05:05.783
and the fact that salt intake
is homeostatically regulated,

01:05:06.830 --> 01:05:09.130
it is the case that if
you're craving salt,

01:05:09.130 --> 01:05:10.810
you probably need it,

01:05:10.810 --> 01:05:13.110
so for those of you that
are sweating excessively,

01:05:13.110 --> 01:05:16.410
or even if you're in a
very hot environment,

01:05:16.410 --> 01:05:19.460
and you're not exercising,
and you're just losing,

01:05:19.460 --> 01:05:22.120
you're losing water and
salt from your system,

01:05:22.120 --> 01:05:24.610
remember, also, that you can
be in a very cold environment,

01:05:24.610 --> 01:05:27.760
very cold, dry environments
often go together,

01:05:27.760 --> 01:05:30.290
and you can be losing a lot
of fluids from your body,

01:05:30.290 --> 01:05:32.890
and you will crave fluids and
salt even though it's cold

01:05:32.890 --> 01:05:36.840
and you're not actually
noticeably perspiring,

01:05:36.840 --> 01:05:38.480
so if you're exercising a lot,

01:05:38.480 --> 01:05:40.840
if you're in a particular
cold, dry environment

01:05:40.840 --> 01:05:42.500
or a particular hot environment,

01:05:42.500 --> 01:05:43.830
you ought to be ingesting

01:05:43.830 --> 01:05:46.490
sufficient amounts of salt and fluid.

01:05:46.490 --> 01:05:51.000
A rule of thumb for exercise-based
replenishment of fluid

01:05:51.000 --> 01:05:54.020
comes from what I, some episodes back,

01:05:54.020 --> 01:05:56.700
referred to as the Galpin equation.

01:05:56.700 --> 01:06:00.400
The Galpin equation, I
named it after Andy Galpin,

01:06:00.400 --> 01:06:03.260
and I think that is the
appropriate attribution there.

01:06:03.260 --> 01:06:06.580
Andy Galpin is an exercise physiologist

01:06:06.580 --> 01:06:09.560
at Cal State, Fullerton, I believe,

01:06:09.560 --> 01:06:11.840
and he's going to be a podcast guest

01:06:11.840 --> 01:06:13.180
here on the Huberman Lab Podcast.

01:06:13.180 --> 01:06:15.080
He's an exceptional muscle physiologist.

01:06:15.080 --> 01:06:17.010
He also lives in the practical realm

01:06:17.010 --> 01:06:20.810
where he gives
recommendations about exercise

01:06:20.810 --> 01:06:24.320
to expert athletes as well
as the everyday person,

01:06:24.320 --> 01:06:26.440
so the Galpin equation
is based on the fact

01:06:26.440 --> 01:06:31.100
that we lose about one to
five pounds of water per hour,

01:06:31.100 --> 01:06:34.120
which can definitely
impact our mental capacity

01:06:34.120 --> 01:06:35.970
and our physical performance,

01:06:35.970 --> 01:06:39.620
and the reason that loss
of water from our system

01:06:39.620 --> 01:06:41.830
impacts mental capacity
and physical performance

01:06:41.830 --> 01:06:44.590
has a lot to do with, literally,

01:06:44.590 --> 01:06:46.700
the changes in the volume of those cells,

01:06:46.700 --> 01:06:47.930
the size of those cells,

01:06:47.930 --> 01:06:49.450
based on how much sodium is contained

01:06:49.450 --> 01:06:50.840
in or outside those cells,

01:06:50.840 --> 01:06:53.670
and something that I've alluded
to before on the podcast,

01:06:53.670 --> 01:06:55.750
and I'll talk about more in a moment,

01:06:55.750 --> 01:06:59.100
which is that neurons
signal to one another

01:06:59.100 --> 01:07:00.680
by way of electricity

01:07:00.680 --> 01:07:02.180
through something called
the action potential,

01:07:02.180 --> 01:07:04.150
and that actually requires sodium

01:07:04.150 --> 01:07:06.080
and potassium and magnesium,

01:07:06.080 --> 01:07:08.860
so the Galpin equation
suggests that we start exercise

01:07:08.860 --> 01:07:12.290
hydrated with electrolytes,
not just with water,

01:07:12.290 --> 01:07:13.530
so that means water

01:07:13.530 --> 01:07:18.240
that has some sodium,
potassium, and magnesium.

01:07:18.240 --> 01:07:21.460
There are simple, low-cost ways
to do that we'll talk about,

01:07:21.460 --> 01:07:23.930
and the formula for hydration,

01:07:23.930 --> 01:07:25.130
the so-called Galpin equation,

01:07:25.130 --> 01:07:30.130
is your body weight in
pounds divided by 30

01:07:30.220 --> 01:07:32.330
equals the ounces of
fluid you should drink

01:07:32.330 --> 01:07:34.220
every 15 minutes.

01:07:34.220 --> 01:07:36.340
That may turn out to be more fluid

01:07:36.340 --> 01:07:37.970
than you can comfortably consume

01:07:37.970 --> 01:07:39.900
during the activity
that you're performing.

01:07:39.900 --> 01:07:42.380
Now, the Galpin equation

01:07:42.380 --> 01:07:45.320
is mainly designed for exercise,

01:07:45.320 --> 01:07:49.110
but I think is actually
a very good rule of thumb

01:07:49.110 --> 01:07:52.290
for any time that you need
to engage mental capacity,

01:07:52.290 --> 01:07:53.863
not just physical performance.

01:07:55.580 --> 01:07:57.690
Your body weight in pounds divided by 30

01:07:57.690 --> 01:08:00.010
equals the ounces of
fluid you should drink

01:08:00.010 --> 01:08:00.950
every 15 minutes

01:08:00.950 --> 01:08:03.480
does not necessarily mean
you have to ingest it

01:08:03.480 --> 01:08:06.270
every 15 minutes on the dot,

01:08:06.270 --> 01:08:10.500
and I think many activities,
physical activities,

01:08:10.500 --> 01:08:12.480
but also cognitive activities

01:08:12.480 --> 01:08:16.420
like Zoom meetings or in-person meetings

01:08:16.420 --> 01:08:20.710
or lecturing or running or cycling

01:08:20.710 --> 01:08:23.370
are going to make it complicated

01:08:23.370 --> 01:08:25.690
to ingest the appropriate amount of fluid

01:08:25.690 --> 01:08:27.890
every 15 minutes on the dot.

01:08:27.890 --> 01:08:30.800
I'm not going to speak
for Andy, for Dr. Galpin,

01:08:30.800 --> 01:08:32.770
but I think he would probably agree

01:08:32.770 --> 01:08:35.990
that these are averages to shoot for,

01:08:35.990 --> 01:08:38.710
and that unless you're hyperneurotic,

01:08:38.710 --> 01:08:42.070
the idea is to make sure that
you're entering the activity,

01:08:42.070 --> 01:08:46.160
cognitive or physical,
sufficiently hydrated,

01:08:46.160 --> 01:08:47.700
and that throughout that activity,

01:08:47.700 --> 01:08:48.840
you're hydrating regularly,

01:08:48.840 --> 01:08:50.160
and it points to the fact

01:08:50.160 --> 01:08:53.190
that most people are
probably underhydrating,

01:08:53.190 --> 01:08:55.080
but not just underhydrating

01:08:55.080 --> 01:08:58.020
from the perspective of
not ingesting enough water,

01:08:58.020 --> 01:08:59.230
that they're probably not getting

01:08:59.230 --> 01:09:01.110
enough electrolytes as well:

01:09:01.110 --> 01:09:03.470
sodium, potassium, and magnesium,

01:09:03.470 --> 01:09:05.820
so I've said two somewhat
contradictory things.

01:09:05.820 --> 01:09:09.947
On the one hand, I've said,
"Follow your salt appetite.

01:09:09.947 --> 01:09:10.977
"Follow your salt thirst.

01:09:10.977 --> 01:09:12.807
"If you're craving salt,

01:09:12.807 --> 01:09:16.300
"ingest some salt until
you stop craving the salt."

01:09:16.300 --> 01:09:17.370
On the other hand,

01:09:17.370 --> 01:09:21.500
I've given you this fairly
specific recommendation

01:09:21.500 --> 01:09:23.730
based on the Galpin equation

01:09:23.730 --> 01:09:25.760
that you should ingest
your body weight in pounds

01:09:25.760 --> 01:09:26.900
divided by 30.

01:09:26.900 --> 01:09:28.480
That's how many ounces
of fluid you should drink

01:09:28.480 --> 01:09:29.680
every 15 minutes,

01:09:29.680 --> 01:09:31.090
which I'm guessing, for most people,

01:09:31.090 --> 01:09:32.030
is going to be more fluid

01:09:32.030 --> 01:09:34.370
than they're currently
drinking, on average,

01:09:34.370 --> 01:09:35.770
and so how could it be

01:09:35.770 --> 01:09:38.070
that you can have a
recommendation for what's optimal

01:09:38.070 --> 01:09:40.740
that's different than the amount

01:09:40.740 --> 01:09:42.050
that you would reflexively drink?

01:09:42.050 --> 01:09:43.440
And it has to do with the fact

01:09:43.440 --> 01:09:45.700
that a lot of the hormone systems

01:09:45.700 --> 01:09:48.510
like vasopressin antidiuretic hormone,

01:09:48.510 --> 01:09:50.210
other hormones like aldosterone,

01:09:50.210 --> 01:09:53.550
and a lot of the neural
and hormonal signals

01:09:53.550 --> 01:09:55.750
that govern salt and water balance

01:09:55.750 --> 01:09:58.060
are fairly slow to kick in,

01:09:58.060 --> 01:10:01.550
so, for instance, if you
eat a fairly salty meal,

01:10:01.550 --> 01:10:03.150
and you sense that salt,

01:10:03.150 --> 01:10:05.940
you'll probably, meaning you
detect it and perceive it

01:10:05.940 --> 01:10:07.490
because the food tastes salty,

01:10:07.490 --> 01:10:09.110
you'll probably want to drink

01:10:09.110 --> 01:10:10.540
a fair amount of fluid with it,

01:10:10.540 --> 01:10:13.730
whereas if some of the salt
is disguised by other flavors,

01:10:13.730 --> 01:10:15.110
something that we'll talk
about in a few minutes

01:10:15.110 --> 01:10:17.330
when we talk about the
neural representation

01:10:17.330 --> 01:10:20.250
of things like salty and sweet,

01:10:20.250 --> 01:10:22.710
well, then, you might not
notice that something's salty,

01:10:22.710 --> 01:10:26.260
and then, a few minutes or
hours after ingesting that meal,

01:10:26.260 --> 01:10:27.820
you might feel very, very tired.

01:10:27.820 --> 01:10:29.030
You might even wonder

01:10:29.030 --> 01:10:32.550
whether or not it's because
of some blood sugar effect.

01:10:32.550 --> 01:10:35.060
Maybe it's a crash in blood
sugar, you might think,

01:10:35.060 --> 01:10:36.700
or something else related to that meal,

01:10:36.700 --> 01:10:37.533
or maybe you think

01:10:37.533 --> 01:10:39.870
it's because of some
other event in your life,

01:10:39.870 --> 01:10:43.080
but actually, what has
happened is you're dehydrated

01:10:43.080 --> 01:10:44.210
because you didn't recognize

01:10:44.210 --> 01:10:46.110
that you needed to drink more fluids,

01:10:46.110 --> 01:10:50.120
so I want to acknowledge the contradiction

01:10:50.120 --> 01:10:52.790
in the idea that everything
is homeostatically regulated,

01:10:52.790 --> 01:10:55.820
and therefore you are
aware of what you need,

01:10:55.820 --> 01:10:58.520
and the counterargument that,

01:10:58.520 --> 01:11:00.480
ah, you need to follow
these strict recommendations

01:11:00.480 --> 01:11:02.600
is actually going to be
somewhere in between,

01:11:02.600 --> 01:11:04.590
and, of course, your body
and brain can start to adapt

01:11:04.590 --> 01:11:06.450
to certain levels of salt intake.

01:11:06.450 --> 01:11:09.060
There's now a fairly famous study

01:11:09.970 --> 01:11:12.350
that was done in Germany

01:11:12.350 --> 01:11:16.840
which looked at different
phases of salt intake,

01:11:16.840 --> 01:11:19.900
meaning they had subjects ingest

01:11:19.900 --> 01:11:22.610
either 12 grams of salt
per day or 9 grams per day

01:11:22.610 --> 01:11:26.270
or 6 grams per day for
fairly long periods of time,

01:11:26.270 --> 01:11:28.410
and they collected urine for testing.

01:11:28.410 --> 01:11:30.210
This was actually a very controlled study.

01:11:30.210 --> 01:11:31.270
I'm just going to paraphrase

01:11:31.270 --> 01:11:35.260
from the National Institutes
of Health report on this study

01:11:35.260 --> 01:11:37.960
because they did a very
nice write-up of it,

01:11:37.960 --> 01:11:41.430
and they say that a big
surprise of these results

01:11:41.430 --> 01:11:44.470
is that whatever the level
of salt that was consumed,

01:11:44.470 --> 01:11:48.670
sodium was stored and released
from the subjects' bodies

01:11:48.670 --> 01:11:52.460
in fairly regular weekly
and monthly patterns,

01:11:52.460 --> 01:11:53.890
meaning people tended to adapt

01:11:53.890 --> 01:11:55.860
to a certain level of salt intake,

01:11:55.860 --> 01:11:59.330
and then it led to a
fairly constant amount

01:11:59.330 --> 01:12:03.400
of salt retention and
urine fluid excretion,

01:12:03.400 --> 01:12:05.800
and that's because of
the various hormones,

01:12:05.800 --> 01:12:06.700
like aldosterone,

01:12:06.700 --> 01:12:08.730
which regulates sodium
excretion from the kidney,

01:12:08.730 --> 01:12:09.960
and glucocorticoids,

01:12:09.960 --> 01:12:11.440
which we'll talk about more in a moment,

01:12:11.440 --> 01:12:13.080
which help regulate metabolism.

01:12:13.080 --> 01:12:16.040
Glucocorticoids are released
from the adrenal glands,

01:12:16.040 --> 01:12:17.850
which ride atop the kidneys,

01:12:17.850 --> 01:12:19.380
and there's a very close relationship

01:12:19.380 --> 01:12:22.110
between the stress system glucocorticoids

01:12:22.110 --> 01:12:23.680
and the salt system,

01:12:23.680 --> 01:12:27.410
so the reason why your salt appetite

01:12:27.410 --> 01:12:30.720
isn't a perfect readout of how
much salt you should ingest

01:12:30.720 --> 01:12:33.180
and why it might be helpful to
follow some of these formulas

01:12:33.180 --> 01:12:34.260
like the Galpin equation,

01:12:34.260 --> 01:12:37.020
especially if you're engaging in exercise

01:12:37.020 --> 01:12:39.560
where you're going to be
perspiring, of course,

01:12:39.560 --> 01:12:41.890
is that your body will tend to adapt

01:12:41.890 --> 01:12:43.680
to a certain amount of
salt intake over time,

01:12:43.680 --> 01:12:45.210
and then, your appetite for salt

01:12:45.210 --> 01:12:47.560
won't necessarily be the best indication

01:12:47.560 --> 01:12:50.223
of how much salt you
should ingest or avoid.

01:12:51.670 --> 01:12:52.720
Before I move on,

01:12:52.720 --> 01:12:55.410
I want to really reemphasize the fact

01:12:55.410 --> 01:12:57.830
that inside of the Galpin equation

01:12:57.830 --> 01:13:00.320
there is that mention of every 15 minutes,

01:13:00.320 --> 01:13:02.960
and people have come back to
me again and again about this

01:13:02.960 --> 01:13:05.267
saying, "I can't drink that
much water every 15 minutes.

01:13:05.267 --> 01:13:08.127
"It's too much volume
of fluid in my stomach.

01:13:08.127 --> 01:13:09.430
"I can't run with that," et cetera.

01:13:09.430 --> 01:13:11.790
Remember, these are averages,

01:13:11.790 --> 01:13:13.310
so that's what you want to average

01:13:13.310 --> 01:13:14.660
around a particular activity.

01:13:14.660 --> 01:13:16.980
These not strict recommendations
where a buzzer goes off

01:13:16.980 --> 01:13:18.440
and every 15 minutes you have to chug

01:13:18.440 --> 01:13:21.330
that exact amount of
electrolyte-containing solution.

01:13:21.330 --> 01:13:24.231
Another key feature of the study

01:13:24.231 --> 01:13:26.050
that I was referring to before,

01:13:26.050 --> 01:13:27.050
which, incidentally,

01:13:27.050 --> 01:13:29.410
was published in the "Journal
of Clinical Investigation,"

01:13:29.410 --> 01:13:32.860
is that the body regulates
its salt and water balance,

01:13:32.860 --> 01:13:35.600
not just by excreting sodium, right?

01:13:35.600 --> 01:13:38.180
But by retaining or releasing water,

01:13:38.180 --> 01:13:39.510
and this is because of the relationship

01:13:39.510 --> 01:13:42.220
between sodium and water that
we were talking about before,

01:13:42.220 --> 01:13:44.660
and the advantage of this
mechanism, they state,

01:13:44.660 --> 01:13:45.840
here I'm paraphrasing,

01:13:45.840 --> 01:13:48.270
is that the long-term
maintenance of body fluids

01:13:48.270 --> 01:13:51.950
is dependent, is not as
dependent on external water

01:13:51.950 --> 01:13:53.210
as once believed, right?

01:13:53.210 --> 01:13:55.560
What this system probably evolved to do

01:13:55.560 --> 01:13:58.450
was to adjust to different
levels of sodium availability

01:13:58.450 --> 01:13:59.670
in the environment,

01:13:59.670 --> 01:14:02.640
and that raises a really key element

01:14:02.640 --> 01:14:06.310
of salt and its importance
in human history

01:14:06.310 --> 01:14:08.460
and human evolution and human health.

01:14:08.460 --> 01:14:09.640
Haven't talked too much about this,

01:14:09.640 --> 01:14:12.320
and there are several very good books

01:14:12.320 --> 01:14:13.930
about the history of salt.

01:14:13.930 --> 01:14:17.200
Salt was a very valuable

01:14:17.200 --> 01:14:19.670
and heavily sought-after substance

01:14:19.670 --> 01:14:21.650
throughout much of human history,

01:14:21.650 --> 01:14:24.880
so much so that there are
actually written reports

01:14:24.880 --> 01:14:28.720
of people being paid for
labor in the form of salt,

01:14:28.720 --> 01:14:32.008
and salt, when it's scarce,

01:14:32.008 --> 01:14:34.980
has been quite expensive in
certain regions of the world,

01:14:34.980 --> 01:14:38.460
especially regions located
further away from the sea,

01:14:38.460 --> 01:14:39.740
and a friend of mine

01:14:39.740 --> 01:14:43.380
who has deep roots within
the culinary community

01:14:43.380 --> 01:14:45.050
told me about traveling

01:14:46.421 --> 01:14:50.680
to some somewhat impoverished
areas of Europe some years ago

01:14:50.680 --> 01:14:53.040
and going into homes where

01:14:53.040 --> 01:14:56.840
in the middle of the kitchen
table, there was a fish,

01:14:56.840 --> 01:14:59.800
a salty fish hanging from a thread

01:15:00.670 --> 01:15:02.000
above the table,

01:15:02.000 --> 01:15:05.460
and that because of a lack of
availability of table salt,

01:15:05.460 --> 01:15:07.850
the common practice was to take any food

01:15:07.850 --> 01:15:10.060
that needed some salt
for additional flavoring

01:15:10.060 --> 01:15:13.240
and to actually rub that
food on this salty fish

01:15:13.240 --> 01:15:17.480
or to squeeze the fish a
bit onto the food substance

01:15:17.480 --> 01:15:19.230
in order to get salt from it,

01:15:19.230 --> 01:15:22.680
so that's a very kind of extreme example.

01:15:22.680 --> 01:15:24.540
Nowadays, we kind of
take salt for granted,

01:15:24.540 --> 01:15:27.670
and most of the discussion out
there is about excess salt,

01:15:27.670 --> 01:15:31.250
but, as I'm pointing out,
that salt, for a long time,

01:15:31.250 --> 01:15:34.490
has been a very sought-after commodity

01:15:34.490 --> 01:15:37.190
and one that people really
cherished for their health.

01:15:38.220 --> 01:15:40.680
In the episode that I did on metabolism,

01:15:40.680 --> 01:15:43.360
I talked about the relationship
between salt and iodine.

01:15:43.360 --> 01:15:45.030
If you're interested in iodine

01:15:45.030 --> 01:15:47.430
and whether or not iodized
salt or noniodized salt

01:15:47.430 --> 01:15:49.530
is best or required,

01:15:49.530 --> 01:15:51.330
I'd encourage you to
listen to that episode,

01:15:51.330 --> 01:15:53.670
which was about, again, metabolism.

01:15:53.670 --> 01:15:56.020
Some people may need more iodine intake.

01:15:56.020 --> 01:15:57.370
Some people perhaps do not.

01:15:57.370 --> 01:15:59.680
Some people might even want
to ingest things like kelp.

01:15:59.680 --> 01:16:00.970
Some people might not,

01:16:00.970 --> 01:16:02.560
so please listen to that episode

01:16:02.560 --> 01:16:05.060
if you're interested in
the iodine aspects of salt,

01:16:05.060 --> 01:16:06.610
which have direct impact

01:16:06.610 --> 01:16:08.910
on thyroid hormone and thyroid function,

01:16:08.910 --> 01:16:10.910
which, of course, relates to metabolism.

01:16:11.870 --> 01:16:13.750
Nowadays. there's a lot of interest in

01:16:13.750 --> 01:16:17.180
and even a kind of proliferation
of what I call fancy salts,

01:16:17.180 --> 01:16:19.320
so whether or not you should
be ingesting sea salts

01:16:19.320 --> 01:16:23.640
or common, whether or not
common table salt will suffice.

01:16:23.640 --> 01:16:26.310
In most cases, for what
we're discussing here,

01:16:26.310 --> 01:16:28.630
common table salt is fine,

01:16:28.630 --> 01:16:30.540
but I should point out that sea salt

01:16:30.540 --> 01:16:33.330
often contains other minerals,
which can be very useful,

01:16:33.330 --> 01:16:35.760
and we will do entire episodes
on those other minerals,

01:16:35.760 --> 01:16:37.640
so sea salt can contain

01:16:38.880 --> 01:16:40.680
dozens or more of minerals,

01:16:40.680 --> 01:16:43.720
some of which can be quite
valuable to our health,

01:16:43.720 --> 01:16:46.040
others of which are less important

01:16:46.040 --> 01:16:48.010
and only need to be
consumed in trace amounts,

01:16:48.010 --> 01:16:50.790
but you're not going to
get many minerals, if any,

01:16:50.790 --> 01:16:52.350
from common table salt,

01:16:52.350 --> 01:16:55.270
and that's why, in addition
to the pretty colors,

01:16:55.270 --> 01:16:57.050
and, perhaps, some people report

01:16:57.050 --> 01:16:59.110
that they actually taste better,

01:16:59.110 --> 01:17:01.960
some of these so-called
fancy salts or sea salts,

01:17:01.960 --> 01:17:03.160
you might want to consume

01:17:03.160 --> 01:17:06.210
a more advanced form of salt, if you will,

01:17:06.210 --> 01:17:07.043
although I suppose

01:17:07.043 --> 01:17:08.620
it's actually the more
primitive form of salt

01:17:08.620 --> 01:17:10.620
if it's actually the one
that comes from the ocean,

01:17:10.620 --> 01:17:12.550
so we've all heard about how excess salt,

01:17:12.550 --> 01:17:14.200
it's bad for blood pressure,

01:17:14.200 --> 01:17:16.440
damage the heart, the brain, et cetera.

01:17:16.440 --> 01:17:19.670
I do want to give some voice to situations

01:17:19.670 --> 01:17:22.910
where too little salt can
actually cause problems,

01:17:22.910 --> 01:17:25.550
and this has everything to
do with the nervous system,

01:17:25.550 --> 01:17:28.690
so without getting into
excessive amounts of detail,

01:17:28.690 --> 01:17:32.100
the kidneys, as we talked about before,

01:17:32.100 --> 01:17:34.810
are going to regulate
salt and fluid balance.

01:17:34.810 --> 01:17:38.400
The adrenal glands, which
ride atop the kidneys,

01:17:38.400 --> 01:17:41.200
are going to make
glucocorticoids, like aldosterone,

01:17:41.200 --> 01:17:43.360
and those are going to directly impact

01:17:43.360 --> 01:17:45.890
things like fluid balance,

01:17:45.890 --> 01:17:47.410
and, in part, they do that

01:17:47.410 --> 01:17:50.560
by regulating how much craving for

01:17:50.560 --> 01:17:54.570
and tolerance of salty solutions we have,

01:17:54.570 --> 01:17:56.620
and there's some really nice studies

01:17:56.620 --> 01:17:59.520
that have looked at
so-called adrenalectomies.

01:17:59.520 --> 01:18:00.840
Now, this is an extreme case,

01:18:00.840 --> 01:18:02.860
and it's typically done in animal models,

01:18:02.860 --> 01:18:05.880
but it illustrates the
role of the adrenals

01:18:05.880 --> 01:18:08.080
in salt preference.

01:18:08.080 --> 01:18:11.710
Basically, when the glucocorticoid system,

01:18:11.710 --> 01:18:14.130
meaning the release of
these particular hormones

01:18:14.130 --> 01:18:15.620
from the adrenal glands,

01:18:15.620 --> 01:18:20.140
is eliminated by adrenalectomy,
-ectomy means removal,

01:18:20.140 --> 01:18:23.100
then, the threshold for
what's considered too salty

01:18:23.100 --> 01:18:24.860
really shifts, okay?

01:18:24.860 --> 01:18:29.240
So typically, when the
adrenals are intact,

01:18:29.240 --> 01:18:31.460
a animal or a human

01:18:31.460 --> 01:18:34.230
will prefer a mildly salty

01:18:34.230 --> 01:18:37.940
to moderately salty
solution if given a choice,

01:18:37.940 --> 01:18:38.880
and at some point,

01:18:38.880 --> 01:18:41.030
it's so salty that it just feels aversive,

01:18:41.030 --> 01:18:42.790
just like taking a gulp of seawater

01:18:42.790 --> 01:18:44.190
is almost always aversive.

01:18:44.190 --> 01:18:46.300
I can't think of an instance
where it's not aversive,

01:18:46.300 --> 01:18:47.950
and actually drinking
seawater can kill you

01:18:47.950 --> 01:18:51.140
because of the high
osmolarity of seawater.

01:18:51.140 --> 01:18:53.750
You certainly don't
want to drink seawater.

01:18:53.750 --> 01:18:56.463
Under conditions where
the adrenals are missing,

01:18:58.270 --> 01:19:00.860
animals and humans will tend to prefer

01:19:00.860 --> 01:19:04.100
a higher sodium concentration fluid,

01:19:04.100 --> 01:19:06.130
and they will be willing to tolerate

01:19:06.130 --> 01:19:08.930
ingesting very high
concentrations of sodium.

01:19:08.930 --> 01:19:10.670
Now, that's a very crude experiment,

01:19:10.670 --> 01:19:13.940
and not one that you want
to do, I promise you,

01:19:13.940 --> 01:19:15.700
but I mention it

01:19:15.700 --> 01:19:19.210
because it illustrates the
very direct relationship

01:19:19.210 --> 01:19:20.790
between the stress system,

01:19:20.790 --> 01:19:22.790
which is the glucocorticoid system,

01:19:22.790 --> 01:19:25.040
and the salt craving system,

01:19:25.040 --> 01:19:26.910
and this actually makes sense.

01:19:26.910 --> 01:19:29.040
Earlier, as we were talking
about hypovolemic thirst,

01:19:29.040 --> 01:19:32.120
when there's a loss of
blood pressure from,

01:19:32.120 --> 01:19:34.450
usually due to a loss
of blood from the body,

01:19:34.450 --> 01:19:35.600
there's a salt craving

01:19:35.600 --> 01:19:37.470
in order to bring that
blood volume back up

01:19:37.470 --> 01:19:38.930
because by ingesting salt,

01:19:38.930 --> 01:19:41.050
you bring fluid into the bloodstream.

01:19:41.050 --> 01:19:42.510
You're increasing that blood pressure,

01:19:42.510 --> 01:19:44.840
and you can restore the blood that's lost.

01:19:44.840 --> 01:19:46.940
Now, there are many examples where,

01:19:46.940 --> 01:19:50.690
if sodium levels get too
low in the bloodstream,

01:19:50.690 --> 01:19:54.080
either because people are
ingesting too little salt,

01:19:54.080 --> 01:19:56.390
or they're ingesting too much water

01:19:56.390 --> 01:19:59.060
and therefore excreting too much salt,

01:19:59.060 --> 01:20:02.540
that it can cause stress and anxiety.

01:20:02.540 --> 01:20:03.670
There's some really nice data

01:20:03.670 --> 01:20:06.470
that point to the fact
that low dietary sodium

01:20:06.470 --> 01:20:10.790
can actually exacerbate
anxiety in animal models,

01:20:10.790 --> 01:20:12.190
and to some extent,

01:20:12.190 --> 01:20:14.730
there's evidence for
this in humans as well,

01:20:14.730 --> 01:20:16.823
and that should not come as a surprise.

01:20:17.770 --> 01:20:20.250
The whole basis for a relationship

01:20:20.250 --> 01:20:21.640
between the adrenal system,

01:20:21.640 --> 01:20:24.050
these glucocorticoids,
things like aldosterone,

01:20:24.050 --> 01:20:25.830
and the craving for sodium

01:20:25.830 --> 01:20:29.960
is that the stress system
is a generic system

01:20:29.960 --> 01:20:33.900
designed to deal with various
challenges to the organism,

01:20:33.900 --> 01:20:36.360
to you or to me or to an animal,

01:20:36.360 --> 01:20:39.933
and those challenges can
arrive in many different forms.

01:20:41.100 --> 01:20:43.310
It can be an infection. It can be famine.

01:20:43.310 --> 01:20:45.523
It can be lack of water and so on,

01:20:46.530 --> 01:20:47.530
but in general,

01:20:47.530 --> 01:20:50.580
the stress response is one
of elevated heart rate,

01:20:50.580 --> 01:20:52.030
elevated blood pressure,

01:20:52.030 --> 01:20:54.290
and an ability to maintain movement

01:20:54.290 --> 01:20:57.100
and resistance to that challenge, 'kay?

01:20:57.100 --> 01:21:00.160
I've said this before, but
I'll emphasize it again,

01:21:00.160 --> 01:21:03.180
there's this common misperception
that stress makes us sick,

01:21:03.180 --> 01:21:05.280
and indeed, if stress lasts too long,

01:21:05.280 --> 01:21:08.150
it has a number of negative
effects on our health,

01:21:08.150 --> 01:21:12.380
but more often than not, if
we're pushing, pushing, pushing,

01:21:12.380 --> 01:21:14.120
we're studying or taking care of somebody

01:21:14.120 --> 01:21:16.130
or traveling like crazy,

01:21:16.130 --> 01:21:18.660
we don't tend to get sick
under those conditions,

01:21:18.660 --> 01:21:20.860
but as soon as we stop,

01:21:20.860 --> 01:21:23.000
as soon as we reduce
our adrenaline output,

01:21:23.000 --> 01:21:25.750
as soon as we reduce our
glucocorticoid output

01:21:25.750 --> 01:21:26.800
from our adrenals,

01:21:26.800 --> 01:21:28.110
then we will get sick.

01:21:28.110 --> 01:21:29.660
That's a very common occurrence,

01:21:29.660 --> 01:21:33.330
and it's because stress actually
activates our immune system

01:21:33.330 --> 01:21:34.510
in the short term,

01:21:34.510 --> 01:21:36.970
so I'd like to try and dispel this myth

01:21:36.970 --> 01:21:39.450
that stress actually
suppresses the immune system,

01:21:39.450 --> 01:21:40.930
at least not in the short term.

01:21:40.930 --> 01:21:43.150
For long-term stress,
it's a different issue.

01:21:43.150 --> 01:21:45.340
You don't want long-term ongoing stress,

01:21:45.340 --> 01:21:47.140
especially of several weeks or more.

01:21:48.950 --> 01:21:50.840
Nonetheless, it makes sense

01:21:50.840 --> 01:21:53.480
that bringing sodium into the body

01:21:53.480 --> 01:21:56.910
would be at least one way
that we would be wired

01:21:56.910 --> 01:22:01.090
to counteract or to
resist stressors, right?

01:22:01.090 --> 01:22:04.290
Stressors being the things
on the outside coming at us,

01:22:04.290 --> 01:22:05.870
so it could be stressful relationships,

01:22:05.870 --> 01:22:09.593
stressful job situation,
again, infection, and so on.

01:22:11.450 --> 01:22:12.900
It's clear from a number of studies

01:22:12.900 --> 01:22:15.830
that if sodium levels are too low,

01:22:15.830 --> 01:22:19.600
that our ability to meet
stress challenges is impaired.

01:22:19.600 --> 01:22:20.433
Now, that doesn't mean

01:22:20.433 --> 01:22:23.920
to place your sodium
intake cosmically high,

01:22:23.920 --> 01:22:25.690
but it does point to the fact that

01:22:25.690 --> 01:22:27.630
if you're feeling anxious,

01:22:27.630 --> 01:22:29.310
perhaps from low blood pressure,

01:22:29.310 --> 01:22:31.060
which can also give symptoms of anxiety,

01:22:31.060 --> 01:22:32.330
as we talked about before,

01:22:32.330 --> 01:22:35.600
but even if it's independent
of low blood pressure,

01:22:35.600 --> 01:22:38.560
that slightly increasing sodium intake,

01:22:38.560 --> 01:22:40.610
again, I would encourage people to do this

01:22:40.610 --> 01:22:42.830
not in the context of
processed foods and drinks,

01:22:42.830 --> 01:22:44.130
but ideally, in the form

01:22:44.130 --> 01:22:46.290
of maybe a little bit
of sea salt and water

01:22:46.290 --> 01:22:48.560
or salting one's food a little bit more,

01:22:48.560 --> 01:22:52.030
that that can stabilize blood pressure

01:22:52.030 --> 01:22:55.480
and one's ability to lean
into stressors and challenges,

01:22:55.480 --> 01:22:58.410
and I say this because I
think that most people assume

01:22:58.410 --> 01:23:01.020
that adding salt is always bad,

01:23:01.020 --> 01:23:03.590
when, in fact, that's simply not the case.

01:23:03.590 --> 01:23:04.670
There are conditions,

01:23:04.670 --> 01:23:06.900
such as when we are
under stress challenge,

01:23:06.900 --> 01:23:10.090
when there is a natural
craving for more sodium,

01:23:10.090 --> 01:23:12.300
and that natural craving for more sodium

01:23:12.300 --> 01:23:16.140
is hardwired into us as a
way to meet that challenge,

01:23:16.140 --> 01:23:19.310
so it's hard for me to know
whether or not people out there,

01:23:19.310 --> 01:23:20.780
especially the listeners of this podcast,

01:23:20.780 --> 01:23:24.860
are getting too much, just
enough, or too little sodium,

01:23:24.860 --> 01:23:25.970
so I can't know that.

01:23:25.970 --> 01:23:27.290
I'm shouting into a tunnel here.

01:23:27.290 --> 01:23:30.333
You have to decide how much
sodium you are ingesting,

01:23:31.180 --> 01:23:34.100
but I think that there's
some, for most people,

01:23:34.100 --> 01:23:36.780
especially people who are not
hypertensive, prehypertensive,

01:23:36.780 --> 01:23:37.613
there's some wiggle room

01:23:37.613 --> 01:23:41.650
to explore whether more intake of sodium

01:23:41.650 --> 01:23:42.880
could actually be beneficial

01:23:42.880 --> 01:23:45.290
for suppressing some of
the anxiety responses

01:23:45.290 --> 01:23:47.310
that they might feel under
conditions of stress.

01:23:47.310 --> 01:23:49.110
Again, more studies need to be done.

01:23:49.110 --> 01:23:51.920
Certainly, more studies
in humans need to be done,

01:23:51.920 --> 01:23:54.437
but the relationship between
stress and sodium intake

01:23:54.437 --> 01:23:58.660
and the fact that additional
sodium intake may be beneficial

01:23:59.530 --> 01:24:03.200
and indeed, is naturally
stimulated by stress

01:24:03.200 --> 01:24:06.760
shouldn't be necessarily looked
at as a pathological event.

01:24:06.760 --> 01:24:08.080
I know when some people get stressed,

01:24:08.080 --> 01:24:09.330
they crave salty foods.

01:24:09.330 --> 01:24:12.190
That's actually a hardwired
biological phenomenon

01:24:12.190 --> 01:24:14.610
that you see, not just in
humans, but in animals,

01:24:14.610 --> 01:24:17.030
because this is a very primitive mechanism

01:24:17.030 --> 01:24:18.950
whereby your body is preparing

01:24:18.950 --> 01:24:21.870
to meet any additional
challenges and stressors.

01:24:21.870 --> 01:24:23.750
Now, we can't have a
discussion about sodium

01:24:23.750 --> 01:24:26.580
without having a discussion
about the other electrolytes,

01:24:26.580 --> 01:24:28.053
magnesium and potassium.

01:24:29.050 --> 01:24:32.200
Magnesium is important enough
and an extensive enough topic

01:24:32.200 --> 01:24:33.670
that we should probably
do an entire episode

01:24:33.670 --> 01:24:34.913
just on magnesium.

01:24:36.000 --> 01:24:37.960
For purposes of today's discussion,

01:24:37.960 --> 01:24:41.800
I just will briefly touch on
some of the forms of magnesium

01:24:41.800 --> 01:24:43.840
that we've discussed on the podcast before

01:24:43.840 --> 01:24:45.670
in different contexts.

01:24:45.670 --> 01:24:47.560
I want to emphasize that many people

01:24:47.560 --> 01:24:50.670
are probably getting enough
magnesium in their diet

01:24:50.670 --> 01:24:53.600
that they don't need to
supplement magnesium.

01:24:53.600 --> 01:24:56.800
Some people, however, opt
to supplement magnesium

01:24:56.800 --> 01:24:58.550
in ways that can support them,

01:24:58.550 --> 01:25:00.650
and there are many different
forms of magnesium,

01:25:00.650 --> 01:25:03.560
and just in very brief passing,

01:25:03.560 --> 01:25:07.240
I'll just say that there is some evidence

01:25:07.240 --> 01:25:11.370
that you can reduce muscle
soreness from exercise

01:25:11.370 --> 01:25:15.760
by ingestion of magnesium
malate, M-A-L-A-T-E.

01:25:15.760 --> 01:25:20.070
I've talked before about
magnesium threonate,

01:25:20.070 --> 01:25:24.290
T-H-R-E-N-O-A-T-E, magnesium threonate,

01:25:24.290 --> 01:25:27.150
for sake of promoting
the transition into sleep

01:25:27.150 --> 01:25:28.130
and for depth of sleep

01:25:28.130 --> 01:25:30.660
and perhaps, again, highlighted perhaps,

01:25:30.660 --> 01:25:33.020
'cause right now it's
mainly animal studies

01:25:33.020 --> 01:25:34.430
and ongoing human studies,

01:25:34.430 --> 01:25:35.770
but the data aren't all in.

01:25:35.770 --> 01:25:38.760
Perhaps magnesium threonate can be used

01:25:38.760 --> 01:25:42.950
as a way to support cognitive
function and longevity.

01:25:42.950 --> 01:25:44.160
That was discussed in the episode

01:25:44.160 --> 01:25:46.193
with Dr. Jack Feldman from UCLA.

01:25:48.110 --> 01:25:49.750
Typically, magnesium threonate is taken

01:25:49.750 --> 01:25:53.040
30 to 60 minutes before bedtime
in order to encourage sleep.

01:25:53.040 --> 01:25:54.660
You can go to our neural
network newsletter

01:25:54.660 --> 01:25:56.380
and look for the one on sleep,

01:25:56.380 --> 01:25:58.110
and you can see the recommendations,

01:25:58.110 --> 01:26:00.960
or I should say, the options for that

01:26:00.960 --> 01:26:03.170
because, again, you should
always check with your physician.

01:26:03.170 --> 01:26:05.420
Those aren't strict
across-the-board recommendations,

01:26:05.420 --> 01:26:07.610
and then, there are
other forms of magnesium:

01:26:07.610 --> 01:26:09.020
magnesium bisglycinate,

01:26:09.020 --> 01:26:12.680
which is a somewhat of an
alternative to threonate,

01:26:12.680 --> 01:26:14.570
not known to have cognitive
enhancing effects,

01:26:14.570 --> 01:26:18.690
but seems, at least on par
with magnesium threonate

01:26:18.690 --> 01:26:20.590
in terms of promoting transition into

01:26:20.590 --> 01:26:22.810
and depth of sleep and so on.

01:26:22.810 --> 01:26:25.620
There are other forms of
magnesium, magnesium citrate,

01:26:25.620 --> 01:26:27.550
which has other functions.

01:26:27.550 --> 01:26:28.900
Actually, magnesium citrate

01:26:30.280 --> 01:26:32.453
is a fairly effective laxative,

01:26:34.100 --> 01:26:36.160
not known to promote sleep
and things of that sort,

01:26:36.160 --> 01:26:37.615
so a lot of different forms of magnesium,

01:26:37.615 --> 01:26:39.830
and there's still other forms out there.

01:26:39.830 --> 01:26:41.750
Many people are not
getting enough magnesium.

01:26:41.750 --> 01:26:42.670
Many people are.

01:26:42.670 --> 01:26:44.103
Okay, that's magnesium.

01:26:45.330 --> 01:26:47.560
Anytime we're talking
about sodium balance,

01:26:47.560 --> 01:26:49.920
we have to take into
consideration potassium

01:26:49.920 --> 01:26:52.040
because the way that the kidney works

01:26:52.040 --> 01:26:53.850
and the way that sodium
balance is regulated,

01:26:53.850 --> 01:26:55.290
both in the body and the brain,

01:26:55.290 --> 01:26:57.100
is that sodium and potassium

01:26:57.100 --> 01:26:59.890
are working in close
concert with one another.

01:26:59.890 --> 01:27:01.410
There are a lot of
different recommendations

01:27:01.410 --> 01:27:03.840
about ratios out there,

01:27:03.840 --> 01:27:05.270
and they range widely

01:27:05.270 --> 01:27:09.780
from two-to-one ratio
of potassium to sodium.

01:27:09.780 --> 01:27:11.890
I've heard it in the other direction too.

01:27:11.890 --> 01:27:15.220
I've heard a two-to-one
sodium to potassium.

01:27:15.220 --> 01:27:16.540
The recommendations vary.

01:27:16.540 --> 01:27:18.640
One of the sponsors of
this podcast, for instance,

01:27:18.640 --> 01:27:22.000
LMNT, which I've talked about
in this episode and before,

01:27:22.000 --> 01:27:23.870
the ratio there is a gram of sodium

01:27:23.870 --> 01:27:27.530
to 200 milligrams of potassium,
60 milligrams of magnesium,

01:27:27.530 --> 01:27:28.840
so there, they've opted

01:27:28.840 --> 01:27:32.430
for a five-to-one ratio

01:27:32.430 --> 01:27:34.193
of sodium to potassium,

01:27:34.193 --> 01:27:35.490
and, of course, many people opt

01:27:35.490 --> 01:27:37.760
to make their own hydration
electrolyte formulas.

01:27:37.760 --> 01:27:39.320
They'll put sea salt into some water,

01:27:39.320 --> 01:27:41.430
maybe even ingest a potassium tablet.

01:27:41.430 --> 01:27:43.020
It all depends on the context,

01:27:43.020 --> 01:27:46.210
and an important contextual
element is your diet,

01:27:46.210 --> 01:27:49.330
so, for instance, carbohydrates
hold water in the body,

01:27:49.330 --> 01:27:50.640
so regardless of how much salt

01:27:50.640 --> 01:27:52.120
and how much fluid you're ingesting,

01:27:52.120 --> 01:27:53.450
if you're ingesting carbohydrate

01:27:53.450 --> 01:27:56.780
and you drink fluids, water,

01:27:56.780 --> 01:27:59.100
some of that fluid is going
to be retained in the body.

01:27:59.100 --> 01:28:02.290
Now, for people that are
following low-carbohydrate diets,

01:28:02.290 --> 01:28:05.560
one of the most immediate effects
of a low-carbohydrate diet

01:28:05.560 --> 01:28:07.430
is that you're going
to excrete more water,

01:28:07.430 --> 01:28:09.130
and so, under those conditions,

01:28:09.130 --> 01:28:11.290
you're also going to lose not just water,

01:28:11.290 --> 01:28:13.800
but you'll probably also
lose sodium and potassium,

01:28:13.800 --> 01:28:16.890
and so some people, many people, in fact,

01:28:16.890 --> 01:28:20.320
find that when they are on a
lower or low-carbohydrate diet,

01:28:20.320 --> 01:28:21.360
then they need to make sure

01:28:21.360 --> 01:28:24.340
that they're getting enough
sodium and enough potassium,

01:28:24.340 --> 01:28:25.210
and some people do that

01:28:25.210 --> 01:28:27.450
by taking 99-milligram potassium tablets

01:28:27.450 --> 01:28:28.450
every time they eat.

01:28:28.450 --> 01:28:29.283
Some people do that

01:28:29.283 --> 01:28:33.820
by ingesting more foods
that contain potassium,

01:28:33.820 --> 01:28:34.653
and, of course,

01:28:34.653 --> 01:28:36.630
some people who are on
low-carbohydrate diets

01:28:36.630 --> 01:28:38.880
do ingest vegetables

01:28:38.880 --> 01:28:42.930
or other forms of food that
carry along with them potassium,

01:28:42.930 --> 01:28:47.260
so it's quite variable
from person to person.

01:28:47.260 --> 01:28:50.300
I mean, you can imagine if
carbohydrate holds water,

01:28:50.300 --> 01:28:52.690
water and salt balance and potassium

01:28:52.690 --> 01:28:54.650
go hand in hand, and hand,

01:28:54.650 --> 01:28:56.530
that if you're on a low-carbohydrate diet,

01:28:56.530 --> 01:28:57.460
that you might need to adjust

01:28:57.460 --> 01:28:59.030
your salt intake and potassium,

01:28:59.030 --> 01:29:02.140
and conversely, that if you're
on a carbohydrate-rich diet

01:29:02.140 --> 01:29:03.420
or a moderate carbohydrate diet,

01:29:03.420 --> 01:29:06.380
then you may need to ingest
less sodium and less potassium,

01:29:06.380 --> 01:29:08.150
and, in fact, a certain amount of water

01:29:08.150 --> 01:29:10.720
is probably coming in through
the foods you eat as well,

01:29:10.720 --> 01:29:12.690
so I don't say all this to confuse you.

01:29:12.690 --> 01:29:17.600
Again, I say this because it
all depends on the context.

01:29:17.600 --> 01:29:18.940
I'll give yet another context

01:29:18.940 --> 01:29:21.450
that I think is fairly common nowadays,

01:29:21.450 --> 01:29:25.030
which is many people are
following a pattern of eating

01:29:25.030 --> 01:29:27.980
that more or less resembles
intermittent fasting

01:29:27.980 --> 01:29:29.460
or at least time-restricted feeding,

01:29:29.460 --> 01:29:32.450
so they're eating between
particular feeding windows,

01:29:32.450 --> 01:29:35.320
and then, in the certain
parts of the 24-hour cycle,

01:29:35.320 --> 01:29:36.340
not just sleep,

01:29:36.340 --> 01:29:38.100
but during certain parts
of their waking cycle,

01:29:38.100 --> 01:29:40.490
they're also actively avoiding food,

01:29:40.490 --> 01:29:44.090
banking on, I think, either the possible,

01:29:44.090 --> 01:29:47.280
I want to say possible,
longevity-promoting effects

01:29:47.280 --> 01:29:48.530
of intermittent fasting,

01:29:48.530 --> 01:29:51.000
or, and/or, I should say,

01:29:51.000 --> 01:29:53.660
they are banking on the
fact that for many people,

01:29:53.660 --> 01:29:56.140
not eating is easier than portion control

01:29:56.140 --> 01:29:56.973
for certain parts of the day,

01:29:56.973 --> 01:30:01.160
and so they find it beneficial
to limit calories overall

01:30:01.160 --> 01:30:03.920
to a given amount, depending
on what their goals are,

01:30:03.920 --> 01:30:07.880
by not consuming food for
certain periods of the day,

01:30:07.880 --> 01:30:09.370
but usually, during
those periods of the day,

01:30:09.370 --> 01:30:10.640
they're consuming fluids,

01:30:10.640 --> 01:30:12.780
and oftentimes, those fluids
include not just water,

01:30:12.780 --> 01:30:15.400
but caffeine, and caffeine is a diuretic.

01:30:15.400 --> 01:30:18.630
It actually causes the excretion
of fluids from the body

01:30:18.630 --> 01:30:22.083
in part, because it causes
the excretion of sodium.

01:30:23.400 --> 01:30:24.360
All of that to say

01:30:24.360 --> 01:30:26.430
that if you're somebody who, for instance,

01:30:26.430 --> 01:30:29.060
eats your first meal around
noon or 1:00 or 2:00 p.m.,

01:30:29.060 --> 01:30:31.010
and you're fasting for
the early part of the day,

01:30:31.010 --> 01:30:32.630
and you're drinking coffee or tea

01:30:32.630 --> 01:30:35.430
or ingesting a lot of water,

01:30:35.430 --> 01:30:39.100
you are going to be excreting
sodium along with that water,

01:30:39.100 --> 01:30:42.470
and so many people, including
myself, find that it's useful,

01:30:42.470 --> 01:30:45.140
especially when I'm drinking caffeine

01:30:45.140 --> 01:30:46.940
during that so-called fasting

01:30:46.940 --> 01:30:51.290
or nonfood intake part of
time-restricted feeding,

01:30:51.290 --> 01:30:53.460
that I'm making sure to get enough salt,

01:30:53.460 --> 01:30:56.240
either in the form of something like LMNT,

01:30:56.240 --> 01:30:57.210
an electrolyte drink,

01:30:57.210 --> 01:30:59.390
or putting some sea salt into some water,

01:30:59.390 --> 01:31:03.710
or certainly, anytime one
is ingesting caffeine,

01:31:03.710 --> 01:31:05.630
replacing some of the lost water

01:31:05.630 --> 01:31:07.650
by increasing one's water intake.

01:31:07.650 --> 01:31:10.560
There are some simple
rules of thumb around this

01:31:10.560 --> 01:31:13.340
that I think can get
most people into a place

01:31:13.340 --> 01:31:15.950
where they're more comfortable
and functioning better,

01:31:15.950 --> 01:31:20.320
which is for every ounce of
coffee or tea that you drink,

01:31:20.320 --> 01:31:22.760
I should say caffeinated
coffee or tea that you drink,

01:31:22.760 --> 01:31:26.300
that you consume 1 1/2
times as much water,

01:31:26.300 --> 01:31:29.010
so let's say you have an 8-ounce coffee,

01:31:29.010 --> 01:31:30.820
try and drink about, you
don't have to be exact,

01:31:30.820 --> 01:31:33.610
but try and drink about a
12-ounce glass of water,

01:31:33.610 --> 01:31:35.810
and you might want to put a
tiny bit of sodium into that.

01:31:35.810 --> 01:31:38.000
By tiny bit, I just mean
a tiny pinch of sodium

01:31:38.000 --> 01:31:40.170
because remember, even if we're talking

01:31:40.170 --> 01:31:43.760
about increasing the amount
of sodium intake overall,

01:31:43.760 --> 01:31:47.030
the total amount of
sodium contained in salt

01:31:47.030 --> 01:31:50.250
is sufficiently high that
even just 1/4 teaspoon

01:31:50.250 --> 01:31:51.770
is going to really start
to move that number

01:31:51.770 --> 01:31:54.670
up towards that range that's
still within the safe range,

01:31:54.670 --> 01:31:57.140
but you're going to, if you
keep doing that all day long,

01:31:57.140 --> 01:31:57.973
you're very quickly

01:31:57.973 --> 01:31:59.870
going to get into that
excessive salt intake range

01:31:59.870 --> 01:32:01.910
that is deleterious for health,

01:32:01.910 --> 01:32:03.550
so again, if you're
consuming more caffeine,

01:32:03.550 --> 01:32:06.310
you're going to be excreting
water and salt and potassium,

01:32:06.310 --> 01:32:07.620
and so you're going to have to find ways

01:32:07.620 --> 01:32:09.640
to bring water, salt,
and potassium back in.

01:32:09.640 --> 01:32:11.550
Again, this has to be evaluated

01:32:11.550 --> 01:32:14.140
for each of your own
individual situations.

01:32:14.140 --> 01:32:17.110
If you're exercising fasted,

01:32:17.110 --> 01:32:19.740
and you're doing that
after drinking caffeine,

01:32:19.740 --> 01:32:23.520
then before, during, and
certainly after exercise,

01:32:23.520 --> 01:32:24.810
you're going to want to replenish

01:32:24.810 --> 01:32:28.350
the fluids and electrolytes
that you lost, including sodium,

01:32:28.350 --> 01:32:29.730
so you can imagine

01:32:29.730 --> 01:32:31.920
how this all starts to
become pretty dizzying,

01:32:31.920 --> 01:32:33.470
and yet it doesn't have to be dizzying.

01:32:33.470 --> 01:32:35.120
We can provide some useful ranges

01:32:35.120 --> 01:32:37.290
that, for most people, will work,

01:32:37.290 --> 01:32:39.050
and so let's talk about
what those ranges are,

01:32:39.050 --> 01:32:40.700
and I'm going to point you to a resource

01:32:40.700 --> 01:32:42.450
that explores what those ranges are

01:32:42.450 --> 01:32:45.850
in these various contexts of
nutrition, exercise, and so on.

01:32:45.850 --> 01:32:47.610
The resource is a book

01:32:47.610 --> 01:32:51.670
that was authored by Dr.
James DiNicolantonio.

01:32:51.670 --> 01:32:53.110
He's not a medical doctor.

01:32:53.110 --> 01:32:54.910
He's a scientist,

01:32:54.910 --> 01:32:58.390
so he's cardiovascular physiology

01:32:58.390 --> 01:33:00.720
as well, I believe, as
a doctor of pharmacy,

01:33:00.720 --> 01:33:02.677
and the title of the
book is "The Salt Fix."

01:33:02.677 --> 01:33:04.260
"The Salt Fix" is an interesting read

01:33:04.260 --> 01:33:06.390
because it points to, first of all,

01:33:06.390 --> 01:33:09.350
the history of salt in society

01:33:09.350 --> 01:33:11.780
and as it relates to health.

01:33:11.780 --> 01:33:14.790
It actually emphasizes
some of the major missteps,

01:33:14.790 --> 01:33:16.990
maybe even pretty drastic errors,

01:33:16.990 --> 01:33:20.370
that have been made in terms
of trying to interpret the role

01:33:20.370 --> 01:33:24.300
that salt has in various diseases

01:33:24.300 --> 01:33:28.470
and emphasizes some of the
ways in which, perhaps,

01:33:28.470 --> 01:33:31.730
increasing salt can actually
improve health outcomes,

01:33:31.730 --> 01:33:33.870
and I think it strikes
a pretty nice balance

01:33:33.870 --> 01:33:36.880
between what's commonly known about salt

01:33:36.880 --> 01:33:40.370
and what I believe ought
to be known about salt,

01:33:40.370 --> 01:33:42.253
or at least taken into consideration.

01:33:43.360 --> 01:33:46.570
The book does provide
certain recommendations,

01:33:46.570 --> 01:33:48.420
and I actually reached out to the author.

01:33:48.420 --> 01:33:51.350
I've never met him in person
or talked to him directly,

01:33:51.350 --> 01:33:54.050
and I asked him outright.

01:33:54.050 --> 01:33:56.207
I said, "How much salt

01:33:56.207 --> 01:33:59.290
"do you recommend people take on average?"

01:33:59.290 --> 01:34:03.030
And he gave, of course,
the appropriate caveats

01:34:03.030 --> 01:34:05.480
about prehypertension,
hypertension, et cetera,

01:34:05.480 --> 01:34:09.990
but made a recommendation
which I'll just share with you,

01:34:09.990 --> 01:34:11.350
and if you want to learn more

01:34:11.350 --> 01:34:13.610
about the support for this recommendation,

01:34:13.610 --> 01:34:15.550
you can check out his book.

01:34:15.550 --> 01:34:16.790
The recommendation he made was

01:34:16.790 --> 01:34:19.560
anywhere from 8 to 12 grams of salt a day,

01:34:19.560 --> 01:34:21.370
which corresponds to

01:34:21.370 --> 01:34:24.950
3.2 to 4.8 grams of sodium,

01:34:24.950 --> 01:34:28.990
so going back to the
current recommendations

01:34:28.990 --> 01:34:32.270
that we talked about before,
2.3 grams of sodium per day,

01:34:32.270 --> 01:34:34.110
this is about 1 1/2 times

01:34:34.110 --> 01:34:37.960
to double the amount of sodium

01:34:37.960 --> 01:34:40.940
that's currently
recommended in most circles,

01:34:40.940 --> 01:34:42.330
and then, what this corresponds to

01:34:42.330 --> 01:34:46.300
is about 1 1/2 to 2
teaspoons of salt per day

01:34:46.300 --> 01:34:49.120
to arrive at that 3.2
to 4.8 grams of sodium.

01:34:49.120 --> 01:34:51.270
Again, this is the recommendation

01:34:51.270 --> 01:34:54.990
that was passed along for
most people, most conditions,

01:34:54.990 --> 01:34:58.800
barring specific health issues.

01:34:58.800 --> 01:34:59.930
Now, what was also interesting is

01:34:59.930 --> 01:35:03.350
he pointed to a sodium-to-potassium ratio,

01:35:03.350 --> 01:35:06.410
which is 4 grams of potassium,

01:35:06.410 --> 01:35:08.940
and he also mentioned 400
milligrams of magnesium,

01:35:08.940 --> 01:35:11.170
and pointed out, and I
generally agree here,

01:35:11.170 --> 01:35:14.090
that many people are
deficient in magnesium,

01:35:14.090 --> 01:35:18.920
so again, that was a 3.2
to 4.8 grams of sodium,

01:35:18.920 --> 01:35:21.060
4 grams of potassium.

01:35:21.060 --> 01:35:21.927
You might think, "Well, gosh,

01:35:21.927 --> 01:35:24.880
"that's 1 1/2 to 2 times
the current recommendation,"

01:35:24.880 --> 01:35:27.670
but we can go back to that study

01:35:27.670 --> 01:35:29.430
that was mentioned earlier in the episode,

01:35:29.430 --> 01:35:30.740
that 2011 study,

01:35:30.740 --> 01:35:34.930
where I describe this
sort of J-shaped curve

01:35:34.930 --> 01:35:37.720
in which, when you look at the occurrence

01:35:37.720 --> 01:35:39.370
of these negative health events,

01:35:39.370 --> 01:35:42.490
they were fairly low at low sodium intake,

01:35:42.490 --> 01:35:45.540
lower still at slightly
higher sodium intake,

01:35:45.540 --> 01:35:48.550
much in line with the
recommendations that are made

01:35:48.550 --> 01:35:53.050
or that Dr. DiNicolantonio
passed along to me,

01:35:53.050 --> 01:35:54.700
and then, they increased quite,

01:35:54.700 --> 01:35:57.520
those health risks increased
quite substantially

01:35:57.520 --> 01:36:00.910
as one moves out past 6 grams of sodium,

01:36:00.910 --> 01:36:02.130
7 grams of sodium per day.

01:36:02.130 --> 01:36:04.793
That's when things really
do seem to get hazardous,

01:36:05.920 --> 01:36:08.620
and really, it makes sense, I think,

01:36:08.620 --> 01:36:10.240
given the consensus around this,

01:36:10.240 --> 01:36:12.323
to really avoid very high salt intake,

01:36:13.240 --> 01:36:17.220
so "The Salt Fix" describes the rationale

01:36:17.220 --> 01:36:19.117
behind those recommendations.

01:36:19.117 --> 01:36:23.430
"The Salt Fix" also describes,
in quite beautiful detail,

01:36:23.430 --> 01:36:27.470
the relationship between salt
intake, potassium intake,

01:36:27.470 --> 01:36:31.090
and the relationship to the
sugar consumption system.

01:36:31.090 --> 01:36:32.500
I'd like to pick up on this idea

01:36:32.500 --> 01:36:35.980
of the relationship between salt and sugar

01:36:35.980 --> 01:36:38.650
because I think that one key aspect

01:36:38.650 --> 01:36:40.510
of the way that salt can work

01:36:40.510 --> 01:36:43.810
and can benefit us or can
harm us has to do with the way

01:36:43.810 --> 01:36:47.240
that sodium and sugar are regulated

01:36:47.240 --> 01:36:49.150
and actually perceived by the brain

01:36:49.150 --> 01:36:49.983
and how,

01:36:49.983 --> 01:36:52.630
under conditions of certain
levels of sodium intake,

01:36:52.630 --> 01:36:54.570
we might be inspired to seek more sugar

01:36:54.570 --> 01:36:56.860
or to crave sweets more or less,

01:36:56.860 --> 01:36:57.693
so up until now,

01:36:57.693 --> 01:36:59.780
we've been talking about
salt as a substance

01:36:59.780 --> 01:37:01.940
and a way to regulate fluid balance

01:37:01.940 --> 01:37:04.240
and blood volume and so on.

01:37:04.240 --> 01:37:06.350
We haven't talked a lot
about salt as a taste

01:37:06.350 --> 01:37:08.960
or the taste of things that are salty,

01:37:08.960 --> 01:37:12.320
and yet we know that
we have salt receptors,

01:37:12.320 --> 01:37:13.990
meaning neurons,

01:37:13.990 --> 01:37:16.350
that fire action potentials

01:37:16.350 --> 01:37:18.820
when salty substances are detected,

01:37:18.820 --> 01:37:20.950
much in the same way that
we have sweet detectors

01:37:20.950 --> 01:37:22.410
and bitter detectors,

01:37:22.410 --> 01:37:26.110
and we have detectors of
umami, the savory flavor,

01:37:26.110 --> 01:37:27.160
on our tongue,

01:37:27.160 --> 01:37:29.180
and earlier, at the
beginning of the episode,

01:37:29.180 --> 01:37:33.070
I talked about the fact that
we have sweet receptors,

01:37:33.070 --> 01:37:35.490
neurons that respond to
the presence of sugar

01:37:35.490 --> 01:37:38.850
or even noncaloric
sweet things in the gut,

01:37:38.850 --> 01:37:42.150
and that signals up to the
brain through the vagus nerve,

01:37:42.150 --> 01:37:44.060
and those signals converge on pathways

01:37:44.060 --> 01:37:45.960
that relate to dopamine and so on.

01:37:45.960 --> 01:37:47.920
Well, we also have salt sensors

01:37:47.920 --> 01:37:51.060
at various locations
throughout our digestive tract,

01:37:51.060 --> 01:37:53.630
although the sensation
and the taste of salt

01:37:53.630 --> 01:37:56.690
actually exerts a very robust effect

01:37:56.690 --> 01:37:59.040
on certain areas of the brain

01:37:59.040 --> 01:38:01.720
that can either make us crave more

01:38:01.720 --> 01:38:06.050
or sate, meaning fulfill,
our desire for salt,

01:38:06.050 --> 01:38:07.940
and you can imagine why
this would be important.

01:38:07.940 --> 01:38:09.270
Your brain actually has to register

01:38:09.270 --> 01:38:11.720
whether or not you're bringing in salt

01:38:11.720 --> 01:38:13.410
in order to know whether or not

01:38:13.410 --> 01:38:16.560
you are going to crave salt more or not,

01:38:16.560 --> 01:38:19.600
and beautiful work that's
been done by the Zuker Lab,

01:38:19.600 --> 01:38:22.440
Z-U-K-E-R, Zuker Lab
at Columbia University,

01:38:22.440 --> 01:38:23.920
as well as many other labs

01:38:23.920 --> 01:38:25.440
have used imaging techniques

01:38:25.440 --> 01:38:27.440
and other techniques
such as molecular biology

01:38:27.440 --> 01:38:29.430
to define these so-called
parallel pathways,

01:38:29.430 --> 01:38:32.970
parallel meaning pathways
that represent sweet

01:38:32.970 --> 01:38:36.000
or the presence of sweet
taste in the mouth and gut,

01:38:36.000 --> 01:38:37.830
parallel pathways, meaning neural circuits

01:38:37.830 --> 01:38:40.030
that represent the
presence of salty tastes

01:38:40.030 --> 01:38:41.910
in the mouth and gut and so on,

01:38:41.910 --> 01:38:44.470
and that those go into the brain,

01:38:44.470 --> 01:38:46.710
move up through brain stem centers

01:38:46.710 --> 01:38:48.230
and up to the neocortex

01:38:48.230 --> 01:38:50.640
indeed where our seed of
our conscious perception is

01:38:50.640 --> 01:38:53.440
to give us a sense and a perception

01:38:53.440 --> 01:38:56.303
of the components of the foods

01:38:56.303 --> 01:38:57.960
that we happen to be ingesting

01:38:57.960 --> 01:39:00.770
and a sense and a perception of the fluids

01:39:00.770 --> 01:39:02.100
and the components of those fluids

01:39:02.100 --> 01:39:04.070
that we happen to be ingesting.

01:39:04.070 --> 01:39:06.950
Now, parallel pathways,
as I'm describing them,

01:39:06.950 --> 01:39:10.730
are a fundamental feature
of every sensory system,

01:39:10.730 --> 01:39:13.400
not just the taste system,
but also the visual system.

01:39:13.400 --> 01:39:14.420
We have parallel pathways

01:39:14.420 --> 01:39:16.810
for perceiving dark objects
versus light objects,

01:39:16.810 --> 01:39:19.860
for perceiving red
versus green, et cetera.

01:39:19.860 --> 01:39:22.620
This is a fundamental
feature of how we are built

01:39:22.620 --> 01:39:24.300
and how our nervous system works,

01:39:24.300 --> 01:39:28.720
and in the taste system, much
like in these other systems,

01:39:28.720 --> 01:39:31.350
these pathways are indeed parallel,

01:39:31.350 --> 01:39:34.530
but they converge, and they
can influence one another,

01:39:34.530 --> 01:39:36.570
and I think the simplest way to put this

01:39:36.570 --> 01:39:38.560
is in the context, first,
of the visual system,

01:39:38.560 --> 01:39:42.340
whereby your ability
to detect the color red

01:39:42.340 --> 01:39:43.880
has everything to do with the fact

01:39:43.880 --> 01:39:45.810
that you have neurons in your eye

01:39:45.810 --> 01:39:49.400
that absorb long wavelengths
of light that we call reds,

01:39:49.400 --> 01:39:50.590
red wavelengths of light,

01:39:50.590 --> 01:39:53.930
which are longer wavelengths
than, say, blue light,

01:39:53.930 --> 01:39:55.240
which are shorter wavelength,

01:39:55.240 --> 01:39:58.210
but it is really the comparison
of the electrical activity

01:39:58.210 --> 01:40:00.360
of the neurons that absorb red light

01:40:00.360 --> 01:40:03.520
with the activity of the
neurons that absorb green light

01:40:03.520 --> 01:40:05.750
which actually gives you
the perception of red,

01:40:05.750 --> 01:40:07.980
so that might seem a
little counterintuitive,

01:40:07.980 --> 01:40:09.300
but indeed, it's not.

01:40:09.300 --> 01:40:12.740
It's actually because something is red

01:40:12.740 --> 01:40:14.890
and has less greenness

01:40:14.890 --> 01:40:18.370
that we perceive it as
more red than the green,

01:40:18.370 --> 01:40:19.630
and this is actually the way

01:40:19.630 --> 01:40:21.670
that your entire nervous system works

01:40:21.670 --> 01:40:23.300
is that we aren't really good

01:40:23.300 --> 01:40:25.860
at evaluating absolute levels of anything

01:40:25.860 --> 01:40:27.080
in the context or perception.

01:40:27.080 --> 01:40:29.470
It's only by comparison,

01:40:29.470 --> 01:40:31.530
and actually, there's a fun
experiment that you can do,

01:40:31.530 --> 01:40:33.387
I think you could probably
find it easily online,

01:40:33.387 --> 01:40:35.120
but you could also do
this experiment at home.

01:40:35.120 --> 01:40:37.143
You can stare at something that's red,

01:40:38.060 --> 01:40:40.170
or green, for that matter, for a while,

01:40:40.170 --> 01:40:44.270
so you make an active
decision to not blink

01:40:44.270 --> 01:40:46.020
and to stare at something that's red,

01:40:46.020 --> 01:40:47.930
and then you look away from that thing,

01:40:47.930 --> 01:40:50.450
and you'll actually see a green afterimage

01:40:50.450 --> 01:40:51.840
of that red object.

01:40:51.840 --> 01:40:53.000
Conversely, if you look at something

01:40:53.000 --> 01:40:54.270
that's green for a while,

01:40:54.270 --> 01:40:55.710
and you stare at it, and you look away,

01:40:55.710 --> 01:40:58.740
you will see the red
afterimage of that thing.

01:40:58.740 --> 01:41:00.160
Now, the taste system doesn't have

01:41:00.160 --> 01:41:04.420
quite the same aftertaste type effect,

01:41:04.420 --> 01:41:07.160
but nonetheless, the pathways,

01:41:07.160 --> 01:41:08.760
the parallel pathways for salty

01:41:08.760 --> 01:41:12.280
and the parallel pathways for
sweet and bitter and so on

01:41:12.280 --> 01:41:13.377
can actually interact,

01:41:13.377 --> 01:41:14.890
and this has important relevance

01:41:14.890 --> 01:41:18.550
in the context of food
choices and sugar craving.

01:41:18.550 --> 01:41:21.730
One of the things that's
commonplace nowadays is

01:41:21.730 --> 01:41:25.410
in many processed foods there
is a business, literally,

01:41:25.410 --> 01:41:28.290
a business of putting
so-called hidden sugars,

01:41:28.290 --> 01:41:29.160
and these hidden sugars

01:41:29.160 --> 01:41:31.150
are not always in the
form of caloric sugars.

01:41:31.150 --> 01:41:33.710
They're sometimes in the
form of artificial sweeteners

01:41:33.710 --> 01:41:35.270
into various foods,

01:41:35.270 --> 01:41:36.727
and you might say, "Well,

01:41:36.727 --> 01:41:38.937
"why would they put more sugar into a food

01:41:38.937 --> 01:41:41.117
"and then disguise the sugary taste

01:41:41.117 --> 01:41:43.387
"given that sweet tastes
often compel people

01:41:43.387 --> 01:41:44.980
"to eat more of these things?"

01:41:44.980 --> 01:41:46.730
Well, it's a way, actually,

01:41:46.730 --> 01:41:51.010
of bypassing some of the
homeostatic mechanisms for sweet,

01:41:51.010 --> 01:41:53.370
even though we might think that
the more sweet stuff we eat,

01:41:53.370 --> 01:41:55.160
the more sweet stuff we crave,

01:41:55.160 --> 01:41:56.740
in general, people have a threshold

01:41:56.740 --> 01:41:59.970
whereby they say, "Okay, I've
had enough sugary stuff."

01:41:59.970 --> 01:42:01.650
You can actually experience this.

01:42:01.650 --> 01:42:04.370
If you ever feel like something
is really, really sweet,

01:42:04.370 --> 01:42:06.450
take a little sip of water

01:42:06.450 --> 01:42:08.210
with a little bit of lemon
juice in it or vinegar,

01:42:08.210 --> 01:42:09.840
and it will quickly quench

01:42:09.840 --> 01:42:12.820
that overly sweet sensation or perception.

01:42:12.820 --> 01:42:14.760
It will disappear almost immediately.

01:42:14.760 --> 01:42:17.270
There's actually a practice in fancy meals

01:42:17.270 --> 01:42:19.200
of cleansing the palate

01:42:19.200 --> 01:42:21.000
through the ingestion of different foods,

01:42:21.000 --> 01:42:22.680
and that's the same idea that
you're cleansing the palate.

01:42:22.680 --> 01:42:25.000
You're actually neutralizing
the previous taste

01:42:25.000 --> 01:42:26.980
so then they can bring yet another dish

01:42:26.980 --> 01:42:30.190
to overindulge you in
decadence and so forth,

01:42:30.190 --> 01:42:32.713
so these sensory systems
interact in this way.

01:42:34.320 --> 01:42:36.990
By putting sugars into foods

01:42:36.990 --> 01:42:40.150
and hiding the sugary
taste of those foods,

01:42:40.150 --> 01:42:43.660
those foods, even if they
contain artificial sweeteners,

01:42:43.660 --> 01:42:45.550
can activate the sorts of neurons

01:42:45.550 --> 01:42:47.180
that we talked about at the
beginning of the episode,

01:42:47.180 --> 01:42:48.800
like the neuropod cells

01:42:48.800 --> 01:42:51.400
that will then signal to the
brain to release more dopamine

01:42:51.400 --> 01:42:52.990
and make you crave more of that food,

01:42:52.990 --> 01:42:54.360
whereas had you been able

01:42:54.360 --> 01:42:57.770
to perceive the true
sweetness of that food,

01:42:57.770 --> 01:42:58.907
you might have consumed less,

01:42:58.907 --> 01:43:00.440
and indeed, that's what happens,

01:43:00.440 --> 01:43:03.340
so these hidden sugars
are kind of diabolical.

01:43:03.340 --> 01:43:05.080
Why am I talking about all of this

01:43:05.080 --> 01:43:07.850
in the context of an episode on salt?

01:43:07.850 --> 01:43:10.540
Well, as many of you
have probably noticed,

01:43:10.540 --> 01:43:12.310
a lot of foods out there

01:43:12.310 --> 01:43:15.440
contain a salty-sweet combination,

01:43:15.440 --> 01:43:18.330
and it's that combination
of salty and sweet

01:43:18.330 --> 01:43:19.870
which can actually lead you to consume

01:43:19.870 --> 01:43:24.810
more of the salty-sweet
food than you would have

01:43:24.810 --> 01:43:28.300
if it had just been sweet
or it had just been salty,

01:43:28.300 --> 01:43:32.590
and that's because both
sweet taste and salty taste

01:43:32.590 --> 01:43:34.440
have a homeostatic balance,

01:43:34.440 --> 01:43:36.550
so if you ingest something
that's very, very salty,

01:43:36.550 --> 01:43:39.400
pretty soon your appetite for
salty foods will be reduced,

01:43:39.400 --> 01:43:41.830
but if you mask some of that with sweet,

01:43:41.830 --> 01:43:44.520
well, because of the interactions

01:43:44.520 --> 01:43:45.960
of these parallel pathways,

01:43:45.960 --> 01:43:48.290
you somewhat shut down your perception

01:43:48.290 --> 01:43:50.680
of how much salt you're ingesting,

01:43:50.680 --> 01:43:53.930
or conversely, by ingesting
some salt with sweet foods,

01:43:53.930 --> 01:43:55.790
you mask some of the sweetness

01:43:55.790 --> 01:43:57.110
of the sweet foods that you're tasting,

01:43:57.110 --> 01:43:58.990
and you will continue to
indulge in those foods,

01:43:58.990 --> 01:44:03.130
so salty-sweet interactions
can be very diabolical.

01:44:03.130 --> 01:44:04.470
They can also be very tasty,

01:44:04.470 --> 01:44:05.840
but they can be very diabolical

01:44:05.840 --> 01:44:08.910
in terms of inspiring you to
eat more of a particular food

01:44:08.910 --> 01:44:10.230
than you would otherwise

01:44:10.230 --> 01:44:13.250
if you were just following
your homeostatic salt

01:44:13.250 --> 01:44:16.700
or your homeostatic sugar balance systems,

01:44:16.700 --> 01:44:18.830
and the beautiful imaging
work that's been done

01:44:18.830 --> 01:44:20.480
by the Zuker Lab and other labs

01:44:20.480 --> 01:44:23.530
has actually been able to reveal
how some of this might work

01:44:23.530 --> 01:44:26.760
by showing, for instance,
that a certain ensemble,

01:44:26.760 --> 01:44:28.340
meaning a certain group of neurons,

01:44:28.340 --> 01:44:30.650
is activated by a sweet taste

01:44:30.650 --> 01:44:34.280
and a nonoverlapping distinct
set of neurons just nearby

01:44:34.280 --> 01:44:37.130
sitting cheek-to-jowl
with those other neurons

01:44:37.130 --> 01:44:39.330
would be activated by salty tastes

01:44:39.330 --> 01:44:41.220
and yet others by bitter taste et cetera,

01:44:41.220 --> 01:44:42.870
so there's a separate map

01:44:42.870 --> 01:44:44.590
of these different parallel pathways,

01:44:44.590 --> 01:44:47.810
but that when foods or fluids are ingested

01:44:47.810 --> 01:44:50.050
that are both salty and sweet,

01:44:50.050 --> 01:44:53.350
you get a yet entirely different ensemble

01:44:53.350 --> 01:44:54.780
of neurons activated,

01:44:54.780 --> 01:44:58.590
so your brain, whether or not
it's for your visual system

01:44:58.590 --> 01:45:00.610
or your auditory system
or your taste system,

01:45:00.610 --> 01:45:03.460
has a way of representing
the pure form of taste,

01:45:03.460 --> 01:45:05.080
salty, sweet, bitter, et cetera,

01:45:05.080 --> 01:45:07.820
and has a way of representing
their combinations,

01:45:07.820 --> 01:45:12.100
and food manufacturers have
exploited this to large degree.

01:45:12.100 --> 01:45:13.190
I mention all of this

01:45:13.190 --> 01:45:16.740
because if you're somebody
who's looking to explore

01:45:16.740 --> 01:45:19.860
either increasing or
decreasing your sodium intake

01:45:19.860 --> 01:45:22.970
for health benefits, for
performance benefits,

01:45:22.970 --> 01:45:25.670
in many ways, it is useful to do that

01:45:25.670 --> 01:45:28.200
in the context of a fairly pure,

01:45:28.200 --> 01:45:31.000
meaning unprocessed
food intake background,

01:45:31.000 --> 01:45:33.963
whether or not that's key
to a carnivore, omnivore,

01:45:34.930 --> 01:45:36.450
intermittent fasting, or what have you.

01:45:36.450 --> 01:45:37.570
It doesn't really matter,

01:45:37.570 --> 01:45:42.060
but the closer that foods are
to their basic form and taste,

01:45:42.060 --> 01:45:44.500
meaning not large combinations

01:45:44.500 --> 01:45:46.330
of large amounts of ingredients

01:45:46.330 --> 01:45:49.140
and certainly avoiding
highly processed foods,

01:45:49.140 --> 01:45:51.750
the more quickly you're
going to be able to hone in

01:45:51.750 --> 01:45:54.910
on your specific salt
appetite and salt needs,

01:45:54.910 --> 01:45:56.960
which, as I've pointed out numerous times

01:45:56.960 --> 01:45:57.793
throughout this episode,

01:45:57.793 --> 01:45:59.400
are going to vary from person to person

01:45:59.400 --> 01:46:01.320
depending on nutrition,
depending on activity,

01:46:01.320 --> 01:46:02.950
depending on hormone status,

01:46:02.950 --> 01:46:06.060
or even portion of your
menstrual cycle, for that matter,

01:46:06.060 --> 01:46:07.940
so if you want to home in

01:46:07.940 --> 01:46:09.840
on the appropriate
amount of sodium for you,

01:46:09.840 --> 01:46:12.780
yes, blood pressure is going
to be an important metric

01:46:12.780 --> 01:46:14.360
to pay attention to as you go along,

01:46:14.360 --> 01:46:16.770
and the parameters for
healthy blood pressure ranges

01:46:16.770 --> 01:46:18.240
are readily available online,

01:46:18.240 --> 01:46:19.990
so I'll let you refer to those

01:46:19.990 --> 01:46:21.900
in order to determine those for yourself,

01:46:21.900 --> 01:46:23.650
but in determining whether or not

01:46:24.560 --> 01:46:27.680
increasing your salt
intake might be beneficial,

01:46:27.680 --> 01:46:29.530
for instance, for reducing anxiety a bit

01:46:29.530 --> 01:46:31.210
or for increasing blood pressure

01:46:31.210 --> 01:46:33.610
to offset some of these postural syndromes

01:46:33.610 --> 01:46:34.930
where you get dizzy, et cetera,

01:46:34.930 --> 01:46:37.830
for improving sports performance
or cognitive performance,

01:46:39.150 --> 01:46:41.840
I can only recommend that you do this

01:46:41.840 --> 01:46:43.620
in a fairly clean context

01:46:43.620 --> 01:46:44.730
where you're not trying to do this

01:46:44.730 --> 01:46:46.480
by ingesting a bunch of salty foods

01:46:46.480 --> 01:46:48.600
or salty-sweet foods, et cetera,

01:46:48.600 --> 01:46:50.090
and indeed, many people find,

01:46:50.090 --> 01:46:51.440
and it's reviewed a bit,

01:46:51.440 --> 01:46:53.040
and some of the data are reviewed

01:46:53.040 --> 01:46:54.890
in the book "The Salt Fix,"

01:46:54.890 --> 01:46:57.860
that when people increase
their salt intake

01:46:57.860 --> 01:47:01.620
in a backdrop of relatively
unprocessed foods,

01:47:01.620 --> 01:47:04.470
that sugar cravings can
indeed be vastly reduced,

01:47:04.470 --> 01:47:05.560
and that makes sense

01:47:05.560 --> 01:47:07.320
given the way that these neural pathways

01:47:07.320 --> 01:47:09.040
for salty and sweet interact.

01:47:09.040 --> 01:47:12.930
Now, thus far, I've already
covered quite a lot of material,

01:47:12.930 --> 01:47:15.440
but I would be completely remiss

01:47:15.440 --> 01:47:19.820
if I didn't emphasize the
crucial role that sodium plays

01:47:19.820 --> 01:47:21.700
in the way that neurons function.

01:47:21.700 --> 01:47:24.610
In fact, sodium is one of the key elements

01:47:24.610 --> 01:47:27.320
that allows neurons to function at all,

01:47:27.320 --> 01:47:28.670
and that's by way of engaging

01:47:28.670 --> 01:47:30.650
what we call the action potential.

01:47:30.650 --> 01:47:32.240
The action potential

01:47:32.240 --> 01:47:37.090
is the firing of electrical
activity by neurons.

01:47:37.090 --> 01:47:39.810
Now, neurons can engage
electrical activity

01:47:39.810 --> 01:47:40.920
in a number of different ways.

01:47:40.920 --> 01:47:44.220
They have graded potentials.
They have gap junctions.

01:47:44.220 --> 01:47:45.750
There's a whole landscape

01:47:45.750 --> 01:47:49.020
of different
electrophysiologies of neurons

01:47:49.020 --> 01:47:51.630
that I don't want to go into just yet,

01:47:51.630 --> 01:47:53.330
at least not in this episode,

01:47:53.330 --> 01:47:55.980
but the action potential
is the fundamental way

01:47:55.980 --> 01:47:58.660
in which neurons communicate
with one another.

01:47:58.660 --> 01:48:00.770
They're sometimes called spikes.

01:48:00.770 --> 01:48:04.290
It's just kind of nomenclature
that neuroscientists use.

01:48:04.290 --> 01:48:06.460
I'm just going to briefly
describe the action potential

01:48:06.460 --> 01:48:08.400
and the role that sodium plays,

01:48:08.400 --> 01:48:10.830
and this will involve a
little bit of chemistry,

01:48:10.830 --> 01:48:13.160
but I promise it will
be accessible to anyone,

01:48:13.160 --> 01:48:15.410
even if you don't have a
chemistry or a physics background

01:48:15.410 --> 01:48:17.010
or electrophysiology background.

01:48:18.140 --> 01:48:20.850
Neurons have an inside and an outside,

01:48:20.850 --> 01:48:25.680
and inside are things like
your genetic material.

01:48:25.680 --> 01:48:27.840
They have a bunch of things
floating around in there

01:48:27.840 --> 01:48:29.770
that allow those cells to function,

01:48:29.770 --> 01:48:32.810
and they tend to have this
wire extending out of them,

01:48:32.810 --> 01:48:34.740
sometimes a very long wire,
sometimes a short one,

01:48:34.740 --> 01:48:36.140
that we call the axon,

01:48:36.140 --> 01:48:39.240
and at the end of that wire, that axon,

01:48:39.240 --> 01:48:42.510
they release little packets of chemicals

01:48:42.510 --> 01:48:47.160
that either cause the next
neuron to fire action potentials

01:48:47.160 --> 01:48:49.970
or prevent the next neuron
from firing action potentials,

01:48:49.970 --> 01:48:53.900
so they kind of vomit out these
little packets of chemicals

01:48:53.900 --> 01:48:57.800
that either inspire or
suppress action potentials

01:48:57.800 --> 01:48:58.803
in other neurons.

01:49:00.240 --> 01:49:03.830
The way that that whole process occurs

01:49:03.830 --> 01:49:05.500
is that a given neuron

01:49:05.500 --> 01:49:08.580
needs to change its electrical activity,

01:49:08.580 --> 01:49:12.020
so normally, neurons are hanging out,

01:49:12.020 --> 01:49:13.870
and they have what we
call a negative charge,

01:49:13.870 --> 01:49:16.360
and the reason they have a negative charge

01:49:16.360 --> 01:49:18.260
is that the inside of the cell

01:49:18.260 --> 01:49:19.670
has things floating around in it

01:49:19.670 --> 01:49:22.460
like potassium, a little bit of sodium,

01:49:22.460 --> 01:49:23.940
and some stuff like chloride.

01:49:23.940 --> 01:49:25.120
These are literally just,

01:49:25.120 --> 01:49:28.500
just imagine these as
little balls of stuff,

01:49:28.500 --> 01:49:31.283
and if they have a
negative charge on them,

01:49:31.283 --> 01:49:32.650
then the inside of the cell

01:49:32.650 --> 01:49:34.080
is going to tend to be more negative,

01:49:34.080 --> 01:49:36.160
and outside of the cell, it turns out,

01:49:36.160 --> 01:49:38.650
you're going to have a bunch of stuff

01:49:38.650 --> 01:49:40.710
that's positively charged,

01:49:40.710 --> 01:49:42.730
and one of the main factors

01:49:42.730 --> 01:49:44.970
in creating that positive
charge is sodium.

01:49:44.970 --> 01:49:47.100
Sodium carries a positive charge,

01:49:47.100 --> 01:49:50.420
so you have neurons that
you can just imagine a,

01:49:50.420 --> 01:49:52.220
for sake of this discussion,

01:49:52.220 --> 01:49:53.670
you can just imagine as a sphere

01:49:53.670 --> 01:49:55.570
with a little wire sticking out of it.

01:49:56.700 --> 01:49:58.810
You can put a little minus
on the inside for negative.

01:49:58.810 --> 01:50:01.300
You can put a little plus
on the outside for positive,

01:50:01.300 --> 01:50:06.020
and when that neuron is
stimulated by another neuron,

01:50:06.020 --> 01:50:07.140
if the stimulation,

01:50:07.140 --> 01:50:09.923
the electrical stimulation
is sufficiently high,

01:50:11.240 --> 01:50:14.670
meaning enough little
packets of neurotransmitter

01:50:14.670 --> 01:50:17.250
have been vomited onto its surface

01:50:17.250 --> 01:50:19.270
at sufficient concentration,

01:50:19.270 --> 01:50:24.040
what happens is little
pores, little spaces,

01:50:24.040 --> 01:50:28.750
little gaps open up in
the membrane of that cell

01:50:28.750 --> 01:50:31.840
that separates the
inside from the outside,

01:50:31.840 --> 01:50:34.150
and because it's positive,

01:50:34.150 --> 01:50:35.950
there's a lot of positive charge outside,

01:50:35.950 --> 01:50:38.350
and there's a lot of negative inside,

01:50:38.350 --> 01:50:40.500
it's like a boulder running downhill.

01:50:40.500 --> 01:50:42.630
All the stuff tends to rush downhill.

01:50:42.630 --> 01:50:44.730
It tries to create even amounts of charge,

01:50:44.730 --> 01:50:47.230
so it's negative on the inside,
positive on the outside,

01:50:47.230 --> 01:50:51.450
and what happens is sodium
rushes into the cell

01:50:51.450 --> 01:50:53.580
carrying a lot of charge into the cell,

01:50:53.580 --> 01:50:55.080
and as a consequence,

01:50:55.080 --> 01:50:57.610
the charge of that cell
goes from negative,

01:50:57.610 --> 01:51:00.330
actually, very negative,
to quite positive,

01:51:00.330 --> 01:51:03.860
and if it hits a certain
threshold of positive charge

01:51:03.860 --> 01:51:07.430
because of all the sodium
ions going into the cell,

01:51:07.430 --> 01:51:10.350
then it fires what's
called an action potential,

01:51:10.350 --> 01:51:13.580
and it vomits out its own set of chemicals

01:51:13.580 --> 01:51:14.470
onto the next neuron,

01:51:14.470 --> 01:51:15.770
and so it sets off a chain

01:51:15.770 --> 01:51:18.340
of one neuron goes from
negative to positive,

01:51:18.340 --> 01:51:20.630
blech, vomits out chemicals
onto the next one.

01:51:20.630 --> 01:51:22.433
The next one, the next neuron,

01:51:23.270 --> 01:51:26.230
that binds to receptors
or enters the cell,

01:51:26.230 --> 01:51:29.631
and that cell goes from
negative to positive charge,

01:51:29.631 --> 01:51:32.180
[imitates vomiting] vomits its
contents onto the next cell,

01:51:32.180 --> 01:51:33.523
and so on and so forth.

01:51:35.510 --> 01:51:37.890
Sodium rushing into the cell, therefore,

01:51:37.890 --> 01:51:41.270
is the way that the action
potential is stimulated.

01:51:41.270 --> 01:51:44.330
In other words, sodium is the way

01:51:44.330 --> 01:51:46.770
that neurons communicate with one another.

01:51:46.770 --> 01:51:50.080
Now, the neurons don't
stay in a positive charge.

01:51:50.080 --> 01:51:52.413
Otherwise, they would just keep
vomiting out their contents,

01:51:52.413 --> 01:51:54.867
[imitates vomiting], but they
need to maintain some of that,

01:51:54.867 --> 01:51:55.810
and they need to go back

01:51:55.810 --> 01:51:58.040
to preparing to do it the
next time and the next time

01:51:58.040 --> 01:51:59.370
by resting a bit,

01:51:59.370 --> 01:52:02.060
and turns out that the way
they restore their charge

01:52:02.060 --> 01:52:06.230
is by pushing that sodium
back out of the cell.

01:52:06.230 --> 01:52:08.320
There are mechanisms in place to do that,

01:52:08.320 --> 01:52:11.260
things like the so-called
sodium-potassium pump.

01:52:11.260 --> 01:52:14.550
There's a change in the
levels of the potassium

01:52:14.550 --> 01:52:16.640
across the cell membrane
and so on and so forth.

01:52:16.640 --> 01:52:18.051
If you want to look at
a demonstration of this,

01:52:18.051 --> 01:52:18.884
you can just,

01:52:18.884 --> 01:52:22.890
you can put into a web
browser the action potential.

01:52:22.890 --> 01:52:24.650
You'll find some beautiful
descriptions there

01:52:24.650 --> 01:52:26.600
on YouTube and elsewhere.

01:52:26.600 --> 01:52:28.370
Maybe some time on Instagram,

01:52:28.370 --> 01:52:30.310
I'll do a description with a diagram

01:52:30.310 --> 01:52:31.240
'cause I realize

01:52:31.240 --> 01:52:33.040
number of people are
just listening to this.

01:52:33.040 --> 01:52:34.130
I can't do that here.

01:52:34.130 --> 01:52:35.520
I won't do that here

01:52:35.520 --> 01:52:36.353
'cause I want everyone

01:52:36.353 --> 01:52:37.880
to be able to get the
same amount of material

01:52:37.880 --> 01:52:39.220
regardless of whether
or not they're watching

01:52:39.220 --> 01:52:40.900
and/or listening to this,

01:52:40.900 --> 01:52:43.730
but the point I'd like to make,

01:52:43.730 --> 01:52:45.800
at least as it relates
to this episode on salt,

01:52:45.800 --> 01:52:49.890
is that having sufficient
levels of salt in your system

01:52:49.890 --> 01:52:51.310
allows your brain to function,

01:52:51.310 --> 01:52:53.390
allows your nervous
system to function at all.

01:52:53.390 --> 01:52:55.430
Again, this is the most basic aspect

01:52:55.430 --> 01:52:57.100
of nervous system function,

01:52:57.100 --> 01:52:58.550
and there are cases

01:52:58.550 --> 01:53:02.290
where this whole system gets disrupted,

01:53:02.290 --> 01:53:06.063
and that brings us to the topic
of sodium and water balance.

01:53:07.210 --> 01:53:08.480
As many of you have probably heard,

01:53:08.480 --> 01:53:10.760
but hopefully, if you haven't,

01:53:10.760 --> 01:53:13.100
you'll take this message seriously,

01:53:13.100 --> 01:53:15.820
if you drink too much water,

01:53:15.820 --> 01:53:17.780
especially in a short amount of time,

01:53:17.780 --> 01:53:20.400
you can actually kill yourself, right?

01:53:20.400 --> 01:53:22.730
And we certainly don't
want that to happen.

01:53:22.730 --> 01:53:27.370
If you ingest a lot of water
in a very short period of time,

01:53:27.370 --> 01:53:29.033
something called hypernatremia,

01:53:30.070 --> 01:53:32.780
you will excrete a lot
of sodium very quickly,

01:53:32.780 --> 01:53:35.080
and your ability to
regulate kidney function

01:53:35.080 --> 01:53:36.170
will be disrupted,

01:53:36.170 --> 01:53:37.660
but in addition to that,

01:53:37.660 --> 01:53:39.640
your brain can actually stop functioning,

01:53:39.640 --> 01:53:43.570
so people have actually
consumed water to excess,

01:53:43.570 --> 01:53:45.850
especially after sports
events and so forth,

01:53:45.850 --> 01:53:49.250
and if that water doesn't
contain sufficient electrolytes,

01:53:49.250 --> 01:53:51.420
you can actually shut
down neurons' ability

01:53:51.420 --> 01:53:52.450
to function at all

01:53:52.450 --> 01:53:56.510
by disrupting this balance
of sodium and potassium

01:53:56.510 --> 01:53:58.430
and the amount of extracellular sodium

01:53:58.430 --> 01:54:01.850
and neurons' ability to
signal to one another

01:54:01.850 --> 01:54:03.330
through action potentials,

01:54:03.330 --> 01:54:04.830
and I can't emphasize the importance

01:54:04.830 --> 01:54:06.110
of action potentials enough.

01:54:06.110 --> 01:54:08.230
They are the way that I
can lift my pen right now.

01:54:08.230 --> 01:54:09.220
They're the way that I can speak.

01:54:09.220 --> 01:54:10.780
They're the way that you breathe.

01:54:10.780 --> 01:54:12.660
They literally control all aspects

01:54:12.660 --> 01:54:14.420
of your nervous system function.

01:54:14.420 --> 01:54:16.140
Now, it takes quite a lot of water intake

01:54:16.140 --> 01:54:18.070
before you excrete enough sodium

01:54:18.070 --> 01:54:20.120
that your nervous system
is going to shut down,

01:54:20.120 --> 01:54:21.690
and I certainly don't want
to give the impression

01:54:21.690 --> 01:54:23.310
that simply by ingesting more sodium,

01:54:23.310 --> 01:54:24.940
your neurons will work better,

01:54:24.940 --> 01:54:26.350
but it absolutely is the case

01:54:26.350 --> 01:54:28.810
that if you don't ingest enough sodium,

01:54:28.810 --> 01:54:31.530
that your neurons won't
function as well as they could,

01:54:31.530 --> 01:54:34.480
and that if your sodium
levels are made too low

01:54:35.630 --> 01:54:36.970
by hemorrhage

01:54:36.970 --> 01:54:41.460
or by ingesting so much water, fluid,

01:54:41.460 --> 01:54:44.340
that you excrete excess amounts of sodium

01:54:44.340 --> 01:54:46.910
or through any other mechanism, that is,

01:54:46.910 --> 01:54:48.360
then indeed your neurons

01:54:48.360 --> 01:54:49.727
won't be able to fire action potentials

01:54:49.727 --> 01:54:52.310
and your brain and nervous
system simply won't work,

01:54:52.310 --> 01:54:54.880
and that's one of the primary
reasons why dehydration

01:54:54.880 --> 01:54:58.850
leads to confusion and dizziness
and lack of coordination,

01:54:58.850 --> 01:55:00.390
and I've talked about this a bit

01:55:00.390 --> 01:55:01.960
in the episode on endurance,

01:55:01.960 --> 01:55:03.690
but there are instances

01:55:03.690 --> 01:55:06.900
in which competitive athletes
have come into the stadium

01:55:06.900 --> 01:55:09.340
to finish a final lap
of a long endurance race

01:55:09.340 --> 01:55:10.890
and are completely disoriented

01:55:10.890 --> 01:55:12.910
and actually can't find
their way to the finish line.

01:55:12.910 --> 01:55:15.500
It might sound like kind
of a silly, crazy example,

01:55:15.500 --> 01:55:17.060
but there are examples

01:55:17.060 --> 01:55:20.470
of people having severe mental issues

01:55:20.470 --> 01:55:22.970
and physical issues post-exercise

01:55:22.970 --> 01:55:25.230
when that exercise
involved a ton of sweating

01:55:25.230 --> 01:55:26.100
or hot environments

01:55:26.100 --> 01:55:29.410
or insufficient ingestion
of fluids and electrolytes

01:55:29.410 --> 01:55:31.420
because included in the
electrolyte formula,

01:55:31.420 --> 01:55:32.810
of course, is sodium,

01:55:32.810 --> 01:55:33.750
and as you just learned,

01:55:33.750 --> 01:55:36.980
sodium is absolutely crucial
for neurons to function,

01:55:36.980 --> 01:55:40.770
so to briefly recap some of
what I've talked about today,

01:55:40.770 --> 01:55:43.830
we talked about how the brain
monitors the amount of salt

01:55:43.830 --> 01:55:45.200
in your brain and body

01:55:45.200 --> 01:55:47.220
and how that relates to thirst

01:55:47.220 --> 01:55:51.780
and the drive to consume more
fluid and/or salty fluids.

01:55:51.780 --> 01:55:53.920
We also talked a little
bit about the hormones

01:55:53.920 --> 01:55:54.753
that come from the brain

01:55:54.753 --> 01:55:57.180
and operate at the level of the kidney

01:55:57.180 --> 01:55:58.980
in order to either retain

01:55:58.980 --> 01:56:02.750
or allow water to leave your system.

01:56:02.750 --> 01:56:05.460
Talked a little bit about the
function of the kidney itself,

01:56:05.460 --> 01:56:06.910
a beautiful organ.

01:56:06.910 --> 01:56:09.410
We talked about the relationship

01:56:09.410 --> 01:56:12.930
between salt intake and
various health parameters

01:56:12.930 --> 01:56:16.420
and how a particular range of salt intake

01:56:16.420 --> 01:56:18.700
might be optimal,

01:56:18.700 --> 01:56:20.850
depending on the context

01:56:20.850 --> 01:56:22.420
in which that range is being consumed,

01:56:22.420 --> 01:56:24.820
meaning depending on whether or not

01:56:24.820 --> 01:56:27.920
you're hypertensive,
prehypertensive, or normal tension.

01:56:27.920 --> 01:56:30.350
We talked about fluid intake
and electrolyte intake,

01:56:30.350 --> 01:56:32.040
so sodium, potassium, and magnesium

01:56:32.040 --> 01:56:35.320
in the context of athletic
or sports performance,

01:56:35.320 --> 01:56:37.620
but also, in terms of
maintaining cognitive function.

01:56:37.620 --> 01:56:39.180
Talked about the Galpin equation,

01:56:39.180 --> 01:56:41.747
which you could easily
adapt to your body weight

01:56:41.747 --> 01:56:43.940
and to your circumstances.

01:56:43.940 --> 01:56:46.870
Of course, adjusting the amount of fluid

01:56:46.870 --> 01:56:49.290
and electrolyte intake upwards

01:56:49.290 --> 01:56:53.060
if you're exercising or working
in very hot environments,

01:56:53.060 --> 01:56:56.140
downwards, maybe, if you're
in less hot environments

01:56:56.140 --> 01:56:57.880
where you're sweating less and so on.

01:56:57.880 --> 01:56:59.290
We also talked about the relationship

01:56:59.290 --> 01:57:02.910
between the stress system
and the salt craving system

01:57:02.910 --> 01:57:05.400
and why those two systems interact

01:57:05.400 --> 01:57:09.060
and why, for some people
who may suffer a bit

01:57:09.060 --> 01:57:12.280
from anxiety or under
conditions of stress,

01:57:12.280 --> 01:57:13.600
increasing salt intake,

01:57:13.600 --> 01:57:15.730
provided it's done through healthy means,

01:57:15.730 --> 01:57:18.070
might actually be beneficial.

01:57:18.070 --> 01:57:19.300
We also talked about conditions

01:57:19.300 --> 01:57:22.410
in which increasing salt
intake might be beneficial

01:57:22.410 --> 01:57:24.980
for offsetting low blood pressure,

01:57:24.980 --> 01:57:26.650
and some of these postural syndromes

01:57:26.650 --> 01:57:29.140
that can lead people to
dizziness and so forth.

01:57:29.140 --> 01:57:30.620
These are things that have to be explored

01:57:30.620 --> 01:57:31.790
on an individual basis,

01:57:31.790 --> 01:57:34.040
and, of course, have to be explored

01:57:34.040 --> 01:57:35.940
with the support of your doctor.

01:57:35.940 --> 01:57:37.470
I mentioned "The Salt Fix,"

01:57:37.470 --> 01:57:39.320
which I think is an interesting read,

01:57:39.320 --> 01:57:41.460
keeping in mind that a lot
of the information in there

01:57:41.460 --> 01:57:43.640
runs counter to the typical narrative

01:57:43.640 --> 01:57:45.070
that you hear around salt,

01:57:45.070 --> 01:57:47.300
but nonetheless, has some
very interesting points

01:57:47.300 --> 01:57:48.920
that you might want to consider

01:57:48.920 --> 01:57:51.350
and certainly will broaden your view

01:57:51.350 --> 01:57:54.230
of the history of and
the applications of salt

01:57:54.230 --> 01:57:56.520
as it relates to a great number

01:57:56.520 --> 01:57:58.440
of health and performance metrics.

01:57:58.440 --> 01:58:00.790
We also talked about
the perception of salt,

01:58:00.790 --> 01:58:02.810
meaning the perception of salty tastes

01:58:02.810 --> 01:58:04.400
and how the perception of salty tastes

01:58:04.400 --> 01:58:06.620
and the perception of
other tastes, like sweet,

01:58:06.620 --> 01:58:08.260
can interact with one another

01:58:08.260 --> 01:58:11.180
to drive things like
increased sugar intake

01:58:11.180 --> 01:58:12.760
when you're not even aware of it,

01:58:12.760 --> 01:58:16.540
and indeed, how the combination
of salty and sweet taste

01:58:16.540 --> 01:58:19.220
can bias you towards craving more,

01:58:19.220 --> 01:58:20.630
for instance, processed foods,

01:58:20.630 --> 01:58:22.830
and why that might be
a good thing to avoid,

01:58:22.830 --> 01:58:24.680
and, of course, we talked about salt

01:58:24.680 --> 01:58:27.260
and its critical role
in the action potential,

01:58:27.260 --> 01:58:28.093
the fundamental way

01:58:28.093 --> 01:58:30.500
in which the nervous
system functions at all,

01:58:30.500 --> 01:58:33.070
so my hope for you, in
listening to this episode,

01:58:33.070 --> 01:58:35.300
is that you consider a question,

01:58:35.300 --> 01:58:36.420
and that question is

01:58:36.420 --> 01:58:39.560
what salt intake is best for you?

01:58:39.560 --> 01:58:41.100
And that you place that question

01:58:41.100 --> 01:58:44.160
in the context of your fluid intake,

01:58:44.160 --> 01:58:47.190
you place that in the context
of the diet you're following,

01:58:47.190 --> 01:58:50.390
the amount of caffeine
you might be ingesting,

01:58:50.390 --> 01:58:52.550
and the diuretic effects of caffeine,

01:58:52.550 --> 01:58:55.700
and crucially, that you
place that in the context

01:58:55.700 --> 01:58:58.160
of the electrolytes, more generally,

01:58:58.160 --> 01:59:01.350
meaning sodium, potassium, and magnesium.

01:59:01.350 --> 01:59:03.820
Someday, there will be an online program

01:59:03.820 --> 01:59:05.260
or an app, I imagine,

01:59:05.260 --> 01:59:08.930
where one could put a bunch
of different parameters in

01:59:08.930 --> 01:59:12.600
about their particular health status,

01:59:12.600 --> 01:59:15.470
their particular diet, their
particular exercise, et cetera.

01:59:15.470 --> 01:59:18.340
Maybe it would all be run
by AI algorithm or something

01:59:18.340 --> 01:59:20.070
where it would monitor all of that for us,

01:59:20.070 --> 01:59:21.970
and then it would spit out for us

01:59:21.970 --> 01:59:25.790
a precise amount of sodium that
we should take in each day.

01:59:25.790 --> 01:59:29.410
Unfortunately, no such tool
or device exists right now,

01:59:29.410 --> 01:59:32.060
and so all of us have to figure out

01:59:32.060 --> 01:59:34.920
the appropriate amount of
sodium intake for ourselves,

01:59:34.920 --> 01:59:36.400
and that has to be done

01:59:36.400 --> 01:59:39.300
under these contextual considerations.

01:59:39.300 --> 01:59:40.133
Who knows?

01:59:40.133 --> 01:59:42.340
Maybe one of you will design
such an app or such a device.

01:59:42.340 --> 01:59:44.233
I think it would be very useful.

01:59:45.390 --> 01:59:46.950
If nothing else,

01:59:46.950 --> 01:59:49.710
today's discussion ought
to illuminate the fact

01:59:49.710 --> 01:59:53.920
that some strict
recommendation of salt intake

01:59:53.920 --> 01:59:56.820
cannot be made universally
across the board for everybody.

01:59:56.820 --> 01:59:59.410
There's just simply no
way that could be done,

01:59:59.410 --> 02:00:03.290
and yet, I think most of
what we've learned about salt

02:00:03.290 --> 02:00:05.720
in the general discussions around health

02:00:05.720 --> 02:00:07.980
are that it's this evil substance.

02:00:07.980 --> 02:00:09.380
Nothing could be further from the truth.

02:00:09.380 --> 02:00:11.010
It's an incredible substance.

02:00:11.010 --> 02:00:13.510
Our physiology is dependent on it.

02:00:13.510 --> 02:00:16.230
Our cognition is dependent on it.

02:00:16.230 --> 02:00:18.027
Indeed, our mental and physical health

02:00:18.027 --> 02:00:20.640
and our performance in
essentially all aspects of life

02:00:20.640 --> 02:00:22.010
is dependent on it,

02:00:22.010 --> 02:00:23.793
and I hope I've been able to illuminate

02:00:23.793 --> 02:00:25.250
some of the beautiful ways

02:00:25.250 --> 02:00:27.930
in which the brain and
the bodily organs interact

02:00:27.930 --> 02:00:29.700
in order to help us regulate this thing

02:00:29.700 --> 02:00:31.100
that we call sodium balance,

02:00:31.100 --> 02:00:33.210
and the fact that we
have neurons in our brain

02:00:33.210 --> 02:00:37.550
that are both tuned to the
levels of salt in our body

02:00:37.550 --> 02:00:40.170
and positioned in a location in the brain

02:00:40.170 --> 02:00:42.970
that allows them to detect
the levels of salt in our body

02:00:42.970 --> 02:00:46.170
and to drive the intake
of more or less salt

02:00:46.170 --> 02:00:48.920
and more or less fluid
and other electrolytes

02:00:48.920 --> 02:00:51.500
really just points to
the beauty of the system

02:00:51.500 --> 02:00:52.790
that we've all evolved

02:00:52.790 --> 02:00:54.740
that allows us to interact
with our environment

02:00:54.740 --> 02:00:55.760
and make adjustments

02:00:55.760 --> 02:00:59.000
according to the context of
our daily and ongoing life.

